parts:
- text: "/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Residual Copy + Low-Rank Factor Forecaster (RC-LR)\"\ntags: \"low-rank, residual, autoregressive, cross-neuron, factor-model, forecasting\"\nabstract: \"We enhance the low-rank factor forecaster with a residual AR(1)-style copy head that anchors short-horizon predictions to the last observed activity and its delta via learned time-dependent coefficients, while retaining a low-rank factorized decoder for long-horizon structure. This targets strong t=1..4 MAE without sacrificing long-horizon gains. Uses Adam, default MAE loss, k=32 with column-normalized loadings.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"\n      Hyperparameters for Residual Copy + Low-Rank Factor model.\n      \"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'rank_k': 32,          # Increased capacity for cross-neuron\
    \ structure\n          'hidden_size': 64,     # Factor MLP hidden size\n          'normalize_loadings': True\n      }\n\n  def _logit(p):\n      p = jnp.clip(p, 1e-4, 1 - 1e-4)\n      return jnp.log(p) - jnp.log(1 - p)\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      AR(1)-style residual head:\n      y_copy[b, t, n] = c0[t] * x_last[b, n] + c1[t] * (x_last[b, n] - x_prev[b, n])\n      where c0[t] in [0,1], c1[t] in [-0.5, 0.5] (via tanh scaling).\n      \"\"\"\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x: (B, 4, N)\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          x_last = x[:, -1, :]          # (B, N)\n          x_prev = x[:, -2, :]          # (B, N)\n          delta = x_last - x_prev       # (B, N)\n\n          # Initialize c0 close to copy for early steps, decaying for later steps.\n          def c0_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              t = jnp.arange(shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n              # Simple decay from ~0.9\
    \ at t=1 to ~0.2 by t=32\n              v = 0.2 + 0.7 * jnp.exp(-t / 8.0)\n              return _logit(v).astype(dtype)\n\n          c0_raw = self.param('c0_raw', c0_init, (self.output_horizon,))\n          c1_raw = self.param('c1_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.output_horizon,))\n\n          c0 = nn.sigmoid(c0_raw)               # (32,) in [0,1]\n          c1 = 0.5 * nn.tanh(c1_raw)            # (32,) in [-0.5, 0.5]\n\n          # Broadcast over batch and neurons\n          c0_b = c0[None, :, None]              # (1, 32, 1)\n          c1_b = c1[None, :, None]              # (1, 32, 1)\n\n          y_copy = c0_b * x_last[:, None, :] + c1_b * delta[:, None, :]\n          return y_copy\n\n  class LowRankFactorForecaster(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Low-rank cross-neuron forecaster with a residual copy head.\n      - Learn neuron loadings L in R^{N x k}\n      - Project inputs x (B, 4, N) to factors (B, 4, k)\n      - Forecast factors with a small MLP (shared across factors): 4 ->\
    \ 32\n      - Project factor trajectories back to neurons and combine with residual copy head\n      \"\"\"\n      rank_k: int = 32\n      hidden_size: int = 64\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      normalize_loadings: bool = True\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          # x: (B, 4, N)\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n\n          # Loadings: (N, k)\n          loadings = self.param(\n              'loadings',\n              nn.initializers.lecun_normal(),\n              (N, self.rank_k)\n          )\n          if self.normalize_loadings:\n              norm = jnp.linalg.norm(loadings, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6\n              L = loadings / norm\n          else:\n              L = loadings\n\n          # Project inputs to factors: (B, 4, k)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,nk->btk', x, L)\n\n          # Small MLP per factor (weights shared across factors)\n          f_flat = factors_in.reshape(B * self.rank_k, T_in)   # (B*k, 4)\n\
    \          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(h)                 # (B*k, 32)\n          factors_out = h.reshape(B, self.rank_k, self.output_horizon).transpose(0, 2, 1)  # (B, 32, k)\n\n          # Optional per-horizon gating for factor strength\n          def gamma_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              # Start moderate to avoid overpowering the residual copy on early steps\n              return _logit(jnp.full(shape, 0.5, dtype=dtype))\n          gamma_raw = self.param('gamma_raw', gamma_init, (self.output_horizon,))\n          gamma = nn.sigmoid(gamma_raw)[None, :, None]  # (1, 32, 1)\n\n          # Project factors back to neurons: (B, 32, k) @ (k, N) -> (B, 32, N)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btk,kn->btn', factors_out, L.T)\n          y_factor = gamma * y_factor\n\n          # Residual copy head for short horizons\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=self.output_horizon)(x)\n\
    \n          # Combine\n          y = y_copy + y_factor\n          return y\n\n  class RCLFWrapper:\n      \"\"\"\n      Wrapper providing the required interface.\n      \"\"\"\n      def __init__(self, rank_k=32, hidden_size=64, normalize_loadings=True):\n          self.model = LowRankFactorForecaster(\n              rank_k=rank_k,\n              hidden_size=hidden_size,\n              normalize_loadings=normalize_loadings\n          )\n          self.needs_rng = False  # No Dropout/BatchNorm\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      rank_k = int(hparams.get('rank_k', 32))\n      hidden_size = int(hparams.get('hidden_size', 64))\n      normalize = bool(hparams.get('normalize_loadings', True))\n      return RCLFWrapper(rank_k=rank_k,\
    \ hidden_size=hidden_size, normalize_loadings=normalize)\n\n  # Use default MAE loss by not overriding compute_loss()\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"\n      Use Adam optimizer (matches system default) for stability.\n      \"\"\"\n      return optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating model improvements**


  I need to plan our next steps after the initial submission with the low-rank factor forecaster (LRFF). Our score slightly improved from -0.029954 to -0.030063, which indicates a marginal gain since lower negative MAE is better. However, looking at the validation metrics, it seems the baseline Val_MAE_1 scored 0.0198 versus our 0.0283, which is concerning. I must remember that the posted Val_MAE scores aren''t cumulative, making this interpretation tricky. Now, I’ll strategize on how to enhance our model further.**Clarifying score interpretations**


  I''m analyzing the comparison between our average score of 0.0300626 and the baseline at 0.029954. Even though 0.0300626 is slightly higher, the scoring is tricky since we''re dealing with negative MAE values. A more negative score is actually worse! The goal is to aim for scores closer to zero, minimizing MAE. The scoreboard only shows one baseline score, which can be confusing. Overall, our submission yields a slightly higher MAE than the baseline, which isn’t ideal. I need to adjust our approach to improve performance!**Analyzing model performance**


  It looks like our method isn''t performing better overall. While our step MAEs are lower at several points besides step 1, I still need to assess what this means. Our scores are 0.0283 at step 1, climbing to 0.0307 by step 32. The baseline scores are consistently lower, especially at step 1, where it''s 0.0198. So, even though we may excel at long horizons, our average across 32 steps is worse. I’m pondering if earlier steps significantly impact the average more than I expected.**Evaluating error contribution**


  I''m realizing that early steps probably have lower absolute errors for all models. The baseline scores are much lower at t=1 and t=4, which might dominate the average over the 32 steps since those initial times are generally easier. However, average errors across all steps don''t account for step difficulty—they''re just equally weighted. Smaller errors at early steps contribute less to the average. So, while we see improvement at step 32, the early step advantage for the baseline results in a slightly worse overall performance for us.**Optimizing model architecture**


  The baseline has an early advantage that overshadows our long-step improvements. I need to analyze the training architecture to ensure it aligns with the system''s default. Our model uses no dropout and the AdamW optimizer, but should I add a loss weighting for early steps? Early errors might reduce our overall average. Each step needs equal weighting, but smaller early step errors won''t impact it as much.


  I’m also considering adding skip connections to anchor early predictions better and possibly combining different decoders for flexible forecasting. I''ll explore using temporal convolutions instead of just the first four steps and try to incorporate condition-specific adaptations. Yet, I need to keep memory and training constraints in mind. We could introduce a copy mechanism across neurons for efficiency while minimizing overhead.**Proposing architecture improvements**


  Our MLP structure includes Dense layers with biases, and while our method improves long horizon predictions, early steps need better anchoring. To address this, I’m proposing the "Residual Copy-Plus-Factor Low-Rank Forecaster (RCF-LR)." This adds per-time-step scalars to leverage the last observed value and enforces adjustments based on deltas.


  I’ll implement learned parameters while keeping complexity minimal. I’m also considering simple initializations to avoid degrading training. Additionally, introducing regularization for loadings seems crucial. I want to ensure we balance early step emphasis without compromising long-term performance. The code adjustments are manageable within our memory constraints, so everything looks promising!**Implementing model improvements**


  I noticed that in the current Low-Rank Forecaster (LRFF), the reshaped dimensions and the dense layers function together while maintaining a moderate parameter count. To enhance performance, I''m settling on rank_k=32 for richer representations. I want to introduce residual gating per time step to adjust predictions dynamically. Switching to Adam from AdamW should unify our approach, and I’ll keep the initial scaling of parameters straightforward.


  Let''s craft the code for this submission and ensure we optimize initialization while considering memory constraints. I also want to clarify our network''s requirement about BatchNorm and dropout, keeping the implementation clear and efficient.**Finalizing implementation details**


  I''m adding a ''gamma'' parameter that controls the amplitude of the factor path. The initial value will be set low to avoid overshadowing during early training steps. I want to prepare a submission with these improvements, ensuring the YAML is correctly formatted, especially with special characters in the title.


  I''ll adjust the BATCH_SIZE to 8 and prepare to execute my action. I must also ensure the code complies with the allowed features, avoiding things like dropout. Now, let''s finish up the code and submission!'
