role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{storage write ariadne/models.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Ariadne lineage models for ZAPBench forecasting.\n  Includes:\n    - ResidualCopyHead: short-horizon AR(1)-style copy\n    - RC_DiagLDSFactorModel: low-rank factor model with diagonal LDS recurrence\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n\n  def _logit(p):\n      p = jnp.clip(p, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n      return jnp.log(p) - jnp.log(1 - p)\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      AR(1)-style residual head:\n      y_copy[b, t, n] = c0[t] * x_last[b, n] + c1[t] * (x_last[b, n] - x_prev[b, n])\n      where c0[t] in [0,1], c1[t] in [-0.5, 0.5].\n      \"\"\"\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x: (B, 4, N)\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          x_last = x[:, -1, :]            # (B, N)\n          x_prev = x[:, -2, :]            # (B, N)\n          delta = x_last - x_prev         # (B,\
    \ N)\n\n          # Initialize c0 close to copy for early steps, decaying for later steps.\n          def c0_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              t = jnp.arange(shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n              v = 0.2 + 0.7 * jnp.exp(-t / 8.0)  # ~0.9 -> ~0.2\n              return _logit(v).astype(dtype)\n\n          c0_raw = self.param('c0_raw', c0_init, (self.output_horizon,))\n          c1_raw = self.param('c1_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.output_horizon,))\n\n          c0 = nn.sigmoid(c0_raw)               # (32,)\n          c1 = 0.5 * nn.tanh(c1_raw)            # (32,)\n\n          c0_b = c0[None, :, None]              # (1, 32, 1)\n          c1_b = c1[None, :, None]              # (1, 32, 1)\n\n          y_copy = c0_b * x_last[:, None, :] + c1_b * delta[:, None, :]\n          return y_copy  # (B, 32, N)\n\n  class RC_DiagLDSFactorModel(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Residual Copy + Diagonal LDS factor model with low-rank neuron coupling.\n\n      Components:\n      \
    \  - Learn neuron loadings L in R^{N x k} (column-normalized if enabled)\n        - Encode factors from inputs x (B, 4, N) -> (B, 4, k)\n        - Aggregate to initial state f0 (B, k) via per-factor linear readout\n        - Diagonal LDS recurrence: f_decay[t] = a^t * f0, a in (-a_max, a_max)\n        - Learned per-horizon increments f_inc via tiny MLP (shared across factors)\n        - Project factors back to neurons: y_factor = f_out @ L^T\n        - Residual Copy head for strong short-horizon anchors\n        - Per-horizon gate gamma[t] mixing factor contribution\n\n      Output:\n        y = y_copy + gamma[t] * y_factor  with shapes (B, 32, N)\n      \"\"\"\n      rank_k: int = 48\n      hidden_size: int = 64\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      normalize_loadings: bool = True\n      a_max: float = 0.99\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          # x: (B, 4, N)\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n\n          # Loadings L: (N, k)\n        \
    \  loadings = self.param(\n              'loadings',\n              nn.initializers.lecun_normal(),\n              (N, self.rank_k)\n          )\n          if self.normalize_loadings:\n              L = loadings / (jnp.linalg.norm(loadings, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          else:\n              L = loadings\n\n          # Project inputs to factor space: (B, 4, k)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,nk->btk', x, L)\n\n          # Initial state f0 per factor via per-factor linear readout\n          f_flat = factors_in.reshape(B * self.rank_k, T_in)        # (B*k, 4)\n          f0 = nn.Dense(1, use_bias=True)(f_flat).reshape(B, self.rank_k)  # (B, k)\n\n          # Diagonal LDS recurrence coefficients a in (-a_max, a_max)\n          a_raw = self.param('a_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))\n          a = self.a_max * nn.tanh(a_raw)                            # (k,)\n          # Precompute powers a^t for t=0..31: (32, k)\n          t = jnp.arange(self.output_horizon,\
    \ dtype=jnp.float32)[:, None]   # (32,1)\n          a_pows = jnp.power(a[None, :], t)                          # (32, k)\n          f_decay = f0[:, None, :] * a_pows[None, :, :]              # (B, 32, k)\n\n          # Per-factor, per-horizon increments from 4 -> 32 using tiny MLP (shared across factors)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat)                     # (B*k, H)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          f_inc = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(h).reshape(B, self.rank_k, self.output_horizon).transpose(0, 2, 1)  # (B, 32, k)\n\n          f_out = f_decay + f_inc                                    # (B, 32, k)\n\n          # Project factors back to neurons: (B, 32, k) @ (k, N) -> (B, 32, N)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btk,kn->btn', f_out, L.T)\n\n          # Horizon-wise gate for factor strength\n          gamma_raw = self.param('gamma_raw', lambda k, s, d=jnp.float32: _logit(jnp.full(s, 0.6, dtype=d)), (self.output_horizon,))\n          gamma = nn.sigmoid(gamma_raw)[None,\
    \ :, None]               # (1, 32, 1)\n          y_factor = gamma * y_factor\n\n          # Residual copy head\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=self.output_horizon)(x)\n\n          return y_copy + y_factor  # (B, 32, N)\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Residual Copy + Diagonal LDS Factor Model (RC-DiagLDS)\"\ntags: \"low-rank, state-space, residual, factor-model, cross-neuron, forecasting\"\nabstract: \"We couple a residual AR(1)-style copy head (anchoring t=1..4) with a low-rank factor decoder whose trajectories are generated by a stable diagonal linear dynamical system (a in (-0.99,0.99)) plus a tiny per-factor 4→32 MLP for learned increments. Factors are projected back via column-normalized loadings (N×k). A per-horizon gate mixes factor vs copy. This targets low short-horizon MAE while maintaining long-horizon structure.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen\
    \ as nn\n  import optax\n\n  from models import RC_DiagLDSFactorModel\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Keep config compact; tune rank if needed in follow-ups\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'rank_k': 48,          # diagonal LDS dimension\n          'hidden_size': 64,\n          'normalize_loadings': True\n      }\n\n  class Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, rank_k=48, hidden_size=64, normalize_loadings=True):\n          self.model = RC_DiagLDSFactorModel(\n              rank_k=rank_k,\n              hidden_size=hidden_size,\n              normalize_loadings=normalize_loadings\n          )\n          self.needs_rng = False  # No BatchNorm/Dropout\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training)\n\n\
    \  def create_network(hparams):\n      return Wrapper(\n          rank_k=int(hparams.get('rank_k', 48)),\n          hidden_size=int(hparams.get('hidden_size', 64)),\n          normalize_loadings=bool(hparams.get('normalize_loadings', True))\n      )\n\n  # Use default MAE loss\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating low-rank factor forecaster**\n\nI need to clarify our low-rank factor forecaster method's results. We submitted it and got a score of -0.030063, which is a bit worse than the baseline score of -0.029954. But wait, I'm wondering if a more negative mean absolute error (MAE) indicates better performance, since typically in this context, a lower MAE is preferred. So, while my score sounds worse due to the negative value, more negative should be better, right? I need to double-check that.**Clarifying MAE scores**\n\nI'm analyzing the negative MAE values: -0.030063 is lower than -0.029954, so in this context, a \"higher\" score actually means less negative. They likely display scores as negative MAEs, sort by the least negative value—closer to zero is better. I see our step MAE at t=1 of 0.0283 compared to the baseline's remarkable 0.0198, which is significantly better. However, our later horizons do outperform the baseline. Overall, our total MAE is slightly worse,\
  \ but we aimed to improve those early scores. We'll see how our RC-LR submission performs!**Planning next steps for experiments**\n\nAs I await evaluation results, I’m brainstorming additional experiments. I want to incorporate per-condition gating, a linear dynamical system with shared autoregressive factors, and temporal convolution across four input steps along with condition embeddings. There's also the idea of using PCA for loadings via SVD of the training data offline, but I realize some constraints prevent custom training loops and pre-trained models. While we can’t initialize from trained data, deterministic initializations from random could be an option. I need to navigate these restrictions carefully!**Exploring model limitations**\n\nI’m realizing that within our model's framework, we can’t compute additional matrices beyond the standard layers. While we could set param initializations to be learned, restrictions prevent us from accessing the dataset for computations like PCA.\
  \ I thought about adding condition-specific factor scaling, but the training loop limits us by loading condition labels and restricting sequences, so the network only sees the primary input. I wonder if we could adjust the network to handle two inputs. Plus, incorporating condition invariance through ephemeral hyperparameters doesn't seem helpful. I might explore using a temporal basis with fixed DCT coefficients instead.**Planning improvements for model architecture**\n\nI’m considering how our current network computes directly and noting that DCT aids in spectral smoothing. A potential improvement is introducing a deep low-rank model with a separate encoder-decoder. Instead of just using the time dimension, we could incorporate temporal mixing before factorization to derive features like velocity and acceleration. I also want to explore adding a lag basis head for scalar combinations across time. Adjusting the learning rate with AdamW and checking our training loop should enhance performance.\
  \ I'll consider implementing a diagonal linear recurrence and possibly propose a new job after receiving evaluation results. It seems there's a cooldown period, but I can start preparing modular code for better organization.**Implementing Diagonal LDS Model**\n\nI'm focusing on implementing a 32-step output recurrence that leverages JAX's lax scan. I plan to generate a dynamic part and use an MLP for increments to get the overall factor output. I'll implement various components like the ResidualCopyHead and DiagLDSFactorForecaster in `models.py`. It’s essential to be mindful of memory usage as we develop this, especially with batch sizes and tensor shapes; its complexity might lead to high memory consumption.\n\nAdditionally, I want to ensure our submission adheres to potential cooldowns while preparing related modules. I'll also streamline our optimizer for stability and explore customizing the loss function for better training performance. Time to get coding!**Implementing Diagonal LDS\
  \ Factor Model**\n\nI’m planning to store code for reusability, especially since we’ve already used 31k tokens, so it seems reasonable to embed code directly. I’ll implement `ariadne/models.py`, focusing on classes like `ResidualCopyHead` and `RC_DiagLDSFactorModel`. \n\nI'll pay close attention to parameter setups and shapes, especially for the large Nxk weights. The optimizer will be based on Adam. We're aiming for a rank_k of 48 and crafting a submission with a title and abstract detailing our model's improvements. Let's ensure all YAML syntax is correct and proceed with storage and submission actions."
