- text: "/execute_action{storage write ariadne/models.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Ariadne lineage models for ZAPBench forecasting.\n  Includes:\n    - ResidualCopyHead: short-horizon AR(1)-style copy\n    - RC_DiagLDSFactorModel: low-rank factor model with diagonal LDS recurrence\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n\n  def _logit(p):\n      p = jnp.clip(p, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n      return jnp.log(p) - jnp.log(1 - p)\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      AR(1)-style residual head:\n      y_copy[b, t, n] = c0[t] * x_last[b, n] + c1[t] * (x_last[b, n] - x_prev[b, n])\n      where c0[t] in [0,1], c1[t] in [-0.5, 0.5].\n      \"\"\"\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x: (B, 4, N)\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          x_last = x[:, -1, :]            # (B, N)\n          x_prev = x[:, -2, :]            # (B, N)\n          delta = x_last - x_prev         # (B,\
    \ N)\n\n          # Initialize c0 close to copy for early steps, decaying for later steps.\n          def c0_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              t = jnp.arange(shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n              v = 0.2 + 0.7 * jnp.exp(-t / 8.0)  # ~0.9 -> ~0.2\n              return _logit(v).astype(dtype)\n\n          c0_raw = self.param('c0_raw', c0_init, (self.output_horizon,))\n          c1_raw = self.param('c1_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.output_horizon,))\n\n          c0 = nn.sigmoid(c0_raw)               # (32,)\n          c1 = 0.5 * nn.tanh(c1_raw)            # (32,)\n\n          c0_b = c0[None, :, None]              # (1, 32, 1)\n          c1_b = c1[None, :, None]              # (1, 32, 1)\n\n          y_copy = c0_b * x_last[:, None, :] + c1_b * delta[:, None, :]\n          return y_copy  # (B, 32, N)\n\n  class RC_DiagLDSFactorModel(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Residual Copy + Diagonal LDS factor model with low-rank neuron coupling.\n\n      Components:\n      \
    \  - Learn neuron loadings L in R^{N x k} (column-normalized if enabled)\n        - Encode factors from inputs x (B, 4, N) -> (B, 4, k)\n        - Aggregate to initial state f0 (B, k) via per-factor linear readout\n        - Diagonal LDS recurrence: f_decay[t] = a^t * f0, a in (-a_max, a_max)\n        - Learned per-horizon increments f_inc via tiny MLP (shared across factors)\n        - Project factors back to neurons: y_factor = f_out @ L^T\n        - Residual Copy head for strong short-horizon anchors\n        - Per-horizon gate gamma[t] mixing factor contribution\n\n      Output:\n        y = y_copy + gamma[t] * y_factor  with shapes (B, 32, N)\n      \"\"\"\n      rank_k: int = 48\n      hidden_size: int = 64\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      normalize_loadings: bool = True\n      a_max: float = 0.99\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          # x: (B, 4, N)\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n\n          # Loadings L: (N, k)\n        \
    \  loadings = self.param(\n              'loadings',\n              nn.initializers.lecun_normal(),\n              (N, self.rank_k)\n          )\n          if self.normalize_loadings:\n              L = loadings / (jnp.linalg.norm(loadings, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          else:\n              L = loadings\n\n          # Project inputs to factor space: (B, 4, k)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,nk->btk', x, L)\n\n          # Initial state f0 per factor via per-factor linear readout\n          f_flat = factors_in.reshape(B * self.rank_k, T_in)        # (B*k, 4)\n          f0 = nn.Dense(1, use_bias=True)(f_flat).reshape(B, self.rank_k)  # (B, k)\n\n          # Diagonal LDS recurrence coefficients a in (-a_max, a_max)\n          a_raw = self.param('a_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))\n          a = self.a_max * nn.tanh(a_raw)                            # (k,)\n\n          # Compute a^t safely for possibly negative a (avoid float exponent on negative base)\n  \
    \        # Build sequence [1, a, a, ..., a] length=output_horizon then cumulative product\n          ones = jnp.ones((1, self.rank_k), dtype=a.dtype)\n          a_rows = jnp.tile(a[None, :], (self.output_horizon - 1, 1))\n          a_stack = jnp.vstack([ones, a_rows])                       # (32, k)\n          a_pows = jnp.cumprod(a_stack, axis=0)                      # (32, k) giving a**t for t=0..31\n\n          f_decay = f0[:, None, :] * a_pows[None, :, :]              # (B, 32, k)\n\n          # Per-factor, per-horizon increments from 4 -> 32 using tiny MLP (shared across factors)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat)                     # (B*k, H)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          f_inc = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(h).reshape(B, self.rank_k, self.output_horizon).transpose(0, 2, 1)  # (B, 32, k)\n\n          f_out = f_decay + f_inc                                    # (B, 32, k)\n\n          # Project factors back to neurons: (B, 32, k) @ (k, N) -> (B, 32, N)\n\
    \          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btk,kn->btn', f_out, L.T)\n\n          # Horizon-wise gate for factor strength\n          def gamma_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              return _logit(jnp.full(shape, 0.6, dtype=dtype))\n          gamma_raw = self.param('gamma_raw', gamma_init, (self.output_horizon,))\n          gamma = nn.sigmoid(gamma_raw)[None, :, None]               # (1, 32, 1)\n          y_factor = gamma * y_factor\n\n          # Residual copy head\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=self.output_horizon)(x)\n\n          return y_copy + y_factor  # (B, 32, N)\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Residual Copy + Diagonal LDS Factor Model (RC-DiagLDS) — NaN Fix\"\ntags: \"low-rank, state-space, residual, factor-model, cross-neuron, forecasting, stability\"\nabstract: \"Fixes NaNs by computing a^t via cumulative products with integer steps to handle negative decay factors safely. RC-DiagLDS combines a residual AR(1)-style copy head with\
    \ a low-rank factor decoder driven by a stable diagonal LDS (|a|<=0.99) plus a tiny per-factor 4→32 MLP, projected back via column-normalized loadings and mixed by a per-horizon gate.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import optax\n  from models import RC_DiagLDSFactorModel\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'rank_k': 48,\n          'hidden_size': 64,\n          'normalize_loadings': True\n      }\n\n  class Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, rank_k=48, hidden_size=64, normalize_loadings=True):\n          self.model = RC_DiagLDSFactorModel(\n              rank_k=rank_k,\n              hidden_size=hidden_size,\n              normalize_loadings=normalize_loadings\n          )\n          self.needs_rng = False\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self,\
    \ params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return Wrapper(\n          rank_k=int(hparams.get('rank_k', 48)),\n          hidden_size=int(hparams.get('hidden_size', 64)),\n          normalize_loadings=bool(hparams.get('normalize_loadings', True))\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing power function issue**\n\nI’m looking at jnp.power(a[None,:], t) and suspecting there might be issues with negative values. Raising a negative base to a non-integer power yields NaNs, especially since t is a float. I’m working with jnp.arange for t, which leads to non-integer exponents when a is negative. To fix this, I could set t to use integer values instead, by changing it to jnp.arange(self.output_horizon, dtype=jnp.int32) for integer exponents.**Fixing jnp.power function**\n\nI’m noticing that jnp.power returns a float, but using an integer exponent with a negative base is acceptable. JAX promotes the integer exponent to float, which allows repeated multiplication and adheres to Python semantics. For negative bases, the result will vary based on the exponent's parity. Changing the dtype to jnp.int32 seems straightforward. \n\nAdditionally, we can enhance the model stability by clipping L with L2 normalization and adjusting initial gamma. I’ll patch models.py\
  \ in ariadne accordingly and resubmit the corrected RC-DiagLDS.**Enhancing training stability**\n\nI’m considering enabling weight decay in create_optimizer to help reduce overfitting while ensuring gradient stability. We might also explore increasing rank but will keep it at 48 for now. The error “argument of type 'NoneType' is not iterable” suggests the evaluator needs a step_maes dict, so fixing that should lead to valid outputs. \n\nAdditionally, we should create a test() function to run a forward pass, but it might skip full training, which isn’t ideal. To address NaNs, I could ultimately override compute_loss to use jnp.nan_to_num, while ensuring that I don’t mask them when they indicate bugs.**Patching the code**\n\nNow that we've addressed a_pows, I don't need to override it anymore. It's time to patch the code in ariadne/models.py. First, I’ll open the file again to confirm the specific areas that need editing.\n\nIn the previous code, we had:\n\nt = jnp.arange(self.output_horizon,\
  \ dtype=jnp.float32)[:, None] \na_pows = jnp.power(a[None, :], t)\n\nWe need to change the dtype of t to integer. For instance:\n\nt = jnp.arange(self.output_horizon, dtype=jnp.int32)[:, None]\na_pows = jnp.power(a[None, :], t)\n\nThis way, jnp.power will handle the negative bases and exponents properly, which could actually model oscillatory behavior in the diagonal LDS effectively.**Preparing for resubmission**\n\nI need to ensure the f0 shape is correct, along with the braces before resubmitting. It looks like the station has a cooldown period, which might allow immediate submissions since we just submitted. I’ll patch ariadne/models.py by changing the dtype of t to int and ensuring a_pows utilizes compatible types.\n\nThe RC-LR results show significant MAE improvements, especially for longer horizons. I plan to implement a test() function for sanity checks but won’t include it in the submission. After resubmission, we can explore additional experiments, considering factors like recurrence\
  \ and gating across neurons. Lastly, I’ll incorporate comments in the code and note potential NaN causes for future reference.**Writing updated model file**\n\nI’m handling a batch size of 8, which results in many operations but is still manageable within the GPU's capacity. Now, I'm ready to output the updated file for ariadne/models.py. \n\nI plan to change the dtype of t and restructure the calculation of a_pows by using the cumprod method instead of jnp.power to avoid any negatives. I’ll stack the values properly to ensure all required shapes are met. Updating the RC_DiagLDSFactorModel __call__ and adjusting c0 decay slope are in the cards too. \n\nOnce the file is ready, I’ll resubmit it."
