parts:
- text: "/execute_action{storage write ariadne/models.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Ariadne lineage models for ZAPBench forecasting.\n  Includes:\n    - ResidualCopyHead: short-horizon AR(1)-style copy\n    - RC_DiagLDSFactorModel: low-rank factor model with diagonal LDS recurrence\n    - RC_DualDecayFactorModel: two-timescale diagonal LDS per factor\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n\n  def _logit(p):\n      p = jnp.clip(p, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n      return jnp.log(p) - jnp.log(1 - p)\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      AR(1)-style residual head:\n      y_copy[b, t, n] = c0[t] * x_last[b, n] + c1[t] * (x_last[b, n] - x_prev[b, n])\n      where c0[t] in [0,1], c1[t] in [-0.5, 0.5].\n      \"\"\"\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x: (B, 4, N)\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          x_last = x[:, -1, :]            # (B, N)\n          x_prev = x[:, -2, :]\
    \            # (B, N)\n          delta = x_last - x_prev         # (B, N)\n\n          # Initialize c0 close to copy for early steps, decaying for later steps.\n          def c0_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              t = jnp.arange(shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n              v = 0.2 + 0.7 * jnp.exp(-t / 8.0)  # ~0.9 -> ~0.2\n              return _logit(v).astype(dtype)\n\n          c0_raw = self.param('c0_raw', c0_init, (self.output_horizon,))\n          c1_raw = self.param('c1_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.output_horizon,))\n\n          c0 = nn.sigmoid(c0_raw)               # (32,)\n          c1 = 0.5 * nn.tanh(c1_raw)            # (32,)\n\n          c0_b = c0[None, :, None]              # (1, 32, 1)\n          c1_b = c1[None, :, None]              # (1, 32, 1)\n\n          y_copy = c0_b * x_last[:, None, :] + c1_b * delta[:, None, :]\n          return y_copy  # (B, 32, N)\n\n  class RC_DiagLDSFactorModel(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Residual Copy + Diagonal LDS factor\
    \ model with low-rank neuron coupling.\n\n      Components:\n        - Learn neuron loadings L in R^{N x k} (column-normalized if enabled)\n        - Encode factors from inputs x (B, 4, N) -> (B, 4, k)\n        - Aggregate to initial state f0 (B, k) via per-factor linear readout\n        - Diagonal LDS recurrence: f_decay[t] = a^t * f0, a in (-a_max, a_max)\n        - Learned per-horizon increments f_inc via tiny MLP (shared across factors)\n        - Project factors back to neurons: y_factor = f_out @ L^T\n        - Residual Copy head for strong short-horizon anchors\n        - Per-horizon gate gamma[t] mixing factor contribution\n\n      Output:\n        y = y_copy + gamma[t] * y_factor  with shapes (B, 32, N)\n      \"\"\"\n      rank_k: int = 48\n      hidden_size: int = 64\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      normalize_loadings: bool = True\n      a_max: float = 0.99\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          # x: (B, 4, N)\n        \
    \  B, T_in, N = x.shape\n\n          # Loadings L: (N, k)\n          loadings = self.param(\n              'loadings',\n              nn.initializers.lecun_normal(),\n              (N, self.rank_k)\n          )\n          if self.normalize_loadings:\n              L = loadings / (jnp.linalg.norm(loadings, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          else:\n              L = loadings\n\n          # Project inputs to factor space: (B, 4, k)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,nk->btk', x, L)\n\n          # Initial state f0 per factor via per-factor linear readout\n          f_flat = factors_in.reshape(B * self.rank_k, T_in)        # (B*k, 4)\n          f0 = nn.Dense(1, use_bias=True)(f_flat).reshape(B, self.rank_k)  # (B, k)\n\n          # Diagonal LDS recurrence coefficients a in (-a_max, a_max)\n          a_raw = self.param('a_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))\n          a = self.a_max * nn.tanh(a_raw)                            # (k,)\n\n          # Compute a^t safely with\
    \ cumulative products\n          ones = jnp.ones((1, self.rank_k), dtype=a.dtype)\n          a_rows = jnp.tile(a[None, :], (self.output_horizon - 1, 1))\n          a_stack = jnp.vstack([ones, a_rows])                       # (32, k)\n          a_pows = jnp.cumprod(a_stack, axis=0)                      # (32, k) giving a**t for t=0..31\n          f_decay = f0[:, None, :] * a_pows[None, :, :]              # (B, 32, k)\n\n          # Per-factor, per-horizon increments from 4 -> 32 using tiny MLP (shared across factors)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat)                     # (B*k, H)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          f_inc = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(h).reshape(B, self.rank_k, self.output_horizon).transpose(0, 2, 1)  # (B, 32, k)\n\n          f_out = f_decay + f_inc                                    # (B, 32, k)\n\n          # Project factors back to neurons: (B, 32, k) @ (k, N) -> (B, 32, N)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btk,kn->btn', f_out, L.T)\n\n        \
    \  # Horizon-wise gate for factor strength (init ~0.6)\n          def gamma_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              return _logit(jnp.full(shape, 0.6, dtype=dtype))\n          gamma_raw = self.param('gamma_raw', gamma_init, (self.output_horizon,))\n          gamma = nn.sigmoid(gamma_raw)[None, :, None]               # (1, 32, 1)\n          y_factor = gamma * y_factor\n\n          # Residual copy head\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=self.output_horizon)(x)\n\n          return y_copy + y_factor  # (B, 32, N)\n\n  class RC_DualDecayFactorModel(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Residual Copy + Two-timescale Diagonal LDS factor model (per-factor slow/fast decays).\n\n      Components:\n        - Loadings L in R^{N x k} (optional column normalization)\n        - Factors from inputs: (B, 4, N) -> (B, 4, k)\n        - f0 via per-factor linear readout from 4 inputs\n        - Two diagonal LDS decays per factor with coefficients a1, a2 in (-a_max, a_max)\n      \
    \  - Per-factor mixing m in [0,1]: f_decay = m * f1 + (1-m) * f2\n        - Tiny shared MLP to produce per-horizon increments (4 -> 32) per factor\n        - Per-horizon gate gamma[t] (init low early, higher late) for factor strength\n        - Residual copy head\n\n      Output: y = y_copy + gamma[t] * (f_decay + f_inc) @ L^T\n      \"\"\"\n      rank_k: int = 64\n      hidden_size: int = 64\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      normalize_loadings: bool = True\n      a_max: float = 0.995\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n\n          # Loadings\n          loadings = self.param('loadings', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, self.rank_k))\n          if self.normalize_loadings:\n              L = loadings / (jnp.linalg.norm(loadings, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          else:\n              L = loadings\n\n          # Project to factors\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,nk->btk', x, L)  # (B, 4, k)\n\n\
    \          # f0 per factor\n          f_flat = factors_in.reshape(B * self.rank_k, T_in)\n          f0 = nn.Dense(1, use_bias=True)(f_flat).reshape(B, self.rank_k)  # (B, k)\n\n          # Two decays per factor\n          a1_raw = self.param('a1_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))\n          a2_raw = self.param('a2_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))\n          a1 = self.a_max * nn.tanh(a1_raw)\n          a2 = self.a_max * nn.tanh(a2_raw)\n\n          def powers(a_vec):\n              ones = jnp.ones((1, self.rank_k), dtype=a_vec.dtype)\n              a_rows = jnp.tile(a_vec[None, :], (self.output_horizon - 1, 1))\n              return jnp.cumprod(jnp.vstack([ones, a_rows]), axis=0)  # (32, k)\n\n          a1_pows = powers(a1)\n          a2_pows = powers(a2)\n          f1 = f0[:, None, :] * a1_pows[None, :, :]  # (B, 32, k)\n          f2 = f0[:, None, :] * a2_pows[None, :, :]  # (B, 32, k)\n\n          # Per-factor mixture m in [0,1]\n          m_raw = self.param('m_raw',\
    \ nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))\n          m = nn.sigmoid(m_raw)[None, None, :]  # (1,1,k)\n          f_decay = m * f1 + (1.0 - m) * f2\n\n          # Tiny MLP increments (shared across factors)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          f_inc = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(h).reshape(B, self.rank_k, self.output_horizon).transpose(0, 2, 1)  # (B,32,k)\n\n          f_out = f_decay + f_inc  # (B, 32, k)\n\n          # Project back to neurons\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btk,kn->btn', f_out, L.T)\n\n          # Horizon-wise gamma init: lower early, higher late\n          def gamma_sched_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n              g = 0.35 + 0.45 * t  # 0.35 -> 0.80\n              return _logit(g).astype(dtype)\n          gamma_raw = self.param('gamma_raw', gamma_sched_init, (self.output_horizon,))\n          gamma = nn.sigmoid(gamma_raw)[None, :, None]\n  \
    \        y_factor = gamma * y_factor\n\n          # Residual copy head\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=self.output_horizon)(x)\n\n          return y_copy + y_factor\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"RC-DualDecay (k=64): Two-Timescale LDS + Residual Copy\"\ntags: \"low-rank, state-space, dual-decay, residual, factor-model, forecasting\"\nabstract: \"Extends RC-DiagLDS by giving each factor two stable decay rates (fast/slow) and a learned per-factor mixture, plus per-horizon gating that increases with horizon. Retains the residual AR(1)-style copy head and shared 4→32 increment MLP. Uses k=64 to better capture population structure while keeping memory linear in N.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import optax\n  from models import RC_DualDecayFactorModel\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'rank_k': 64,\n      \
    \    'hidden_size': 64,\n          'normalize_loadings': True\n      }\n\n  class Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, rank_k=64, hidden_size=64, normalize_loadings=True):\n          self.model = RC_DualDecayFactorModel(\n              rank_k=rank_k,\n              hidden_size=hidden_size,\n              normalize_loadings=normalize_loadings\n          )\n          self.needs_rng = False\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return Wrapper(\n          rank_k=int(hparams.get('rank_k', 64)),\n          hidden_size=int(hparams.get('hidden_size', 64)),\n          normalize_loadings=bool(hparams.get('normalize_loadings', True))\n      )\n\n  # Use default MAE loss\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return\
    \ optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering model improvements**\n\nI'm thinking about increasing rank_k beyond 48 to see if that improves performance. It might pose some memory risk since our model is already quite heavy. If we go up to k=128, that might still be manageable on a 24GB GPU, but the time could exceed 45 minutes. Also, I want to explore adding per-condition bias or gating since our dataset includes condition labels. However, the current interface doesn't support these labels directly in the inputs.**Exploring model adjustments**\n\nI realized that I can’t modify the training loop, but maybe I can add something to compute_loss for regularization with condition-specific approaches. Adding per-neuron biases seems like a possibility since the model currently uses factor projection only. This could capture baseline offset patterns. However, while it may help reduce the Mean Absolute Error (MAE), there’s a risk that it could degrade generalization. I'm also considering if introducing per-horizon\
  \ per-neuron scaling is an option to explore.**Considering model enhancements**\n\nI’m noticing another significant parameter here. I think introducing \"temporal convolution\" across inputs for the factor aggregator could be worthwhile, using a 1D convolution of length 4. Currently, it uses Dense layers, but moving to a learned AR(2) or moving average might help improve things. \n\nI’m also contemplating adding exponential smoothing multi-time-scale gating, which could involve two decay rates per factor for short and long times, effectively doubling k. Additionally, I could explore using per-factor time-dependent amplitudes and slopes for more granular control.**Evaluating model adjustments**\n\nI noticed that in the code, Dense is applied to f_flat, which has a shape of (B*k, T_in), without factoring in the factor index. The shared Dense weights across all factors might still be acceptable. \n\nI'm considering adding positivity constraints, but they may not be necessary. Adding small\
  \ batch normalization on factor outputs could introduce complications. \n\nI’ll also think about using AdamW for weight decay since it may help regularize loadings, despite prior orthogonal regularization decreasing performance. Lastly, I could explore increasing the BATCH_SIZE to 16 to help reduce gradient noise.**Examining model strategies**\n\nI’m considering memory usage with N=71721 features, where a batch of shape (B, 4, N) can quickly add up to significant amounts, especially with B=8 or B=16, which might push memory limits. \n\nOne key idea is introducing \"local linear readouts\" per neuron, mixing factors effectively. Additionally, incorporating multi-step training with teacher forcing across the horizon is also on my mind. I want to explore reparametrizing L using low-rank factoring and possibly adjusting compute_loss to emphasize early timesteps for better performance. \n\nI think adding learnable gamma for time-dependent residual mixing could enhance results. Overall, carefully\
  \ managing memory while implementing these improvements seems crucial.**Implementing new model**\n\nI want to keep the default settings while considering the training time for k=64, which should still be manageable based on previous durations. Next, I'll draft the code for a new model, RC_DualDecayFactorModel, in models.py, incorporating the necessary parameters and methods for decay and mixing coefficients.\n\nI plan to avoid batch normalization and dropout, while retaining a straightforward implementation. After setting the batch size to 8 and preparing for submission, I’ll make sure the YAML structure is clear and correctly formatted. I’ll also think about introducing hyperparameter improvements like rank_k set to 64. Let’s get to coding!"
