role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{storage write ariadne/models.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Ariadne lineage models for ZAPBench forecasting.\n  Includes:\n    - ResidualCopyHead: short-horizon AR(1)-style copy\n    - RC_DiagLDSFactorModel: low-rank factor model with diagonal LDS recurrence\n    - RC_DualDecayFactorModel: two-timescale diagonal LDS per factor, with configurable gamma schedule\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n\n  def _logit(p):\n      p = jnp.clip(p, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n      return jnp.log(p) - jnp.log(1 - p)\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          x_last = x[:, -1, :]\n          x_prev = x[:, -2, :]\n          delta = x_last - x_prev\n          def c0_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              t = jnp.arange(shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n              v = 0.2 + 0.7 * jnp.exp(-t / 8.0)  # ~0.9 -> ~0.2\n \
    \             return _logit(v).astype(dtype)\n          c0_raw = self.param('c0_raw', c0_init, (self.output_horizon,))\n          c1_raw = self.param('c1_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.output_horizon,))\n          c0 = nn.sigmoid(c0_raw)\n          c1 = 0.5 * nn.tanh(c1_raw)\n          y_copy = c0[None, :, None] * x_last[:, None, :] + c1[None, :, None] * delta[:, None, :]\n          return y_copy  # (B, 32, N)\n\n  class RC_DiagLDSFactorModel(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int = 48\n      hidden_size: int = 64\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      normalize_loadings: bool = True\n      a_max: float = 0.99\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          loadings = self.param('loadings', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, self.rank_k))\n          L = loadings / (jnp.linalg.norm(loadings, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6) if self.normalize_loadings else loadings\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,nk->btk', x, L)\n \
    \         f_flat = factors_in.reshape(B * self.rank_k, T_in)\n          f0 = nn.Dense(1, use_bias=True)(f_flat).reshape(B, self.rank_k)\n          a_raw = self.param('a_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))\n          a = self.a_max * nn.tanh(a_raw)\n          ones = jnp.ones((1, self.rank_k), dtype=a.dtype)\n          a_rows = jnp.tile(a[None, :], (self.output_horizon - 1, 1))\n          a_pows = jnp.cumprod(jnp.vstack([ones, a_rows]), axis=0)  # (32, k)\n          f_decay = f0[:, None, :] * a_pows[None, :, :]\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat); h = nn.gelu(h)\n          f_inc = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(h).reshape(B, self.rank_k, self.output_horizon).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          f_out = f_decay + f_inc\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btk,kn->btn', f_out, L.T)\n          def gamma_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              return _logit(jnp.full(shape, 0.6, dtype=dtype))\n          gamma_raw = self.param('gamma_raw', gamma_init, (self.output_horizon,))\n\
    \          gamma = nn.sigmoid(gamma_raw)[None, :, None]\n          y_factor = gamma * y_factor\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=self.output_horizon)(x)\n          return y_copy + y_factor\n\n  class RC_DualDecayFactorModel(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Residual Copy + Two-timescale Diagonal LDS factor model (per-factor slow/fast decays).\n      Adds configurable horizon-wise factor gate gamma in [gamma_start, gamma_end].\n      \"\"\"\n      rank_k: int = 64\n      hidden_size: int = 64\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      normalize_loadings: bool = True\n      a_max: float = 0.995\n      gamma_start: float = 0.35\n      gamma_end: float = 0.80\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          loadings = self.param('loadings', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, self.rank_k))\n          L = loadings / (jnp.linalg.norm(loadings, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6) if self.normalize_loadings else loadings\n\
    \          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,nk->btk', x, L)  # (B, 4, k)\n          f_flat = factors_in.reshape(B * self.rank_k, T_in)\n          f0 = nn.Dense(1, use_bias=True)(f_flat).reshape(B, self.rank_k)\n          a1_raw = self.param('a1_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))\n          a2_raw = self.param('a2_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))\n          a1 = self.a_max * nn.tanh(a1_raw)\n          a2 = self.a_max * nn.tanh(a2_raw)\n          def powers(a_vec):\n              ones = jnp.ones((1, self.rank_k), dtype=a_vec.dtype)\n              a_rows = jnp.tile(a_vec[None, :], (self.output_horizon - 1, 1))\n              return jnp.cumprod(jnp.vstack([ones, a_rows]), axis=0)  # (32, k)\n          f1 = f0[:, None, :] * powers(a1)[None, :, :]\n          f2 = f0[:, None, :] * powers(a2)[None, :, :]\n          m_raw = self.param('m_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))\n          m = nn.sigmoid(m_raw)[None, None, :]\n          f_decay = m * f1 + (1.0 - m) * f2\n\
    \          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat); h = nn.gelu(h)\n          f_inc = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(h).reshape(B, self.rank_k, self.output_horizon).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          f_out = f_decay + f_inc\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btk,kn->btn', f_out, L.T)\n          def gamma_sched_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n              g = self.gamma_start + (self.gamma_end - self.gamma_start) * t\n              return _logit(g).astype(dtype)\n          gamma_raw = self.param('gamma_raw', gamma_sched_init, (self.output_horizon,))\n          gamma = nn.sigmoid(gamma_raw)[None, :, None]\n          y_factor = gamma * y_factor\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=self.output_horizon)(x)\n          return y_copy + y_factor\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"RC-DualDecay (k=96) + Curvature Reg + Stronger Factor Gate\"\ntags: \"low-rank, state-space, dual-decay, residual, temporal-regularization,\
    \ forecasting\"\nabstract: \"Scales RC-DualDecay to k=96 and increases factor gating from 0.3→0.9 across the horizon to boost long-range accuracy. Trains with MAE + temporal curvature regularizer (L2 on second differences) and global-norm clipping. Retains residual copy head and column-normalized loadings.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import optax\n  from models import RC_DualDecayFactorModel\n  from losses import mae_with_temporal_curvature\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'rank_k': 96,\n          'hidden_size': 64,\n          'normalize_loadings': True,\n          'gamma_start': 0.3,\n          'gamma_end': 0.9\n      }\n\n  class Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, rank_k=96, hidden_size=64, normalize_loadings=True, gamma_start=0.3, gamma_end=0.9):\n          self.model = RC_DualDecayFactorModel(\n              rank_k=rank_k,\n           \
    \   hidden_size=hidden_size,\n              normalize_loadings=normalize_loadings,\n              gamma_start=gamma_start,\n              gamma_end=gamma_end\n          )\n          self.needs_rng = False\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return Wrapper(\n          rank_k=int(hparams.get('rank_k', 96)),\n          hidden_size=int(hparams.get('hidden_size', 64)),\n          normalize_loadings=bool(hparams.get('normalize_loadings', True)),\n          gamma_start=float(hparams.get('gamma_start', 0.3)),\n          gamma_end=float(hparams.get('gamma_end', 0.9))\n      )\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      # Encourage smooth long-horizon trajectories\n      return mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions,\
    \ targets, params, x, lam=1e-4)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8)\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing MAE Results**\n\nI need to respond with actions and commentary. In Research Counter, we've had several submissions. The best score achieved is a negative MAE value of -0.027506 for RC-DualDecay with k=64, which is better than the baseline of -0.029954. I realize now that a less negative MAE means a better performance. Our improved models generally show MAEs around 0.0275. The RC-LR yielded -0.027614, and today's submission with the temporal curvature regularizer is currently running. I think further experiments might be beneficial.**Exploring Model Adjustments**\n\nI'm considering some possibilities for adjustments. The DualDecay with k=64 performed best, but maybe increasing k to 96 could help. I’m aware of memory constraints since complexity is O(N*k), and with N being 71,721, k=96 would need significant resources, but it seems doable. However, training time might increase dramatically to about 45 minutes per trial.\n\nI’m also thinking of adding condition\
  \ embeddings using the dataset's condition labels. We’d need to incorporate those without altering the training loop. That could involve modifying how we create batches, but I’ll need to ensure it fits within the existing setup.**Evaluating Model Constraints**\n\nI’m realizing that in train_single evaluate, the create_batches function uses condition_labels for segmentation, but those aren't included in x, so we can’t access labels in the network. That feels limiting.\n\nI'm also considering whether per-neuron biases and AR(1) coefficients for conditional time gating could be an option, but it seems that's not feasible.\n\nOn another note, I wonder about adding normalization, perhaps LayerNorm across factors or time, as it's essential for stability. I can consider including BatchNorm, even though dropout pretraining has rules against it. \n\nAdditionally, I’m thinking about improving the factor encoders, possibly by applying more complex mappings, including 1D convolution across time. But\
  \ I notice we are already flattening across the time dimension, so that solution seems okay.**Exploring AR Model Options**\n\nI'm thinking about building a small autoregressive (AR) model over time for the factors themselves. This could involve estimating per-factor AR coefficients to produce forecasts for 32 steps through recursion, enhancing alignment with time series.\n\nI could use a dynamic factor model with autoregressive polynomials to generate specific factor coefficients from history. Implementing this with jax.lax.scan might be helpful for managing complexity.\n\nAnother option is integrating a low-rank model with learned graph adjacency, but that may be limited by memory constraints. Adding a more robust per-horizon gating schedule could also improve performance, especially for long horizons.**Planning Future Experiments**\n\nThe temporal curvature regularizer is currently running, but we don't know the outcome yet. I am considering trying rank_k=80 with dual decay and curvature\
  \ for potentially better capacity. However, I should be aware of a cooldown between submissions, even though the duration isn’t specified.\n\nI’m planning a set of ablation experiments for a paper, exploring different configurations of RC models. While we can't publish until reaching 50 ticks, we should gather results and consider caching or improving our training code, despite some restrictions. Additionally, I’m looking into methods like mean reversion and normalization within batches to reduce biases and improve our predictions. We could also explore using existing data and dimensionality reduction techniques for initialization. Overall, I'm focused on leveraging what we have to optimize our models further!**Exploring Model Improvements**\n\nI have some new ideas trying to improve predictions. Adding neuron-specific biases to the output might help, but I need to consider the potential for unnatural offsets. I want to focus on long-horizon improvements since the main criterion is the\
  \ mean negative MAE across 32 timesteps. \n\nExploring weighted mixtures of factors and adjusting gamma schedules for predictions could also provide better results. Additionally, I might use features specific to time or per-horizon gating methods in the model. I’ll keep an eye on memory usage since increasing 'k' will impact performance times. It’s essential we maintain balanced experiments while seeking improvements!**Modifying Gamma Schedule**\n\nI'm working on updating the gamma scheduling in the RC_DualDecayFactorModel to include new parameters, `gamma_start` and `gamma_end`. This should help us create a smoother decay process without breaking existing evaluations. I'll modify the model's code to add these parameters while ensuring it remains compatible with previous versions.\n\nNext, I want to define a new submission that includes temporal curvature with a better gamma range, potentially between 0.3 and 0.9. I’ll also take care not to overwrite crucial parts of the original code.\
  \ This is all about improving our accuracy moving forward!"
