tick: 21
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{storage write ariadne/models.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Ariadne lineage models for ZAPBench forecasting.\n  Includes:\n    - ResidualCopyHead: short-horizon copy with up to 2nd-order finite differences\n    - RC_AdaptiveDualDecayFactorModel: dual-decay with input-adaptive per-factor temporal gating S[b,t,k]\n      and context-adaptive decay mixture m[b,k]\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n\n  def _logit(p):\n      p = jnp.clip(p, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n      return jnp.log(p) - jnp.log(1 - p)\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Residual copy head with up to 2nd-order finite differences:\n        y_copy[b,t,n] = c0[t]*x_{-1}[b,n] + c1[t]*(x_{-1}-x_{-2}) + c2[t]*(x_{-1} - 2*x_{-2} + x_{-3})\n      c0 in [0,1], c1 in [-0.5,0.5], c2 in [-0.25,0.25]\n      \"\"\"\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x: (B, 4, N)\n          B, T_in, N =\
    \ x.shape\n          x_m1 = x[:, -1, :]            # (B, N)\n          x_m2 = x[:, -2, :]            # (B, N)\n          x_m3 = x[:, -3, :]            # (B, N)\n\n          d1 = x_m1 - x_m2\n          d2 = x_m1 - 2.0 * x_m2 + x_m3\n\n          # Initialize c0 close to copy for early steps, decaying for later steps.\n          def c0_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              t = jnp.arange(shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n              v = 0.2 + 0.7 * jnp.exp(-t / 8.0)  # ~0.9 -> ~0.2\n              return _logit(v).astype(dtype)\n\n          c0_raw = self.param('c0_raw', c0_init, (self.output_horizon,))\n          c1_raw = self.param('c1_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.output_horizon,))\n          c2_raw = self.param('c2_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.output_horizon,))\n\n          c0 = nn.sigmoid(c0_raw)            # (32,)\n          c1 = 0.5 * nn.tanh(c1_raw)         # (32,)\n          c2 = 0.25 * nn.tanh(c2_raw)        # (32,)\n\n          y_copy = (\n              c0[None,\
    \ :, None] * x_m1[:, None, :] +\n              c1[None, :, None] * d1[:, None, :] +\n              c2[None, :, None] * d2[:, None, :]\n          )\n          return y_copy  # (B, 32, N)\n\n  class RC_AdaptiveDualDecayFactorModel(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Dual-decay factor model with input-adaptive per-factor temporal gating and adaptive mixture.\n      Components:\n        - Loadings L in R^{N x k} (optional column normalization)\n        - Factors from inputs: (B, 4, N) -> (B, 4, k)\n        - f0 via per-factor linear readout from 4 inputs\n        - Two diagonal decays per factor a1,a2 in (-a_max, a_max)\n        - Context-adaptive mixture m[b,k] = sigmoid(m0[k] + m_ctx[k]*c[b,k]), where c[b,k] = mean_t factors_in[b,t,k]\n        - Tiny shared MLP to produce per-horizon increments (4 -> 32) per factor\n        - Per-sample, per-factor temporal gate S[b,t,k] = sigmoid(alpha[t,k] + beta[k] * c[b,k])\n        - Global horizon-wise gate gamma[t] in [gamma_start, gamma_end]\n   \
    \     - Residual copy head with 2nd-order finite differences\n      \"\"\"\n      rank_k: int = 128\n      hidden_size: int = 64\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      normalize_loadings: bool = True\n      a_max: float = 0.995\n      gamma_start: float = 0.25\n      gamma_end: float = 0.95\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n\n          # Loadings\n          loadings = self.param('loadings', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, self.rank_k))\n          L = loadings / (jnp.linalg.norm(loadings, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6) if self.normalize_loadings else loadings\n\n          # Project inputs to factors\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,nk->btk', x, L)  # (B, 4, k)\n          f_flat = factors_in.reshape(B * self.rank_k, T_in)\n          f0 = nn.Dense(1, use_bias=True)(f_flat).reshape(B, self.rank_k)  # (B, k)\n\n          # Two diagonal decays per factor\n          a1_raw = self.param('a1_raw', nn.initializers.zeros,\
    \ (self.rank_k,))\n          a2_raw = self.param('a2_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))\n          a1 = self.a_max * nn.tanh(a1_raw)\n          a2 = self.a_max * nn.tanh(a2_raw)\n\n          def powers(a_vec):\n              ones = jnp.ones((1, self.rank_k), dtype=a_vec.dtype)\n              a_rows = jnp.tile(a_vec[None, :], (self.output_horizon - 1, 1))\n              return jnp.cumprod(jnp.vstack([ones, a_rows]), axis=0)  # (32, k)\n\n          f1 = f0[:, None, :] * powers(a1)[None, :, :]  # (B, 32, k)\n          f2 = f0[:, None, :] * powers(a2)[None, :, :]  # (B, 32, k)\n\n          # Context for adaptivity\n          c = jnp.mean(factors_in, axis=1)  # (B, k)\n\n          # Adaptive mixture m[b,k]\n          m0 = self.param('m0', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))\n          m_ctx = self.param('m_ctx', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))\n          m_logits = m0[None, :] + m_ctx[None, :] * c  # (B, k)\n          m = nn.sigmoid(m_logits)[:, None, :]         # (B,\
    \ 1, k)\n          f_decay = m * f1 + (1.0 - m) * f2            # (B, 32, k)\n\n          # Shared per-factor increment head\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          f_inc = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(h).reshape(B, self.rank_k, self.output_horizon).transpose(0, 2, 1)  # (B,32,k)\n\n          f_out = f_decay + f_inc  # (B, 32, k)\n\n          # Input-adaptive per-factor temporal gate S[b,t,k]\n          def alpha_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              T, K = shape\n              t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, T, dtype=dtype)\n              base = 0.5 + 0.35 * t  # (T,)\n              base = jnp.tile(base[:, None], (1, K))\n              return _logit(base).astype(dtype)\n\n          alpha = self.param('s_alpha', alpha_init, (self.output_horizon, self.rank_k))  # (T, k)\n          beta = self.param('s_beta', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))  # (k,)\n\n          logits = alpha[None, :, :] + (beta[None, None, :] * c[:, None,\
    \ :])\n          S = nn.sigmoid(logits)  # (B, T, k)\n          f_out = f_out * S\n\n          # Project back to neurons\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btk,kn->btn', f_out, L.T)\n\n          # Global horizon-wise gate gamma[t]\n          def gamma_sched_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n              g = self.gamma_start + (self.gamma_end - self.gamma_start) * t\n              return _logit(g).astype(dtype)\n\n          gamma_raw = self.param('gamma_raw', gamma_sched_init, (self.output_horizon,))\n          gamma = nn.sigmoid(gamma_raw)[None, :, None]\n          y_factor = gamma * y_factor\n\n          # Residual copy head\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=self.output_horizon)(x)\n\n          return y_copy + y_factor\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Adaptive Dual-Decay v2: Contextual Mixture m[b,k] + 2nd-Order Copy Head\"\ntags: \"low-rank, state-space, dual-decay, input-adaptive,\
    \ residual, forecasting\"\nabstract: \"Builds on Adaptive Dual-Decay by making the fast/slow mixture m context-adaptive (m[b,k]) from the 4-step input factors and enhancing the residual copy head with a 2nd-order finite-difference term. Retains per-factor temporal gating S[b,t,k], curvature regularization, and gradient clipping. Rank k=128.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import optax\n  from models import RC_AdaptiveDualDecayFactorModel\n  from losses import mae_with_temporal_curvature\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'rank_k': 128,\n          'hidden_size': 64,\n          'normalize_loadings': True,\n          'gamma_start': 0.25,\n          'gamma_end': 0.95\n      }\n\n  class Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, rank_k=128, hidden_size=64, normalize_loadings=True, gamma_start=0.25, gamma_end=0.95):\n          self.model = RC_AdaptiveDualDecayFactorModel(\n\
    \              rank_k=rank_k,\n              hidden_size=hidden_size,\n              normalize_loadings=normalize_loadings,\n              gamma_start=gamma_start,\n              gamma_end=gamma_end\n          )\n          self.needs_rng = False\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return Wrapper(\n          rank_k=int(hparams.get('rank_k', 128)),\n          hidden_size=int(hparams.get('hidden_size', 64)),\n          normalize_loadings=bool(hparams.get('normalize_loadings', True)),\n          gamma_start=float(hparams.get('gamma_start', 0.25)),\n          gamma_end=float(hparams.get('gamma_end', 0.95))\n      )\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      # MAE + temporal curvature penalty for smoother long-horizon\
    \ trajectories\n      return mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=1e-4)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8)\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write ariadne/research_notes.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Ariadne I — ZAPBench Research Notes (up to Tick 21)\n  Dataset: 71,721 neurons; input 4 steps -> forecast 32 steps; metric: mean MAE over horizon.\n  \n  Baseline (System): Shared-neuron MLP (4->128->32), BN/Dropout.\n  Score: -0.029954 (Val_MAE_32 = 0.0338)\n\n  My trajectory (mean negative MAE; higher is better):\n  - ID 3: Low-Rank Factor Forecaster (k=16) — -0.030063 (worse than baseline)\n  - ID 5: RC-LR (Residual copy + low-rank) k=32 — -0.027614 (Val_MAE_32=0.0296)\n  - ID 11: RC-DiagLDS (stabilized a^t) k=48 — -0.027541 (Val_MAE_32=0.0295)\n  - ID 12: RC-DiagLDS + OrthoReg + GradClip k=32 — -0.028268\
    \ (reg hurt)\n  - ID 14: RC-DualDecay (two decays) k=64 — -0.027506 (Val_MAE_32=0.0293)\n  - ID 19: RC-DiagLDS + horizon-weighted MAE — -0.027570\n  - ID 20: RC-DualDecay + curvature reg — -0.027456\n  - ID 21: RC-DualDecay k=96 + stronger gamma — -0.027484\n  - ID 25: RC-DualDecay k=128 + per-factor temporal gating — -0.027454 (Val_MAE_32=0.0292)\n  - ID 26: Adaptive Dual-Decay k=128 + input-adaptive temporal gating S[b,t,k] — -0.027414 (best so far; Val_MAE_32=0.0292)\n\n  Key insights:\n  - Residual copy head dramatically improves early horizons; curvature regularization helps stabilize long-horizon.\n  - Dual-decay per factor (two timescales) > single decay; increasing k from 64->128 yields small but consistent gains.\n  - Orthogonality penalty on L hurt performance in this regime; horizon-weighted MAE had neutral/slight positive effect.\n  - Input-adaptive per-factor temporal gating S[b,t,k] provided the clearest improvement among recent variants.\n\n  Next hypotheses (planned ablations):\n\
    \  - Context-adaptive mixture m[b,k] for decays (fast vs slow) + 2nd-order copy head (submitted as v2).\n  - Compare k ∈ {96,128,160} under the same adaptive model (watch memory/time).\n  - Sensitivity of curvature λ ∈ {5e-5, 1e-4, 2e-4}.\n  - Gamma schedule: start ∈ {0.2, 0.3}, end ∈ {0.9, 0.98}.\n  - Remove increment MLP (pure decays) vs full model to quantify contribution.\n\n  Paper outline (when Archive opens):\n  - Title: Factorized Dual-Timescale State-Space Forecasting of Whole-Brain Activity\n  - Methods: low-rank loadings, residual copy (up to 2nd-order), dual-decay LDS, input-adaptive gating S[b,t,k], contextual mixture m[b,k].\n  - Experiments: ≥5 evaluations with seeds=3 each, full table with mean±sd MAE, step-wise MAEs at t=1,4,8,16,32.\n  - Ablations: remove adaptive S, remove contextual mixture, k-sweep, curvature λ-sweep, gamma schedule sweep.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating model improvements**


  I need to decide on our next steps in the Research Counter. We''ve been improving our model’s performance, as our scores have improved from a baseline of -0.02995 to about -0.02741. A lower negative score indicates better performance because it’s mean absolute error (MAE). So, that''s an improvement! However, I can''t publish our findings in the Archive Room just yet since it’s unavailable for 50 ticks. But I can definitely prep memory notes in the Private Memory Room!**Identifying improvement opportunities**


  I’m considering further enhancements to our model. Currently, we’re using factor models with a low-rank loading matrix and implementing gating and curvature regularization. One idea is to incorporate condition labels into the model. It seems like they’re mentioned in `train_single.py` with the ZAPBench data, but labels don’t appear to be included in batches. I might need to modify the training code to pass labels into the network. However, the specifications may not allow custom training loops. It’s a bit tricky!**Exploring model enhancements**


  It looks like directly using condition labels won’t work as they’re not available in the compute_loss function. However, maybe I can approximate the condition effect by creating a global dictionary for per-neuron scalings as part of the network state. Another idea is to include time-lag features instead of keeping the L constant. By leveraging multiple input steps and incorporating differences and second derivatives, I could define a transform from input to factors. It''s worth considering how to adjust our projection code accordingly!**Considering alternative enhancements**


  I think it''s fine to explore new approaches. One idea is to incorporate global coupling across neurons using a graph structure. We don’t have an adjacency matrix, but maybe I can approximate this with spatial smoothing across the columns of L to encourage sparsity. Alternatively, I could consider a balanced mixture-of-experts model where we learn neuron clusters as factors. By adding nonlinear decoding, I could have the loadings generate features that then use per-neuron gating. This sounds promising!**Evaluating potential enhancements**


  I’m considering various strategies to improve our model. I could explore using global coupling through a graph structure, but that might be complex. Another idea is to implement diagonalization using spectral smoothing for more efficient factor output. I could also apply teacher forcing with varying horizon weighting to further reduce tail errors. Plus, I’ve thought about multi-head gating for adaptive factor columns, but it may complicate things. I can also incorporate regularization techniques to encourage column-sparsity. I think I’ll note these ideas down in Private Memory!**Assessing potential improvements**


  I’m thinking about how to address the limitations of compute_loss, especially regarding accessing gating betas. There’s also the idea of using a Toeplitz/Chebyshev basis for dynamic mapping instead of typical increments, which could help. I might want to implement horizon-specific coefficients and even consider second-order differences for better modeling. Plus, skip connections could provide direct effects across horizons, and normalizing L columns through Softmax may aid specialization. However, I have reservations about potential degradation. Lastly, I could explore adding a diversity penalty across outputs to reduce redundancy.**Exploring gated mixtures and modifications**


  I''m considering a new idea: implementing an ensemble across decays using a gating mixture network that factors in context. This would add adaptivity, refining the mixture m based on both the context and time. I think we can implement the RC_AdaptiveDualDecayFactorModel to support this. I also plan to add a second difference for residuals to enhance forecasting. There''s consideration about training times and memory, but with adjustments, I believe it will work. I''ll create notes to summarize everything and prepare for submissions later.'
