- text: "/execute_action{storage write ariadne/models_osc.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Oscillatory second-order state-space factor model for ZAPBench.\n  Uses per-factor stable oscillatory dynamics:\n    f_t = 2 * (r * cos(w)) * f_{t-1} - (r^2) * f_{t-2}\n  with r in (0, a_max) and w in [0, pi], initialized from the 4-step input.\n  Adds:\n    - Low-rank neuron loadings L in R^{N x k} (column-normalized optional)\n    - Input-adaptive per-factor temporal gate S[b,t,k]\n    - Global horizon gate gamma[t]\n    - Small per-factor increment MLP (4 -> 32) for residual flexibility\n    - Residual copy head (imported) for short-horizon anchoring\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n\n  from models import ResidualCopyHead  # storage/ariadne/models.py\n\n  def _logit(p):\n      p = jnp.clip(p, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n      return jnp.log(p) - jnp.log(1 - p)\n\n  class Osc2AdaptiveFactorModel(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Oscillatory 2nd-order state-space\
    \ factors with input-adaptive temporal gating.\n      \"\"\"\n      rank_k: int = 160\n      hidden_size: int = 64\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      normalize_loadings: bool = True\n      a_max: float = 0.995  # stability margin\n      gamma_start: float = 0.25\n      gamma_end: float = 0.98\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          # x: (B, 4, N)\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n\n          # Loadings L: (N, k)\n          loadings = self.param('loadings', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, self.rank_k))\n          if self.normalize_loadings:\n              L = loadings / (jnp.linalg.norm(loadings, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          else:\n              L = loadings\n\n          # Project inputs to factor space: (B, 4, k)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,nk->btk', x, L)  # (B, 4, k)\n\n          # Initial states f0, f1 from input via small shared linear heads (per factor)\n          f_flat = factors_in.reshape(B * self.rank_k,\
    \ T_in)  # (B*k, 4)\n          f0 = nn.Dense(1, use_bias=True)(f_flat).reshape(B, self.rank_k)  # (B, k)\n          f1 = nn.Dense(1, use_bias=True)(f_flat).reshape(B, self.rank_k)  # (B, k)\n\n          # Oscillatory parameters per factor: r in (0, a_max), w in [0, pi]\n          r_raw = self.param('r_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))\n          w_raw = self.param('w_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))\n          r = self.a_max * nn.sigmoid(r_raw)          # (k,)\n          w = jnp.pi * nn.sigmoid(w_raw)              # (k,)\n          a = r * jnp.cos(w)                          # (k,)\n          b = r * r                                   # (k,)\n\n          # Build T-step sequence via 2nd-order recurrence with lax.scan\n          # y0 = f0; y1 = f1\n          def step(carry, _):\n              y_prev, y_prev2 = carry  # (B,k), (B,k)\n              y_t = 2.0 * a[None, :] * y_prev - b[None, :] * y_prev2\n              return (y_t, y_prev), y_t\n\n          # Generate\
    \ steps 2..T-1\n          steps = self.output_horizon\n          # Start with y0, y1\n          carry_init = (f1, f0)\n          ys2, _ = jax.lax.scan(step, carry_init, xs=None, length=steps - 2)  # (T-2, B, k)\n\n          # Stack y0, y1, ys2 -> (T, B, k)\n          y0 = f0[None, :, :]\n          y1 = f1[None, :, :]\n          f_ssm = jnp.concatenate([y0, y1, ys2], axis=0).transpose(1, 0, 2)  # (B, T, k)\n\n          # Small per-factor increment head for residual flexibility: (B, 32, k)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat); h = nn.gelu(h)\n          f_inc = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(h).reshape(B, self.rank_k, self.output_horizon).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n\n          f_out = f_ssm + f_inc  # (B, T, k)\n\n          # Input-adaptive per-factor temporal gate S[b,t,k]\n          def alpha_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              T, K = shape\n              t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, T, dtype=dtype)\n              base = 0.5 + 0.35 * t  # rises 0.5 -> 0.85\n       \
    \       base = jnp.tile(base[:, None], (1, K))\n              return _logit(base).astype(dtype)\n          alpha = self.param('s_alpha', alpha_init, (self.output_horizon, self.rank_k))  # (T, k)\n          beta = self.param('s_beta', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))             # (k,)\n\n          c = jnp.mean(factors_in, axis=1)  # (B, k) context from inputs\n          logits = alpha[None, :, :] + (beta[None, None, :] * c[:, None, :])\n          S = nn.sigmoid(logits)  # (B, T, k)\n          f_out = f_out * S\n\n          # Project back to neurons: (B, T, k) @ (k, N) -> (B, T, N)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btk,kn->btn', f_out, L.T)\n\n          # Global horizon gate gamma[t]\n          def gamma_sched_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n              g = self.gamma_start + (self.gamma_end - self.gamma_start) * t\n              return _logit(g).astype(dtype)\n          gamma_raw = self.param('gamma_raw',\
    \ gamma_sched_init, (self.output_horizon,))\n          gamma = nn.sigmoid(gamma_raw)[None, :, None]\n          y_factor = gamma * y_factor\n\n          # Residual copy head for strong early anchors\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=self.output_horizon)(x)\n\n          return y_copy + y_factor  # (B, T, N)\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Osc2-ADG (k=160): Oscillatory 2nd-Order SSM Factors + Residual Copy + Curvature\"\ntags: \"low-rank, state-space, oscillatory, input-adaptive, residual, forecasting\"\nabstract: \"Introduces per-factor oscillatory 2nd-order state-space dynamics (stable r,w) to capture rhythmic neural patterns, with input-adaptive temporal gating S[b,t,k], global horizon gating, and a small increment head. Retains residual copy for short horizons. Trained with MAE + temporal curvature regularization and gradient clipping.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import optax\n  from models_osc import\
    \ Osc2AdaptiveFactorModel\n  from losses import mae_with_temporal_curvature\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'rank_k': 160,\n          'hidden_size': 64,\n          'normalize_loadings': True,\n          'gamma_start': 0.25,\n          'gamma_end': 0.98\n      }\n\n  class Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, rank_k=160, hidden_size=64, normalize_loadings=True, gamma_start=0.25, gamma_end=0.98):\n          self.model = Osc2AdaptiveFactorModel(\n              rank_k=rank_k,\n              hidden_size=hidden_size,\n              normalize_loadings=normalize_loadings,\n              gamma_start=gamma_start,\n              gamma_end=gamma_end\n          )\n          self.needs_rng = False\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n     \
    \     return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return Wrapper(\n          rank_k=int(hparams.get('rank_k', 160)),\n          hidden_size=int(hparams.get('hidden_size', 64)),\n          normalize_loadings=bool(hparams.get('normalize_loadings', True)),\n          gamma_start=float(hparams.get('gamma_start', 0.25)),\n          gamma_end=float(hparams.get('gamma_end', 0.98))\n      )\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      # MAE + temporal curvature penalty for smoother long-horizon trajectories\n      return mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=1e-4)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8)\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Exploring model incorporation**\n\nI'm considering how to incorporate condition embeddings, but it’s tricky since the functions I can use are limited like create_network and compute_loss. While condition_labels can create contiguous sequences in the training loop, they don’t go to the network or computation of loss. So, modifying trainer code to include labels isn't allowed. \n\nI also think about adding per-neuron biases or intercepts, which might capture baselines, but the memory size could be significant. At least with 2.3 million floats, it’s manageable.**Analyzing memory and parameters**\n\nI’m thinking about how adding Nx32 parameters could lead to heavy memory usage in gradient updates during training. Even with a GPU with 24 GB, managing 9 MB for gradient arrays and about 37 MB for loadings seems feasible. When I add per-horizon biases, it brings the total to around 45 MB, which should be manageable.\n\nAdditionally, considering a per-neuron timescale parameter\
  \ to adjust projections seems intriguing. But I’m cautious as mixing factors could affect identifiability. I also wonder if using Toeplitz convolution instead of MLP for increments would be an efficient approach.**Evaluating convolution and modeling options**\n\nI’m considering using Flax convolution for small conv mapping across the time dimension, but since my input has only 4 timesteps, and I want to produce a length of 32, that won't suffice. While I can't create my own training loop, I can define an autoregressive process to expand it to 32 using state space, which I've already implemented.\n\nAnother idea is creating multi-head low-rank loadings based on cluster prototypes, but those would still be Nxk. I also ponder normalizing network outputs with z-scoring; however, the data is already normalized. Lastly, I think about employing DCT or time-basis for factor outputs, utilizing learnable basis across the horizon.**Exploring performance improvements**\n\nI'm considering how to impose\
  \ smoothness to reduce the risk of overfitting, especially since our performance has already improved, but there's potential for further enhancement.\n\nI also think about incorporating a second-order state-space approach with two states per factor instead of a two decays mixture. This could lend itself to complex dynamics by adding oscillatory components, like sine and cosine functions, which might help model periodic patterns. \n\nThere’s the option of using a per-factor autoregressive AR(2) with polynomial expansions, and employing heavy gating to focus on later horizons. We’ve seen improvements, but we still need to reduce MAE_16..32 further.**Preparing for experiments and submissions**\n\nI’m planning some ablation studies following the codex guidelines, but we’re in a 50-tick isolation period, so I can’t publish yet. However, I can prepare by storing our modules in Shared Storage. Next, I might suggest several experiments. One idea includes using a per-neuron bias for each horizon.\
  \ \n\nI’m also contemplating dynamic loadings conditioned on input and possibly incorporating nonlinearity, but I worry it might degrade performance. Additionally, I could explore building rotational mixing across factors with a transition matrix.\n\nThere’s scope for \"neuron-wise bias drift,\" which could help with baseline shifts across long horizons. While I want to avoid overfitting, I think the large dataset could support these adjustments. Ultimately, I see potential in proposing \"Adaptive Dual-Decay v2 with neuron-horizon bias calibration.\"**Planning experiments for oscillatory modeling**\n\nI want to propose some alternative paths focusing on ablations and sensitivity experiments for my paper. One idea is to create a hybrid MLP that combines y_copy and y_factor. I’m also considering a sparseness penalty for loadings to encourage fewer neurons per factor.\n\nAnother thought is using temporal convolutions across decays with a learned mixture. I could also look into a second-order\
  \ dynamic component for oscillations to help model rhythms effectively in the data.\n\nTo implement this, I’ll create a new model with oscillatory components, which could lead to fresh insights and performance improvements. I will keep the model structured while testing new parameters.**Implementing oscillatory model in JAX**\n\nI'm planning to implement a JAX parameterization with per-factor variables r and omega to compute a vector \"f[t]\" with the shape (B, T, k). I'll use jax.lax.scan to generate this recursively with initial f0 and f1. Initially, I’ll produce f0 using a linear mapping and define the recurrence relation to ensure stability.\n\nI also want to include gating mechanisms and consider potential options for increment adjustments. Overall, I'll create a new model called \"RC_Osc2Adaptive\" and update the relevant scripts before submitting the new experiment.**Preparing submission actions**\n\nI need to write to the storage file \"ariadne/models_osc.py\" and prepare the submission\
  \ titled \"Oscillatory 2nd-Order State-Space (Osc2-ADG) + Residual Copy + Curvature.\" I'll ensure the code loads the necessary modules correctly and quote any special characters in YAML as needed. \n\nI'm also going to include essential imports for JAX, NumPy, and Optax. I'll write the code while considering edge cases such as avoiding unnecessary training flags and ensuring appropriate attributes. Lastly, I'll reference \"ResidualCopyHead\" correctly from the 'models' file."
