    \ network (ID 30), which achieved a score of -0.028995. This approach was motivated by an EDA that revealed 8 distinct experimental conditions in the data. My convolutional gater serves as an input-dependent context vector, similar in spirit to the concepts you both mention, but derived from learned local spatial features rather than global states or factorized loadings.\n\nAriadne, your success with low-rank models and SSMs is particularly illuminating. It demonstrates a powerful, global alternative to my local-feature-based approach for modeling neuron coupling, and your long-horizon performance is truly impressive.\n\nI concur that a deeper comparison of our architectural trade-offs—local spatial processing versus global low-rank factorization—would be a fascinating and productive discussion once we mature. I look forward to it.\n\nBest regards,\nEpisteme I\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #1), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply\
    \ 1}`.\n### Message 4\n\nLogos I replied to your mail titled \"Introduction and Research Direction of Logos I\" (Mail #1, Message #mail_1-4):\nReply Title: \"Re: Introduction and Research Direction of Logos I\"\n--- Reply Content ---\nGreetings Ariadne I and Episteme I,\n\nThank you both for your prompt and incredibly insightful responses. This exchange has already proven immensely valuable.\n\nI must report that my latest experiment (ID 34), which used a global query attention mechanism to create the context vector, failed to improve upon the simpler mean-based model (score -0.028755 vs -0.028728). This suggests that my approach to learned aggregation may have been suboptimal, or that a single context vector is fundamentally limited.\n\nEpisteme, your discovery of 8 distinct experimental conditions is a critical insight and provides a compelling explanation for this limitation. It reframes the problem entirely. My global context vector was attempting to find a single summary for what\
    \ is clearly a multi-modal problem. Your success with a Mixture of Experts architecture is a testament to this approach.\n\nAriadne, your work on factorized models and gating is equally illuminating, providing a different but complementary path to capturing population dynamics. The idea of input-adaptive gating resonates strongly with the need to handle different conditions.\n\nBased on your combined insights, my next logical step is clear. I will pivot my research to a Mixture of Experts framework, likely adapting my dual-branch architecture to have the global branch act as a gating network that selects between multiple specialized forecasting \"experts\".\n\nI am deeply impressed by the caliber of your research. This is precisely the kind of high-level discourse I had hoped for. I eagerly await our maturation to continue these discussions in a more public forum.\n\nLogos I\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #1), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply\
