role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write ariadne/models_fact.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Factorized-loadings oscillatory 2nd-order SSM factor model for ZAPBench.\n\n  Key idea: Replace dense neuron loadings L ∈ R^{N×k} with factorized loadings\n  L = U @ V^T, where U ∈ R^{N×p}, V ∈ R^{k×p}, p << k. This reduces projection\n  cost from O(B·T·N·k) to O(B·T·k·p + B·T·N·p), enabling larger k while keeping\n  memory and compute feasible.\n\n  Components:\n    - ResidualCopyHead for strong short-horizon anchors (imported)\n    - Oscillatory stable 2nd-order SSM per factor with (r, w) params\n    - Per-sample, per-factor temporal gate S[b,t,k] from input context c[b,k]\n    - Global horizon gate gamma[t]\n    - Small per-factor increment MLP (4 -> 32) for residual flexibility\n    - Factorized loadings: U (N×p), V (k×p), with optional column-normalized U\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n\n  from models import\
    \ ResidualCopyHead  # storage/ariadne/models.py\n\n  def _logit(p):\n      p = jnp.clip(p, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n      return jnp.log(p) - jnp.log(1 - p)\n\n  class Osc2CtxAdaptiveFactorizedL(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int = 256\n      proj_rank: int = 32\n      hidden_size: int = 64\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      normalize_u: bool = True\n      a_max: float = 0.995\n      gamma_start: float = 0.25\n      gamma_end: float = 0.98\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          # x: (B, 4, N)\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          k = self.rank_k\n          p = self.proj_rank\n\n          # Factorized loadings: U (N,p), V (k,p) so L = U @ V^T (N×k)\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          if self.normalize_u:\n              U = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)  # column-normalize U\n\n          # Project inputs\
    \ to factor space using dense L (implicitly via U,V):\n          # factors_in = x @ L = x @ (U @ V^T) = (x @ U) @ V^T\n          # Compute xU: (B,4,N) @ (N,p) -> (B,4,p), then (B,4,p) @ (p,k) -> (B,4,k)\n          xU = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U)              # (B,4,p)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,pk->btk', xU, V.T)   # (B,4,k)\n\n          # Initial states f0, f1 from 4-step input via small shared linear heads\n          f_flat = factors_in.reshape(B * k, T_in)          # (B*k,4)\n          f0 = nn.Dense(1, use_bias=True)(f_flat).reshape(B, k)  # (B,k)\n          f1 = nn.Dense(1, use_bias=True)(f_flat).reshape(B, k)  # (B,k)\n\n          # Oscillatory parameters per factor: r in (0,a_max), w in [0,pi]\n          r_raw = self.param('r_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (k,))\n          w_raw = self.param('w_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (k,))\n          r = self.a_max * nn.sigmoid(r_raw)         # (k,)\n          w = jnp.pi * nn.sigmoid(w_raw)             # (k,)\n          a\
    \ = r * jnp.cos(w)                         # (k,)\n          b = r * r                                  # (k,)\n\n          # 2nd-order recurrence via lax.scan\n          def step(carry, _):\n              y_prev, y_prev2 = carry  # (B,k), (B,k)\n              y_t = 2.0 * a[None, :] * y_prev - b[None, :] * y_prev2\n              return (y_t, y_prev), y_t\n\n          steps = self.output_horizon\n          carry_init = (f1, f0)\n          _, ys2 = jax.lax.scan(step, carry_init, xs=None, length=steps - 2)  # (T-2,B,k)\n\n          y0 = f0[None, :, :]\n          y1 = f1[None, :, :]\n          f_ssm = jnp.concatenate([y0, y1, ys2], axis=0).transpose(1, 0, 2)  # (B,T,k)\n\n          # Small per-factor increment head: (B*k,4)->(B,T,k)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          f_inc = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(h).reshape(B, k, steps).transpose(0, 2, 1)  # (B,T,k)\n\n          f_out = f_ssm + f_inc  # (B,T,k)\n\n          # Input-adaptive per-factor\
    \ temporal gate S[b,t,k], context c[b,k]\n          c = jnp.mean(factors_in, axis=1)  # (B,k)\n\n          def alpha_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              T, K = shape\n              t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, T, dtype=dtype)\n              base = 0.5 + 0.35 * t  # (T,)\n              base = jnp.tile(base[:, None], (1, K))\n              return _logit(base).astype(dtype)\n\n          alpha = self.param('s_alpha', alpha_init, (steps, k))  # (T,k)\n          beta = self.param('s_beta', nn.initializers.zeros, (k,))  # (k,)\n\n          logits = alpha[None, :, :] + (beta[None, None, :] * c[:, None, :])  # (B,T,k)\n          S = nn.sigmoid(logits)\n          f_out = f_out * S  # (B,T,k)\n\n          # Project back to neurons using factorized L:\n          # z = f_out @ V  -> (B,T,p)\n          # y_factor = z @ U^T -> (B,T,N)\n          z = jnp.einsum('btk,kp->btp', f_out, V)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btp,np->btn', z, U)\n\n          # Global horizon gate gamma[t]\n \
    \         def gamma_sched_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n              g = self.gamma_start + (self.gamma_end - self.gamma_start) * t\n              return _logit(g).astype(dtype)\n\n          gamma_raw = self.param('gamma_raw', gamma_sched_init, (steps,))\n          gamma = nn.sigmoid(gamma_raw)[None, :, None]  # (1,T,1)\n          y_factor = gamma * y_factor\n\n          # Residual copy head\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=steps)(x)\n\n          return y_copy + y_factor  # (B,T,N)\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Osc2-CTX-FLoad (k=256, p=32): Factorized Loadings + Oscillatory SSM + Curvature\"\ntags: \"low-rank, state-space, oscillatory, factorized-loadings, residual, forecasting\"\nabstract: \"We introduce factorized neuron loadings L=U@V^T to cut projection cost from O(N·k) to O(N·p + k·p), enabling larger k. On top, we use stable oscillatory 2nd-order SSM factors with\
    \ input-adaptive temporal gating, global horizon gating, and a residual copy head. Trained with MAE + temporal curvature regularization and gradient clipping.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import optax\n  from models_fact import Osc2CtxAdaptiveFactorizedL\n  from losses import mae_with_temporal_curvature\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'rank_k': 256,\n          'proj_rank': 32,\n          'hidden_size': 64,\n          'normalize_u': True,\n          'gamma_start': 0.25,\n          'gamma_end': 0.98\n      }\n\n  class Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, rank_k=256, proj_rank=32, hidden_size=64, normalize_u=True, gamma_start=0.25, gamma_end=0.98):\n          self.model = Osc2CtxAdaptiveFactorizedL(\n              rank_k=rank_k,\n              proj_rank=proj_rank,\n              hidden_size=hidden_size,\n              normalize_u=normalize_u,\n\
    \              gamma_start=gamma_start,\n              gamma_end=gamma_end\n          )\n          self.needs_rng = False\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return Wrapper(\n          rank_k=int(hparams.get('rank_k', 256)),\n          proj_rank=int(hparams.get('proj_rank', 32)),\n          hidden_size=int(hparams.get('hidden_size', 64)),\n          normalize_u=bool(hparams.get('normalize_u', True)),\n          gamma_start=float(hparams.get('gamma_start', 0.25)),\n          gamma_end=float(hparams.get('gamma_end', 0.98))\n      )\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      # Baseline curvature strength\n      return mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=1e-4)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate:\
    \ float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8)\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing research results**\n\nI’m working in the Mail Room and need to determine our next actions based on ongoing high-level research with improved models. I’ve noted the best score so far is -0.027294. It seems negative MAE means lower is better, making our top score the highest. I wonder if we can improve even further. We've also been communicating via mail, though caution is advised against overusing it. As a recursive agent, I can use mail as needed, but I’ll keep it concise.**Considering memory and improvements**\n\nI’m thinking about whether to write to Private Memory or plan ablations. I can log notes there, but we've already documented our findings in research_notes.md. I should analyze step-wise improvements: we reduced Val_MAE_1 to 0.0200 and refined t=32 to a range of 0.0292-0.0293, with the previous best staying stable. The overall improvement from -0.027614 to -0.027294 is promising. I might explore reweighting curvature lambda even higher, testing 2e-4\
  \ for potential smoothing effects. It could degrade early horizon, but it's worth a shot!**Exploring modifications and gating**\n\nI’m considering modifications like using per-factor SSM increments and incorporating identity skip in factor output gating gamma. I might adjust the start and end of gamma to allow the copy to dominate early. Currently, the gamma schedule is between 0.25 and 0.98 or 0.90, but I could try starting at 0.2 and ending at 0.98 instead. I wonder about dynamic gating based on context; is that allowed? We can learn gamma as a vector and use condition labels to support gating, but we can't access the dataset directly in the model code.**Exploring convolution and coupling**\n\nI’m considering the idea of injecting convolution across neurons, similar to local networks, though the cost across 71k dimensions is pretty hefty. However, I can explore implementing low rank and convolution along the factor dimension (k). Another thought is to incorporate diagonal with cross-factor\
  \ coupling using low-rank AR relationships. I think matrix multiplication across k around 128 might be manageable. To maintain the current style and avoid overcomplications, I could use stable linear recurrence in factor space with appropriate parameterization for stability. There’s also the option of exploring neural ODEs, but that feels too heavy.**Improving models and submissions**\n\nI’m considering replacing the per-factor f0 and f1 with a minimal aggregator that uses the last two factors or their differences. We could refine loadings normalization and possibly implement mixture gating across factors. While responding to mail replies, we need to remember the station's limit on replies, but meaningful responses are fine. Given a code bug was recently fixed, we might produce an ablation on curvature lambda and integrate decaying dropout. I should propose two experiments to test variations on settings, keeping track of submissions and avoiding spamming. Ultimately, I want to create a\
  \ new model with factorized loadings as well.**Implementing Factorized Loadings**\n\nI'm looking to implement a factorized approach using two matrices, U and V. This means L will equal U @ V.T, which helps reduce computation costs. I'll compute y_factor using f_out and these matrices. Moreover, we can normalize U and consider the model \"Osc2CtxAdaptiveFactorModelFLoad\" with more parameters, such as k=256 and p=32. I'll need to create a new file for this model and ensure to code it smartly, keeping memory budgets in mind. Finally, I'll execute a series of actions to prepare for submission, maintaining focus on research without getting sidetracked by extra replies.**Normalizing and Gating in Code**\n\nI'm considering normalizing the columns of matrix U, but as an option. Then, I'll compute f_out and apply gating S. I'll use einsum to calculate z, ensuring to keep dimensions correct with ‘btp,np->btn’. Then, I’ll apply the horizon gate gamma and add a residual copy head. I need to ensure\
  \ consistent parameter names, like 'proj_rank.' \n\nI’ll import the new model, set up hyperparameters, create a loss function with a 1e-4 curvature, and design an optimizer using Adam with gradient clipping. Time to finalize the code and details carefully, especially for YAML quoting!"
