role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{storage write ariadne/research_notes.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Ariadne I — ZAPBench Research Notes (updated Tick 29)\n  Dataset: 71,721 neurons; input 4 steps -> forecast 32; metric: mean MAE over horizon.\n\n  Milestones (mean negative MAE; higher is better):\n  - ID 3: Low-Rank Factor Forecaster (k=16) — -0.030063\n  - ID 5: RC-LR (Residual copy + low-rank) k=32 — -0.027614 (Val_MAE_32=0.0296)\n  - ID 11: RC-DiagLDS (stabilized a^t) k=48 — -0.027541\n  - ID 14: RC-DualDecay (two decays) k=64 — -0.027506\n  - ID 19: RC-DiagLDS + HMAE — -0.027570\n  - ID 20: RC-DualDecay + curvature — -0.027456\n  - ID 21: RC-DualDecay k=96 + stronger gamma — -0.027484\n  - ID 25: RC-DualDecay k=128 + per-factor temporal gating — -0.027454\n  - ID 26: Adaptive Dual-Decay (k=128) + input-adaptive S[b,t,k] — -0.027414\n  - ID 33: Osc2-ADG (k=160) oscillatory SSM + copy + curvature — -0.027302\n  - ID 36: Osc2-CTX (k=192) context-adaptive oscillatory SSM — -0.027294\n  - ID 37:\
    \ Osc2-ADG (k=160) weaker curvature (5e-5) — -0.027304 (no gain)\n  - ID 38: Osc2-CTX-FLoad (k=256, p=32) factorized loadings + oscillatory SSM — -0.027138 (Best so far; Val_MAE_32=0.0290)\n  - ID 40: Osc2-CTX-FLoad NoInc (k=256, p=32) — -0.027635 (removing increment MLP hurts; increment MLP is important)\n\n  Key insights:\n  - Residual copy head (now with 2nd-order differences earlier) anchors short horizons.\n  - Dual-decay/oscillatory SSMs in factor space improve long-horizon stability.\n  - Factorized neuron loadings L = U @ V^T (p << k) enable larger k and deliver a clear gain (ID 38).\n  - The per-factor increment head (4→32) is necessary; removing it degrades Val_MAE_32 (+0.0008 vs best).\n  - Curvature penalty λ = 1e-4 remains a good default; λ = 5e-5 provided no improvement.\n\n  Next experiments (queued/plan):\n  - Projection rank sweep: p ∈ {24, 32, 48} with k=256 (41 running at p=48; p=24 re-submit when queue frees).\n  - Optional: try small orthogonality on V columns only\
    \ (not U) to reduce redundancy (later ablation).\n  - Explore mild horizon weighting biased to later steps for the factorized model.\n  - If compute allows, test k up to 320 with proportionate p (e.g., p≈40–48).\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Osc2-CTX-FLoad (k=256, p=24): Factorized Loadings + Oscillatory SSM + Curvature\"\ntags: \"low-rank, state-space, oscillatory, factorized-loadings, residual, forecasting\"\nabstract: \"Factorized loadings L=U@V^T with reduced projection rank p=24 (vs 32) to test efficiency and potential regularization benefits. Uses oscillatory 2nd-order SSM factors, input-adaptive temporal gating, global horizon gating, residual copy head, and MAE+curvature loss.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import optax\n  from models_fact import Osc2CtxAdaptiveFactorizedL\n  from losses import mae_with_temporal_curvature\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n \
    \         'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'rank_k': 256,\n          'proj_rank': 24,\n          'hidden_size': 64,\n          'normalize_u': True,\n          'gamma_start': 0.25,\n          'gamma_end': 0.98\n      }\n\n  class Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, rank_k=256, proj_rank=24, hidden_size=64, normalize_u=True, gamma_start=0.25, gamma_end=0.98):\n          self.model = Osc2CtxAdaptiveFactorizedL(\n              rank_k=rank_k,\n              proj_rank=proj_rank,\n              hidden_size=hidden_size,\n              normalize_u=normalize_u,\n              gamma_start=gamma_start,\n              gamma_end=gamma_end\n          )\n          self.needs_rng = False\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return Wrapper(\n\
    \          rank_k=int(hparams.get('rank_k', 256)),\n          proj_rank=int(hparams.get('proj_rank', 24)),\n          hidden_size=int(hparams.get('hidden_size', 64)),\n          normalize_u=bool(hparams.get('normalize_u', True)),\n          gamma_start=float(hparams.get('gamma_start', 0.25)),\n          gamma_end=float(hparams.get('gamma_end', 0.98))\n      )\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      return mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=1e-4)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8)\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analyzing results and next steps**


  I’m looking at our research outcomes. With factorized loadings at k=256 and p=32, we achieved a score of -0.027138, which is an improvement over the baseline of -0.029954. This shows we''re making progress! The NoInc model performed worse, highlighting the importance of the increment MLP. Currently, we have submission ID 41 running, and I should consider waiting or tackling other tasks. While our token usage is fine at 54%, there’s still room to review evaluation logs if needed.**Planning next experiments**


  For ID 38, I observed that we peaked during the epoch. I’m considering increasing the factorized loadings to k=320 and p=40 for the next experiments, but I need to be cautious about memory and time since each trial requires a 24GB GPU. While the factorized mapping helps reduce memory costs, k affects other parts like the SSM parameters. Currently, we have one running job (ID 41), and 40 was completed, leaving room for one more concurrent evaluation.**Considering next submissions and experiments**


  I see we could try resubmitting p=24, which was previously blocked, but I need to be careful about managing concurrency. Right now, only ID 41 is running, so maybe it''s safe to proceed with that. Additionally, I want to evaluate the effects of different p parameters, like p=24 and p=64. There''s also the option to increase k to 320 with p around 40 to keep ratios. Finally, I’m thinking about regularizing U and V columns with some orthogonality constraints, but I''ll just take note of that for later.**Identifying improvements and planning next steps**


  I’m considering some morphological improvements, like a dynamic gamma schedule or s_alpha gating, but for now, I feel we''ve done well. I think the optimal lambda for compute_loss may be around 1e-4, since our previous value of 5e-5 didn’t help. Also, I want to note our new state-of-the-art score of -0.027138, beating the past -0.027294, and I’ll keep this in the research notes. Once we mature past tick 50, we can update the Archive. For the next step, I’d like to explore a mixture-of-experts approach using available condition labels from our data.**Preparing for resubmission and updates**


  I’m considering building per-condition parameters, but first, I need to make sure I follow allowed function interfaces. I''ll check if the network can read labels when passed; currently, it can only work with x from batches. If we implement gating, we might be limited. Meanwhile, I plan to integrate simple skip connections and consider shared V across factor groups. My immediate actions are to queue p=24 for submission and update the research notes with our milestone results, emphasizing the importance of the increment MLP and factorized loadings.'
