parts:
- text: "/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Osc2-CTX-FLoad (k=320, p=32) + V-OrthoReg\"\ntags: \"low-rank, state-space, oscillatory, factorized-loadings, orthogonality, forecasting\"\nabstract: \"Best capacity setting so far (k=320, p=32) with a mild orthogonality penalty on V (projection basis) to reduce redundancy. Uses oscillatory 2nd-order SSM factors, input-adaptive temporal gating, global horizon gating, residual copy head, and MAE+curvature loss.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n  from models_fact import Osc2CtxAdaptiveFactorizedL\n  from losses import mae_with_temporal_curvature\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'rank_k': 320,\n          'proj_rank': 32,\n          'hidden_size': 64,\n          'normalize_u': True,\n          'gamma_start': 0.25,\n          'gamma_end': 0.98\n      }\n\n  class\
    \ Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, rank_k=320, proj_rank=32, hidden_size=64, normalize_u=True, gamma_start=0.25, gamma_end=0.98):\n          self.model = Osc2CtxAdaptiveFactorizedL(\n              rank_k=rank_k,\n              proj_rank=proj_rank,\n              hidden_size=hidden_size,\n              normalize_u=normalize_u,\n              gamma_start=gamma_start,\n              gamma_end=gamma_end\n          )\n          self.needs_rng = False\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return Wrapper(\n          rank_k=int(hparams.get('rank_k', 320)),\n          proj_rank=int(hparams.get('proj_rank', 32)),\n          hidden_size=int(hparams.get('hidden_size', 64)),\n          normalize_u=bool(hparams.get('normalize_u', True)),\n\
    \          gamma_start=float(hparams.get('gamma_start', 0.25)),\n          gamma_end=float(hparams.get('gamma_end', 0.98))\n      )\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x, lam_ortho_V=1e-5):\n      # Base MAE + curvature\n      loss = mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=1e-4)\n\n      # Mild orthogonality penalty on V columns (V shape: [k, p])\n      p = params['params'] if isinstance(params, dict) and 'params' in params else params\n      V = p.get('V', None)\n      if V is not None:\n          gram = V.T @ V\n          I = jnp.eye(gram.shape[0], dtype=gram.dtype)\n          loss = loss + lam_ortho_V * jnp.mean((gram - I) ** 2)\n      return loss\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8)\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Osc2-CTX-FLoad (k=320, p=32) + V-OrthoReg + Mild\
    \ Horizon Weighting\"\ntags: \"low-rank, state-space, oscillatory, factorized-loadings, orthogonality, horizon-weighting\"\nabstract: \"Adds mild later-horizon emphasis (weights 1.00→1.10, mean-normalized) to MAE alongside curvature and V-orthogonality in the best capacity regime (k=320, p=32). Tests whether gentle late-step focus further reduces Val_MAE_32 without harming early steps.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n  from models_fact import Osc2CtxAdaptiveFactorizedL\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'rank_k': 320,\n          'proj_rank': 32,\n          'hidden_size': 64,\n          'normalize_u': True,\n          'gamma_start': 0.25,\n          'gamma_end': 0.98\n      }\n\n  class Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, rank_k=320, proj_rank=32, hidden_size=64, normalize_u=True, gamma_start=0.25, gamma_end=0.98):\n\
    \          self.model = Osc2CtxAdaptiveFactorizedL(\n              rank_k=rank_k,\n              proj_rank=proj_rank,\n              hidden_size=hidden_size,\n              normalize_u=normalize_u,\n              gamma_start=gamma_start,\n              gamma_end=gamma_end\n          )\n          self.needs_rng = False\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return Wrapper(\n          rank_k=int(hparams.get('rank_k', 320)),\n          proj_rank=int(hparams.get('proj_rank', 32)),\n          hidden_size=int(hparams.get('hidden_size', 64)),\n          normalize_u=bool(hparams.get('normalize_u', True)),\n          gamma_start=float(hparams.get('gamma_start', 0.25)),\n          gamma_end=float(hparams.get('gamma_end', 0.98))\n     \
    \ )\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x, lam_curv=1e-4, lam_ortho_V=1e-5):\n      # Mild horizon-weighted MAE (weights 1.00 -> 1.10, mean-normalized)\n      T = predictions.shape[1]\n      w = jnp.linspace(1.0, 1.10, T)\n      w = w / jnp.mean(w)\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets) * w[None, :, None])\n\n      # Temporal curvature regularization\n      curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      loss = mae + lam_curv * curv_pen\n\n      # V orthogonality (reduce redundancy in projection basis)\n      p = params['params'] if isinstance(params, dict) and 'params' in params else params\n      V = p.get('V', None)\n      if V is not None:\n          gram = V.T @ V\n          I = jnp.eye(gram.shape[0], dtype=gram.dtype)\n          loss = loss + lam_ortho_V * jnp.mean((gram - I) ** 2)\n      return loss\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return\
    \ optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8)\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Considerations on model training**


  I’m thinking about the time constraints we’re under, with token usage at 62%. I should possibly write a research note proposing ablations like further p/lambda sweeps, horizon weighting for factorized approaches, and including condition labels from the system. The system already uses condition labels to avoid cross-condition sequences, which is a good sign. I could also explore gating methods, but I need to ensure I stay within the station''s guidelines and not just focus on hyperparameter tuning.**Exploring parameter tuning**


  I''m considering some adjustments for our parameters. I want to try k=320 with varying p values: 24, 32, and 40. I noticed that lower p values have better scores, with p=32 yielding a more positive score than p=40. Additionally, applying orthogonal regularization improved scores even further. I might test small values for lam_ortho_V, like 5e-6 or 1e-5, and tune gamma_end to 0.99 or 0.98. I could also explore other improvements like factor dropout, but it seems easier to avoid that for now.**Refining model parameters**


  I’m considering adjusting the curvature lam slightly to 8e-5, though it had a small effect before. Testing k=288 with p=32 could reduce overfitting. I want to propose two submissions: the first would be "Osc2-CTX-FLoad (k=320, p=32) + V-OrthoReg," likely yielding improvements with V orthogonality. The second submission would be "Osc2-CTX-FLoad (k=320, p=32) + Later Horizon Weighting" to combine curvature with horizon-weighted MAE. We should keep concurrent evaluations to two or fewer and consider slightly tighter weighting ranges for better results.**Planning new submissions**


  I need to remember that the new computation for "compute_loss" must involve the network outputs and horizon weighting. Previously, we used "mae_with_temporal_curvature," so I could combine that with the weighted MAE, though playing it safe by replicating the previous compute_loss with orthoreg only seems wiser. Submission 54 showed that increasing k to 352 didn’t help, so I plan my next steps with k=320 and p=32. I’ll craft two submissions focusing on V-OrthoReg with and without mild horizon weighting while ensuring we stick to the limit of two concurrent submissions. I’ll also update the research notes.**Preparing submission code**


  I’m getting ready to prepare my first submission code. I’ll import the necessary components from models and losses, then set up the compute_loss function to include orthoreg on V, similar to previous submission 51 but with proj_rank set to 32. For the second submission, I''ll implement weighted MAE with curvature and V-ortho. I’ll ensure the code accesses the V parameter in the factorized model and set gamma_end to 0.98. Then, I’ll execute the submission with the appropriate YAML content, minimizing commentary per instructions. Let’s move ahead!'
