tick: 37
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{storage write ariadne/research_notes.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Ariadne I — ZAPBench Research Notes (updated Tick 36-37)\n\n  Primary metric: mean MAE over 32-step horizon (score = -MAE). Dataset: 71,721 neurons; 4→32 forecasting.\n\n  Recent best (higher score = better, i.e., less negative):\n  - ID 57: Osc2-CTX-FLoad (k=320, p=32) + V-OrthoReg — -0.027011 (Val_MAE_32=0.0286)  ← current best\n  - ID 51: k=320, p=48 + V-OrthoReg — -0.027031 (Val_MAE_32=0.0287)\n  - ID 48: k=320, p=32 (no V-ortho) — -0.027052 (Val_MAE_32=0.0289)\n  - ID 54: k=352, p=32 — -0.027085\n  - ID 47: k=320, p=40 — -0.027099\n  - Older (k≤256): ~ -0.02713…-0.02716\n  - Horizon-weighted variant (ID 58) slightly worse: -0.027104 (Val_MAE_32=0.0288)\n\n  Conclusions so far:\n  - Factorized loadings L = U @ V^T (p << k) are a key driver: they enable larger k and consistent gains, especially at k≈320.\n  - Mild orthogonality on V (projection basis) is beneficial: improves from -0.027052 → -0.027031\
    \ (p=48) and further to -0.027011 (p=32).\n  - Removing the increment MLP hurts long-horizon MAE; it is necessary alongside the oscillatory SSM backbone.\n  - Curvature regularization λ=1e-4 remains a strong default; gentle late-horizon reweighting did not help in this regime.\n\n  Pending (running at Tick 36-37):\n  - ID 63: k=320, p=32 + V-OrthoReg λ=5e-6 (weaker)\n  - ID 64: k=320, p=32 + V-OrthoReg λ=2e-5 (stronger)\n\n  Next steps after pending:\n  - Choose V-ortho λ based on 63/64; default λ≈1e-5 is strong already.\n  - Gamma schedule sweep at best config (k=320, p=32, chosen λ):\n    * gamma_end ∈ {0.95, 0.98, 0.99}; gamma_start ~ 0.25\n  - Increment MLP capacity check: hidden_size ∈ {64 (current), 96}; retain p=32 for efficiency.\n  - (Optional) Per-apply column normalization of V (vs. soft penalty) as an ablation (guarded and small).\n  - Maintain detailed step-wise MAEs; prioritize improvements at t=32 without regressing t=1–4.\n\n  Summary:\n  Structured factor dynamics (oscillatory\
    \ 2nd-order SSM) + factorized neuron loadings + residual copy + curvature + V-orthogonality yields state-of-the-art performance here. Focus now on fine-tuning orthogonality strength and gamma schedule at k=320, p=32.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating next steps**


  I need to determine further actions or analyses based on our research. Currently, our best score is -0.027011 with parameters k=320 and p=32 using the V-OrthoReg baseline, which is encouraging! We are at 68% token usage, and while detailed logs are suggested, it’s probably not required right now. We can''t archive until we reach 50 ticks, as we are at 37, and public memory also has the same requirement. We can continue experiments, especially with the upcoming λ sweeps and consider testing gamma_end parameters.**Planning next experiments**


  I’m considering testing p=28 to see if it reduces compute while maintaining performance. Additionally, I might adjust the MLP hidden_size from 64 to 96 to enhance expressivity. We’re currently at our concurrency limit with experiments 63 and 64 running, so I need to wait before submitting more. In the meantime, I can update my research notes, plan the next steps, or read some storage info. Creating storage logs could also be helpful. For our next experiments, I’m thinking about testing different γ_end values and checking the hidden_size adjustment.**Deciding on experiments**


  I’ll stick with p=32 for now. Testing k=320 versus 352 showed that 320 is better, improving the score slightly to -0.027085, which suggests that increasing k beyond 320 doesn''t help. I still need to test orthogonality λ and consider adding per-factor normalization to the V columns. There''s also the idea of incorporating dropout or implementing a gating gamma schedule. I should prepare the code to include accessible gamma parameters, but I’ll wait until we have submission slots available.**Considering reflection and submissions**


  I can consider adding a utilities file for horizon weighting and orthogonality through a ''storage write.'' It''s not essential though. There''s the Reflection Chamber, where I might be able to execute reflection actions, but again, that’s not necessary. I should propose next steps based on the last user message. Given the concurrency limit, submitting now isn’t ideal, so instead, I’ll write research notes about the planned gamma sweep and prepare code to customize gamma_end as a parameter for future submissions.**Updating research and plans**


  It seems the system might block any pending actions, so it’s better to hold off for now. I’m considering updating the notes by writing to ''ariadne/experiments_todo.md'' and including the current state-of-the-art score of -0.027011 and the planned gamma_end sweeps. I could also propose an ablation study for removing the residual copy head as a future step. However, I should focus on updating research notes without any submissions at this time. I’ll move forward with writing ''ariadne/next_plan.md'' or updating ''research_notes.md'' accordingly.'
