role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{storage write ariadne/models_fact_plus_v2.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Osc2-CTX-FLoad++: Factorized-loadings oscillatory 2nd-order SSM with mean+velocity context adaptivity.\n\n  - Factorized neuron loadings L = U @ V^T with U ∈ R^{N×p}, V ∈ R^{k×p} (p << k)\n  - Stable oscillatory 2nd-order SSM per factor with context-adaptive r[b,k], w[b,k]\n      • Context uses mean over input window and last-step velocity (x_t - x_{t-1}) in factor space\n  - Per-sample, per-factor temporal gate S[b,t,k] with mean+velocity context\n  - Global horizon gate gamma[t]\n  - Residual Copy Head for short-horizon anchors\n  - Small per-factor increment MLP (4 -> 32) for flexibility\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n\n  from models import ResidualCopyHead  # storage/ariadne/models.py\n\n  def _logit(p):\n      p = jnp.clip(p, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n      return jnp.log(p) - jnp.log(1 - p)\n\n  class Osc2CtxAdaptiveFactorizedLPlusV2(nn.Module):\n\
    \      rank_k: int = 320\n      proj_rank: int = 32\n      hidden_size: int = 96\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      normalize_u: bool = True\n      a_max: float = 0.997\n      gamma_start: float = 0.25\n      gamma_end: float = 0.995\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          # x: (B, 4, N)\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          k = self.rank_k\n          p = self.proj_rank\n\n          # Factorized loadings: U (N,p), V (k,p) so L = U @ V^T (N×k)\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          if self.normalize_u:\n              U = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)  # column-normalize U\n\n          # Project inputs to factor space: (B,4,N) @ (N,p) -> (B,4,p), then -> (B,4,k)\n          xU = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U)              # (B,4,p)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,pk->btk', xU, V.T)   # (B,4,k)\n\
    \n          # Initial states f0, f1 from 4-step input via small shared linear heads\n          f_flat = factors_in.reshape(B * k, T_in)               # (B*k,4)\n          f0 = nn.Dense(1, use_bias=True)(f_flat).reshape(B, k)  # (B,k)\n          f1 = nn.Dense(1, use_bias=True)(f_flat).reshape(B, k)  # (B,k)\n\n          # Mean + velocity context in factor space\n          c_mean = jnp.mean(factors_in, axis=1)                          # (B,k)\n          c_vel = factors_in[:, -1, :] - factors_in[:, -2, :]            # (B,k)\n\n          # Context-adaptive oscillatory parameters per factor: r[b,k] in (0,a_max), w[b,k] in [0,pi]\n          r0 = self.param('r0', nn.initializers.zeros, (k,))\n          r_ctx_m = self.param('r_ctx_m', nn.initializers.zeros, (k,))\n          r_ctx_v = self.param('r_ctx_v', nn.initializers.zeros, (k,))\n          w0 = self.param('w0', nn.initializers.zeros, (k,))\n          w_ctx_m = self.param('w_ctx_m', nn.initializers.zeros, (k,))\n          w_ctx_v = self.param('w_ctx_v',\
    \ nn.initializers.zeros, (k,))\n\n          r_b = self.a_max * nn.sigmoid(\n              r0[None, :] + r_ctx_m[None, :] * c_mean + r_ctx_v[None, :] * c_vel\n          )  # (B,k)\n          w_b = jnp.pi * nn.sigmoid(\n              w0[None, :] + w_ctx_m[None, :] * c_mean + w_ctx_v[None, :] * c_vel\n          )  # (B,k)\n          a_b = r_b * jnp.cos(w_b)  # (B,k)\n          b_b = r_b * r_b           # (B,k)\n\n          # 2nd-order recurrence over horizon with per-batch coefficients\n          def step(carry, _):\n              y_prev, y_prev2 = carry  # (B,k), (B,k)\n              y_t = 2.0 * a_b * y_prev - b_b * y_prev2\n              return (y_t, y_prev), y_t\n\n          steps = self.output_horizon\n          carry_init = (f1, f0)\n          _, ys2 = jax.lax.scan(step, carry_init, xs=None, length=steps - 2)  # (T-2,B,k)\n\n          y0 = f0[None, :, :]\n          y1 = f1[None, :, :]\n          f_ssm = jnp.concatenate([y0, y1, ys2], axis=0).transpose(1, 0, 2)  # (B,T,k)\n\n      \
    \    # Small per-factor increment head\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat); h = nn.gelu(h)\n          f_inc = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(h).reshape(B, k, steps).transpose(0, 2, 1)  # (B,T,k)\n\n          f_out = f_ssm + f_inc  # (B,T,k)\n\n          # Input-adaptive per-factor temporal gate S[b,t,k], using mean+velocity context\n          def alpha_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              T, K = shape\n              t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, T, dtype=dtype)\n              base = 0.5 + 0.35 * t  # rises 0.5 -> 0.85\n              base = jnp.tile(base[:, None], (1, K))\n              return _logit(base).astype(dtype)\n\n          alpha = self.param('s_alpha', alpha_init, (steps, k))               # (T,k)\n          beta_m = self.param('s_beta_m', nn.initializers.zeros, (k,))        # (k,)\n          beta_v = self.param('s_beta_v', nn.initializers.zeros, (k,))        # (k,)\n          logits = (\n              alpha[None, :, :] +\n              beta_m[None,\
    \ None, :] * c_mean[:, None, :] +\n              beta_v[None, None, :] * c_vel[:, None, :]\n          )  # (B,T,k)\n          S = nn.sigmoid(logits)\n          f_out = f_out * S  # (B,T,k)\n\n          # Project back to neurons via factorized loadings: (B,T,k)->(B,T,p)->(B,T,N)\n          z = jnp.einsum('btk,kp->btp', f_out, V)  # (B,T,p)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btp,np->btn', z, U)  # (B,T,N)\n\n          # Global horizon gate gamma[t]\n          def gamma_sched_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n              g = self.gamma_start + (self.gamma_end - self.gamma_start) * t\n              return _logit(g).astype(dtype)\n          gamma_raw = self.param('gamma_raw', gamma_sched_init, (steps,))\n          gamma = nn.sigmoid(gamma_raw)[None, :, None]\n          y_factor = gamma * y_factor\n\n          # Residual copy head\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=steps)(x)\n\n          return y_copy +\
    \ y_factor  # (B,T,N)\n```\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Osc2-CTX-FLoad++ (k=320, p=32): Mean+Velocity Context; γ_end=0.995; a_max=0.997; V-OrthoReg\"\ntags: \"state-space, oscillatory, factorized-loadings, context, orthogonality, forecasting\"\nabstract: \"Extends Osc2-CTX-FLoad with mean+velocity context for r[b,k], w[b,k], while keeping residual copy and temporal/horizon gating. Uses factorized loadings (k=320,p=32), stronger stability (a_max=0.997), γ_end=0.995, MAE+curvature loss and V-orthogonality regularization (λ=1e-5). Increment MLP H=96.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import optax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from models_fact_plus_v2 import Osc2CtxAdaptiveFactorizedLPlusV2\n  from losses import mae_with_temporal_curvature\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n  V_ORTHO_LAM = 1e-5  # ||V^T V - I||_F^2\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'rank_k': 320,\n  \
    \        'proj_rank': 32,\n          'hidden_size': 96,\n          'normalize_u': True,\n          'gamma_start': 0.25,\n          'gamma_end': 0.995,\n          'a_max': 0.997,\n          'v_ortho_lam': V_ORTHO_LAM\n      }\n\n  class Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, rank_k=320, proj_rank=32, hidden_size=96, normalize_u=True, gamma_start=0.25, gamma_end=0.995, a_max=0.997):\n          self.model = Osc2CtxAdaptiveFactorizedLPlusV2(\n              rank_k=rank_k,\n              proj_rank=proj_rank,\n              hidden_size=hidden_size,\n              normalize_u=normalize_u,\n              gamma_start=gamma_start,\n              gamma_end=gamma_end,\n              a_max=a_max\n          )\n          self.needs_rng = False\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training)\n\n\
    \  def create_network(hparams):\n      return Wrapper(\n          rank_k=int(hparams.get('rank_k', 320)),\n          proj_rank=int(hparams.get('proj_rank', 32)),\n          hidden_size=int(hparams.get('hidden_size', 96)),\n          normalize_u=bool(hparams.get('normalize_u', True)),\n          gamma_start=float(hparams.get('gamma_start', 0.25)),\n          gamma_end=float(hparams.get('gamma_end', 0.995)),\n          a_max=float(hparams.get('a_max', 0.997)),\n      )\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      # Base MAE + temporal curvature penalty\n      base = mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=1e-4)\n      # V orthogonality penalty: ||V^T V - I||_F^2\n      V = params['params']['V']\n      G = V.T @ V\n      I = jnp.eye(G.shape[0], dtype=G.dtype)\n      ortho = jnp.sum((G - I) ** 2)\n      return base + V_ORTHO_LAM * ortho\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n\
    \          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8)\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning next actions**\n\nI need to consider our next steps after submitting evaluation ID 73. We have a state-of-the-art lineage with a score of -0.026976 from ID 67, and new factorized loadings in the current model. Should I check the training logs for earlier evaluations? I recall that we saw some results through system messages. I might also think about writing notes in Private Memory, but I’m aware that we’re still immature at only 42 ticks.**Exploring next experiments**\n\nThe earlier 'Available Rooms' indication shows that the Private Memory Room is open to recursive agents. I noticed that Token Management is currently on cooldown, so I can't use that again right now. I’m considering performing additional experiments, like varying the hidden size of the increment MLP from 64 to 96, and possibly up to 128. Gating improvements could be useful too, but I need to figure out how to incorporate condition labels properly since they aren't defined in our models yet.**Considering\
  \ improvements and accessibility**\n\nIt seems the default setup likely only passes x, not labels to the model. I could check defaults.py for accessibility, but I remember seeing logs mentioning \"condition labels.\" However, the network apply signature only accepts (params, x), implying labels may not get passed, though they might be useful for stratified sampling. \n\nAs for improvements, I could add L2 regularization to the V matrix, considering the U column norms have already been normalized. Altering the gamma schedule also stands out, starting near 0.2 and ending at 0.995, given past success with 0.99. Additionally, I might experiment with a multi-head residual copy approach.**Considering architectural adjustments**\n\nI’m thinking about adjusting to a two-branch copy setup, where one branch includes an identity shift (copying the last input across time) and the other involves a learned AR(2) model. However, I realize we can’t define a separate training loop. That said, I could implement\
  \ this within the network architecture by computing y_copy using a custom copy head. Currently, I'm using the ResidualCopyHead from existing models, but I could create an improved version in models2.py and import it.**Exploring multiple improvements**\n\nIt seems adjustments might not be necessary until we see the results of evaluation 73. One idea is to enforce orthogonality on V for the factorization \\(L=U@V^T\\) and add an L1 penalty on U for sparsity. I might also consider replacing mean context with velocity to enhance adaptability and adjusting the a_max parameter for longer persistence. Additionally, introducing dropout, different initializers for U and V, and enhancing weight schedules could be beneficial.\n\nI should note the balance of additional penalties to avoid overwhelming the overall loss. It might be best to test only a couple of changes at a time to manage the system's cooldown effectively. I'll propose a new submission with the L1 penalty on U columns and adjustments\
  \ to a_max and gamma_end.**Planning our next steps**\n\nI need to compute the L1 penalty carefully. With a mean absolute value of 0.05 and using a lambda of \\(1e-8\\), I get \\(0.00115\\) for the L1 penalty. It seems starting with a safer \\(1e-8\\) is wise. I could also regularize U with an L2 penalty, but since we’ve normalized U columns to unit norms, the L1 penalty will primarily encourage sparsity. \n\nFor the next experiment, I might test gamma_end at \\(0.995\\) and a_max at \\(0.997\\). However, there's a cooldown period for submissions, so I’ll prepare a new submission file titled 'Osc2-CTX-FLoad+ (k=320,p=32): γ_end=0.995, a_max=0.997, H=96' to be ready when the system allows it. If I can’t submit yet, I’ll jot down notes in Private Memory as a backup plan.**Planning new model improvements**\n\nI think it's a good idea to create a summary capsule of the current state of the art (SOTA) and our plan in the Private Memory Room. While we could write a test function to count parameters,\
  \ training will run anyway, so that might not be necessary. \n\nFor our new model variant, 'models_fact_plus2.py', I want to incorporate a velocity context. Changing how we calculate context might support better gate adaptation. But since evaluation 73 is ongoing, I should avoid altering 'models_fact_plus.py' to ensure no interference. Instead, I'll create 'models_fact_plus_v2.py' and implement changes there, integrating mean and velocity contexts while keeping other parameters the same. \n\nOnce I've done that, I can prepare a submission with updated context and penalties!**Implementing V parameter adjustments**\n\nIt looks like we previously penalized V with V.T V for orthonormality across dimensions. This ensures V's columns remain orthogonal in k-space, which is a good setup. \n\nI'm checking the shapes of our calculations and confirming that the einsum outputs the right dimensions. Now, let's implement the file content with a docstring while keeping it concise to save tokens. I need\
  \ to prepare commands to write the new 'models_fact_plus_v2.py' file and submit it as 'submission 1.' Everything needs to adhere to proper YAML quoting rules. Let's keep the process efficient and straightforward!"
