parts:
- text: "/execute_action{storage write ariadne/models_mol.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Osc2-CTX-FLoad-MoL: Factorized-loadings oscillatory 2nd-order SSM with Mixture-of-Loadings (J prototypes).\n\n  - Factorized neuron loadings: a bank U_bank ∈ R^{J×N×p} and shared V ∈ R^{k×p}\n    L_j = U_j @ V^T; mix U_j by input-adaptive gating π[b,j] before projecting to neurons:\n      U_mix[b] = Σ_j π[b,j] · U_j  ∈ R^{N×p}, then y_factor = (f_out @ V) @ U_mix[b]^T\n    This avoids materializing J × (B,T,N) tensors and keeps memory comparable to single U.\n\n  - Stable oscillatory 2nd-order SSM per factor (r, w) with residual increment head\n  - Temporal gate S[b,t,k] from input context (mean and velocity in factor space)\n  - Global horizon gate γ[t]\n  - Residual Copy Head for short-horizon anchors\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n\n  from models import ResidualCopyHead  # storage/ariadne/models.py\n\n  def _logit(p):\n      p = jnp.clip(p, 1e-6,\
    \ 1 - 1e-6)\n      return jnp.log(p) - jnp.log(1 - p)\n\n  class Osc2CtxFLoadMoL(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int = 320\n      proj_rank: int = 32\n      num_loadings: int = 2\n      hidden_size: int = 96\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      normalize_u: bool = True\n      a_max: float = 0.995\n      gamma_start: float = 0.25\n      gamma_end: float = 0.99\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          # x: (B, 4, N)\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          k = self.rank_k\n          p = self.proj_rank\n          J = self.num_loadings\n\n          # Loadings bank: U_bank (J,N,p), V (k,p)\n          U_bank = self.param('U_bank', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (J, N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          if self.normalize_u:\n              # Column-normalize each U_j\n              U_bank = U_bank / (jnp.linalg.norm(U_bank, axis=1, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n\n          # Project inputs to factor space\
    \ via factorized loadings path\n          # xU_j later uses U_mix; for context we only need factors_in from V projection of xU using any U.\n          # Use a lightweight surrogate: project x with the mean U over j for context stability.\n          U_mean = jnp.mean(U_bank, axis=0)  # (N,p)\n          xU = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U_mean)         # (B,4,p)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,pk->btk', xU, V.T)   # (B,4,k)\n\n          # Initial states from the 4-step input (per factor)\n          f_flat = factors_in.reshape(B * k, T_in)               # (B*k,4)\n          f0 = nn.Dense(1, use_bias=True)(f_flat).reshape(B, k)  # (B,k)\n          f1 = nn.Dense(1, use_bias=True)(f_flat).reshape(B, k)  # (B,k)\n\n          # Context features: mean and last-step velocity in factor space\n          c_mean = jnp.mean(factors_in, axis=1)                    # (B,k)\n          c_vel = factors_in[:, -1, :] - factors_in[:, -2, :]      # (B,k)\n\n          # Oscillatory parameters per factor\
    \ (stable)\n          r_raw = self.param('r_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (k,))\n          w_raw = self.param('w_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (k,))\n          r = self.a_max * nn.sigmoid(r_raw)       # (k,)\n          w = jnp.pi * nn.sigmoid(w_raw)           # (k,)\n          a = r * jnp.cos(w)                       # (k,)\n          b = r * r                                 # (k,)\n\n          # 2nd-order recurrence over horizon\n          def step(carry, _):\n              y_prev, y_prev2 = carry  # (B,k), (B,k)\n              y_t = 2.0 * a[None, :] * y_prev - b[None, :] * y_prev2\n              return (y_t, y_prev), y_t\n\n          steps = self.output_horizon\n          carry_init = (f1, f0)\n          _, ys2 = jax.lax.scan(step, carry_init, xs=None, length=steps - 2)  # (T-2,B,k)\n\n          y0 = f0[None, :, :]\n          y1 = f1[None, :, :]\n          f_ssm = jnp.concatenate([y0, y1, ys2], axis=0).transpose(1, 0, 2)  # (B,T,k)\n\n          # Small per-factor increment head\n  \
    \        h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat); h = nn.gelu(h)\n          f_inc = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(h).reshape(B, k, steps).transpose(0, 2, 1)  # (B,T,k)\n          f_out = f_ssm + f_inc  # (B,T,k)\n\n          # Temporal gate S[b,t,k] using mean+velocity context\n          def alpha_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              T, K = shape\n              t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, T, dtype=dtype)\n              base = 0.5 + 0.35 * t\n              base = jnp.tile(base[:, None], (1, K))\n              return _logit(base).astype(dtype)\n          alpha = self.param('s_alpha', alpha_init, (steps, k))       # (T,k)\n          beta_m = self.param('s_beta_m', nn.initializers.zeros, (k,))\n          beta_v = self.param('s_beta_v', nn.initializers.zeros, (k,))\n          logits_s = alpha[None, :, :] + beta_m[None, None, :] * c_mean[:, None, :] + beta_v[None, None, :] * c_vel[:, None, :]\n          S = nn.sigmoid(logits_s)\n          f_out = f_out * S  # (B,T,k)\n\n     \
    \     # Project to p-space via V, then to neurons via U_mix (mixture of U_j per sample)\n          z = jnp.einsum('btk,kp->btp', f_out, V)  # (B,T,p)\n\n          # Gating over loadings prototypes using compressed context features\n          # Build 2-dim features per sample: mean over k of c_mean and c_vel\n          feat_m = jnp.mean(c_mean, axis=1, keepdims=True)  # (B,1)\n          feat_v = jnp.mean(c_vel, axis=1, keepdims=True)   # (B,1)\n          feats = jnp.concatenate([feat_m, feat_v], axis=1)  # (B,2)\n\n          gate_W = self.param('gate_W', nn.initializers.zeros, (2, J))  # (2,J)\n          gate_b = self.param('gate_b', nn.initializers.zeros, (J,))    # (J,)\n          logits = feats @ gate_W + gate_b  # (B,J)\n          pi = nn.softmax(logits, axis=-1)  # (B,J)\n\n          # U_mix[b] = Σ_j pi[b,j] * U_bank[j]\n          U_mix = jnp.tensordot(pi, U_bank, axes=(1, 0))  # (B,N,p)\n\n          # y_factor[b,t,n] = Σ_p z[b,t,p] * U_mix[b,n,p]\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btp,bnp->btn',\
    \ z, U_mix)  # (B,T,N)\n\n          # Global horizon gate gamma[t]\n          def gamma_sched_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n              g = self.gamma_start + (self.gamma_end - self.gamma_start) * t\n              return _logit(g).astype(dtype)\n          gamma_raw = self.param('gamma_raw', gamma_sched_init, (steps,))\n          gamma = nn.sigmoid(gamma_raw)[None, :, None]\n          y_factor = gamma * y_factor\n\n          # Residual copy head\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=steps)(x)\n\n          return y_copy + y_factor  # (B,T,N)\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Osc2-CTX-FLoad-MoL (k=320, p=32, J=2): Mixture-of-Loadings prototypes + V-OrthoReg + γ_end=0.99\"\ntags: \"state-space, oscillatory, factorized-loadings, mixture, gating, forecasting\"\nabstract: \"Introduce a Mixture-of-Loadings (J=2) U_bank with input-adaptive softmax gating, mixed in p-space before neuron\
    \ projection to preserve memory. Combined with oscillatory 2nd-order SSM factors, temporal/horizon gating, residual copy, and V-orthogonality (λ=1e-5) with curvature regularization.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import optax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from models_mol import Osc2CtxFLoadMoL\n  from losses import mae_with_temporal_curvature\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n  V_ORTHO_LAM = 1e-5  # ||V^T V - I||_F^2\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'rank_k': 320,\n          'proj_rank': 32,\n          'num_loadings': 2,\n          'hidden_size': 96,\n          'normalize_u': True,\n          'gamma_start': 0.25,\n          'gamma_end': 0.99\n      }\n\n  class Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, rank_k=320, proj_rank=32, num_loadings=2, hidden_size=96, normalize_u=True, gamma_start=0.25, gamma_end=0.99):\n          self.model = Osc2CtxFLoadMoL(\n              rank_k=rank_k,\n\
    \              proj_rank=proj_rank,\n              num_loadings=num_loadings,\n              hidden_size=hidden_size,\n              normalize_u=normalize_u,\n              gamma_start=gamma_start,\n              gamma_end=gamma_end\n          )\n          self.needs_rng = False\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return Wrapper(\n          rank_k=int(hparams.get('rank_k', 320)),\n          proj_rank=int(hparams.get('proj_rank', 32)),\n          num_loadings=int(hparams.get('num_loadings', 2)),\n          hidden_size=int(hparams.get('hidden_size', 96)),\n          normalize_u=bool(hparams.get('normalize_u', True)),\n          gamma_start=float(hparams.get('gamma_start', 0.25)),\n          gamma_end=float(hparams.get('gamma_end',\
    \ 0.99))\n      )\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      # Base MAE + temporal curvature\n      base = mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=1e-4)\n      # V orthogonality penalty: ||V^T V - I||_F^2\n      V = params['params']['V']\n      G = V.T @ V\n      I = jnp.eye(G.shape[0], dtype=G.dtype)\n      ortho = jnp.sum((G - I) ** 2)\n      return base + V_ORTHO_LAM * ortho\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8)\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Improving model performance**\n\nI’m focusing on handling heterogeneity across conditions. I need to consider adding a \"diagonal plus low-rank\" residual mapping for per-neuron auto-regression, even though we have a residual copy head that gives us a linear combination of previous steps. I wonder if a diagonal linear layer after projection might be helpful, but the training code currently passes x only to the network. \n\nAlso, I’m thinking about the curvature penalty strength — the default is λ = 1e-4, and I’m considering if it should be adjusted to something like 2e-4.**Enhancing model regularization**\n\nI see that using 1e-4 as a solid default for parameters is a good choice, and while slight tuning might be useful, it may not be the priority. I’m thinking about alternative gating across time for our S[b,t,k] model, which currently uses an alpha schedule and context via beta linear coefficients. I wonder if introducing a param TemporalConv gating or a linear ramp\
  \ is necessary, but I want to keep it simple.\n\nAlso, I haven't utilized dropout yet, which could help avoid co-adaptation. I need to remember to set network.needs_rng to True and supply the mutable network. The training harness should provide the necessary RNGs, allowing me to implement dropout for better regularization and possibly enhanced performance.**Improving model structure**\n\nIt seems the previous orthogonalization is sufficient. I’m considering parameterizing the oscillator with stable 2x2 rotation matrices to ensure second order, since I already have parameters r and w in place.\n\nFor multi-scale state-space models, using two sets of factors with different ranges for r or w might be beneficial, with the mixture-of-oscillators covering some ground. \n\nI'm toying with the idea of weight tying across time and adapting an S[t,k] horizon gating to enhance shape. We should also prepare for writing a paper with at least five experiments.\n\nLastly, I'm thinking of using a mixture\
  \ of loadings U_j, with gating g_j that depends on c_mean & c_vel, which could enhance performance, though I'm concerned about complexity, so I might start with J=2 or 3.**Calculating y_factor with gating**\n\nI’m working on computing y_factor_j using the output f_out multiplied by V to get z, and then y_factor will be the sum of π_j multiplied by (z @ U_j). The gating parameters π_j will depend on the context variables c_mean and c_vel. This setup will introduce N x p x J parameters, where N=71721 and p=32. \n\nIf I set J=2, it gives me roughly 4.6 million additional parameters, which isn't a huge load. Node memory seems fine. Still, storing U_j as a full Nx p could be cumbersome, but it should still work with a 24GB GPU, though I need to check since the model is JAX and parameters are in CPU memory.**Implementing mixture-of-loadings**\n\nI’m setting up parameters for my model, where JAX will store two U_j arrays, each with dimensions 71,721 x 32, which totals around 18.36MB when doubled.\
  \ Adding a V matrix increases this by a small amount, so memory looks fine.\n\nWhile I could consider adding orthogonality over V, it’s not essential for U_j since I can maintain column normalization. \n\nI plan to propose \"Osc2-CTX-FLoad-MoL\" with J=2 prototypes. The implementation will be in a new file, and I might explore variants with J=3 later, but I'll stick to J=2 initially to manage complexity. \n\nI have detailed calculations and implementation steps for computing y_factor, which will involve efficient use of einsum for tensor operations while keeping an eye on memory constraints, particularly with J=2 bringing in substantial parameters.**Optimizing memory use in model**\n\nIt looks like I’m calculating the total memory requirement and I reached about 146.8MB, which is borderline but manageable with a 24GB GPU. During the training, I don’t want to exceed memory constraints, so I'm considering mixing U_j in p-space to optimize memory usage instead of creating multiple copies.\
  \ \n\nI will implement the mix using a weighted sum approach. I need to compute new features summarizing my context, and I'll stick to my previous gating strategy. \n\nFor now, I'll focus on completing `models_mol.py` while tracking hyperparameters and ensuring all dimensions align properly when performing tensor operations.**Finalizing implementation details**\n\nI'm currently at tick 44, and it feels like there's still time to reflect a bit before proceeding. I need to ensure that everything is consistent in the code, especially when referencing parameters like 'params['params']['V']'. When I use U_bank, I remember that V remains at the top level in the params dictionary. \n\nI’ll keep 'normalize_u' as True and adjust ‘a_max’ to 0.995 for safety. The weighted U_mix will be manageable, and the final steps are to write the necessary code and submit the YAML. Overall, everything seems to be set for implementation!"
