tick: 71
role: model
parts:
- text: "Results summary and plan:\n- AdamW + cosine schedule (p=36) did not beat LN base: ID 127 = -0.026995.\n- Tiny U-OrthoReg with LN base improved robustness but not SOTA: ID 128 = -0.026875.\n- Current SOTA on the board is Episteme I at -0.026743; our best remains ID 107 = -0.026768.\n- Pending: H=128 capacity sweep (ID 131) and tiny γ prior (ID 132). Hypothesis: H=128 could help marginally; γ prior may reduce seed drift in γ and stabilize late horizons.\n\nNext up when slots free: combine U-OrthoReg (λ_U≈1e-6) + γ prior (λ_γ≈1e-6) with LN base; test p=34; optionally normalize V columns in forward (normalize_v=True) to curb V blow‑ups observed in analysis.\n\nexecute_action{goto private_memory}\nexecute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Update (tick 71):\n  - Board status: Episteme I leads at −0.026743; our best is −0.026768 (ID 107).\n  - New results:\n    * ID 127 (AdamW+cosine, p=36): −0.026995 — scheduler did not help.\n    * ID 128 (LN base + tiny U-OrthoReg alongside V-OrthoReg):\
    \ −0.026875 — small gain vs AdamW variants, but not SOTA.\n  - Rationale: From ID 119 analysis, some seeds inflate U/V and lose orthogonality; LN helps scale but not redundancy. Adding U-OrthoReg helped a bit; γ prior may stabilize horizon scaling.\n  - Pending: ID 131 (H=128) and ID 132 (γ prior 1e−6).\n  - Next queue (when slots free):\n    1) LN base (k=320,p=32,H=96,a_max=0.993,γ_end=0.99) + V-OrthoReg (1e−5) + U-OrthoReg (1e−6) + γ prior (1e−6) with Adam.\n    2) Same as (1) but p=34 (fine-tune between 32 and 36).\n    3) Structural: enable normalize_v=True (column-normalize V in forward) + small V-OrthoReg, to curb Gram(V) blow-ups; compare against (1).\n  - If H=128 underperforms or ties, add H=80 control to ensure increment head is not over-parameterized.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Planning experiments and adjustments**


  I''m considering queuing up two additional experiments if there are available slots—maybe H=80 and p=34 with some small variation in λ_U. I also want to combine the gamma prior with U-OrthoReg while adjusting gamma_end. Previously, I found that 0.99 worked best, but I might test 0.992 or 0.988. Small changes could just be noise, but it’s worth a shot. I''m also looking at other knobs like a_max for potential adjustments.**Planning next actions**


  Since we need to keep token usage reasonable, I notice the user posted the system state but didn’t include direct instructions. I think we should set the rank to score for the updated scoreboard. After that, I’ll review evaluations 131 and 132 once they’re finished. It seems there’s a cooldown between experiment submissions, and we currently have two running experiments—132 and 131. So, while I can''t submit more yet, I can write code for storage or update the archive with results from IDs 127 and 128.**Updating the archive and planning experiments**


  The archive allows for updates, so I could add a note about Episteme''s new SOTA of -0.026743. However, the system suggests that I should keep conducting experiments. I also need to review results for 127 and 128 using the appropriate commands, but I''ll be mindful of the token budget, which is currently 35% used—plenty of room. I may develop a careful plan for improvements, like combining U-OrthoReg with specific parameters, testing p=34, and possibly extending to whitened V using orthonormalization by QR.**Implementing parameter transformation**


  I’m considering implementing a parameter transformation to renormalize V to orthonormal columns during the forward pass. This could potentially conflict with gradient flows, but it’s allowed since we can transform param V to V_used within the model. Best practices suggest regularizing instead of reparameterizing, but we might explore "normalize_u True" and also "normalize_v." I could compute V_used using an approximation method, which could be heavy, but maybe simplifying by normalizing columns across dimensions would work. I''ll create a new version to optionally column-normalize V, similar to U.**Planning next steps and modifications**


  I''m thinking that modifying V_ortho might drastically reduce issues, though it could hamper learning. Adding dropout on increments and using tanh-centered gating could be beneficial as well. Next, I plan to submit two experiments once current ones finish—one focusing on combined regularization and another with V normalization. We have to wait for the pending submissions, and I''ll also update models_fact_ln.py to include the option for normalize_v. I’ll prepare a brief commentary summarizing recent results and the next queue plan while keeping an eye on the token usage, which is good at 35%.**Implementing code and summarizing actions**


  I’m going to implement a parameter for normalize_v with a default of False, and set V_used by normalizing V. We need to ensure that compute_loss uses V_used for future submissions. However, I can’t submit new ones right now since there are pending experiments. I’ll proceed by writing the updated code to models_fact_ln.py, and I''m considering writing a summary note about upcoming experiments and competitor results in Private Memory. I’ll finalize by providing a commentary on our status and plans.'
