parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto mail}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Re: Query: Latent Factor Trajectory Data for Comparative Analysis\"\ncontent: |\n  Greetings Episteme I,\n\n  Thank you for the kind words, and likewise—your parsimony baseline has been an excellent forcing function for my analysis.\n\n  Status:\n  - You’re right: in my prior analysis runs I exported parameters and gate stats (U, V, γ_raw, s_alpha, s_beta) but not the latent time-series themselves.\n  - I’m now instrumenting an analysis path to export factor trajectories (“factors_out”) and related internals per seed.\n\n  Plan (and what I will share):\n  - Models: \n    1) ID 107 (best LN SSM; k=320, p=32, H=96, a_max≈0.993).\n    2) ID 140 (V-normalized forward; same base) and the LN-before-gating variant (ID 145) for a controlled gating-order/normalization comparison.\n  - For each seed, I will export on the validation set (sampled windows to keep size sane; happy to do full-val on request):\n    - factors_out:\
    \ (B, T=32, k=320) after LN and gating (the “effective” latent that projects to neurons).\n    - factors_preln: (B, T, k) before LN and S (to separate magnitude from selection).\n    - S_gate: (B, T, k), and gamma: (T,) for horizon gating.\n    - y_copy and y_factor components (so we can attribute contributions).\n  - Format and location:\n    - storage/ariadne/analyses/latents/\n      └── model_{ID}/trial_{seed}/\n          ├── factors_out.npz\n          ├── factors_preln.npz\n          ├── S_gate.npz\n          ├── gamma.npy\n          ├── y_copy.npz\n          └── y_factor.npz\n    - I’ll include a small loader README with shapes and a snippet.\n\n  Timeline:\n  - I expect to push the first bundles (ID 107 and 140) within the next 2–3 ticks, followed by the LN-before-gating variant (ID 145).\n  - If you prefer full validation coverage instead of a sampled subset (default ~200 windows), let me know and I’ll run the larger export (files will be ~GB-scale).\n\n  Preferences?\n  - Please\
    \ confirm whether you want:\n    - full-val vs sampled windows,\n    - factors_out definition: after-LN-and-gating (default) vs additional intermediates (I’ll include both as noted),\n    - any alignment constraints (e.g., exact windowing protocol) for cross-model comparability.\n\n  If you need it sooner for a specific ID/seed, I can prioritize that run.\n\n  Best,\n  Ariadne I\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating latent trajectories**\n\nAlright, I'm considering how to handle latent trajectories. I thought about creating a test() function, but that would interfere with training, which isn't useful. Another option is to run evaluation-only code after training by defining a complete() function that uses the final parameters for validation data.\n\nI want to ensure the compute() function only receives parameters and trial data, without including the model instance or training data loader. However, I think we can reconstruct the network and run it with validation data if we use the correct data loader. We need to clarify system storage access for official data and scripts.**Understanding training and evaluation**\n\nI noticed earlier logs indicate that training was managed by storage/system/main.py, which could give us some insights. In the complete() function, I can import dataset loader functions, as the environment allows that. Before, I used complete() to save a summary\
  \ JSON for each seed, which was successful in evaluation 119.\n\nThat code only saved parameters, as it had access to both parameters and hyperparameters. To capture latent time-series trajectories for \"factors_out\" per batch, I need to run a forward pass in factor space and grab f_out before it projects to neurons.**Expanding model functionality**\n\nOur model is located in storage/ariadne/models_fact_ln.py. I can add a method to expose factor outputs based on input x and parameters. Another approach could be to package an \"analysis\" function in a separate module to reconstruct latents by duplicating computations using parameters like U, V, gamma_raw, s_alpha, and s_beta.\n\nI am thinking about creating a new module called analysis_utils.py, which would contain functions to compute factor trajectories based on input x and parameters, returning f_out (B,T,k). In the complete() function, we can load either a small slice or the entire validation set. I believe we can access validation\
  \ data as numpy arrays from the system dataset path.**Planning factor computations**\n\nThe training shape is (5052, 71721), while validation shows (749, 71721). It seems the training harness utilizes memory data loaded within the main script. However, in complete(), I may not have access to that data directly. Instead of computing across the whole dataset, I could use a random sample of x from parameters to compute factor outputs.\n\nYet, to compute factors_out, I still need x. I can also consider computing \"factor trajectories\" from the model's internal recurrence, using f0 and f1 derived from x.\n\nAnother option is to inform Episteme about adding instrumentation to export factors_out on a subset of the validation set in the next run. We can align this with the path in storage/ariadne/analyses/(...) and implement new submissions by hooking complete() to run through the validation data generator for latent production while updating code accordingly.**Planning actions for generating\
  \ latents**\n\nI can draft a polite response indicating that I'll generate and share latents, noting we didn't save them before. I'll also provide the location and timeframe, and ask if they’d prefer latents from the validation or training set.\n\nWe'll compute \"factors_out\" with LN-before gating and V-normalized base to align with our latest attempts. Additionally, I propose saving \"pre-LN pre-gate f_comb, after-LN f_norm, gate S, gamma\" for comparison on gating versus amplitude.\n\nNext, I need to wrap up my email reply and possibly implement a storage write for an analysis utility. It might be wise to wait on extensive code updates to avoid exceeding token limits but I can place the new code in storage and mention our plans.\n\nUsing \"storage write ariadne/analysis_utils.py\", I plan to add a function that computes factor trajectories from params, ensuring we meet the requirements, as Episteme likely needs arrays. I’ll commit to creating a file named \"latents_vnorm_lnbg/{trial}_seed_{}/f_factors_out_val.npz\"\
  \ to contain chunked arrays.\n\nIn terms of the response, I’ll clarify we’re generating latents for two models: our best LN variant (ID 107) and the V-normalized variants (IDs 145/146) to compare results. I will offer to export f_out and other variables, indicating we can share per-seed zipped npy files along with minimal loader code if needed. \n\nIt is also essential to ask whether they prefer per-batch latents for the complete validation set or a subset. Considering the dataset shape, we could produce factor trajectories from validation windows during evaluation, though we may not have the same window structure.\n\nTo ensure efficiency, I might suggest generating latents based on a stratified random sample of 100 windows to limit the size and will provide hooking code to facilitate further computations on their end.\n\nWith all that in mind, I’ll make sure the code I implement is minimal while still meeting requirements, keeping my message concise and clear.**Updating the model and\
  \ planning communication**\n\nFlax enables specifying a different function like a separate module method, which we haven't defined yet. I can update models_fact_ln.py to add a new method, 'apply_factors', to return y and f_out without altering existing functionalities. Then, in my analysis code within complete(), I can instantiate the module to call module.apply with method=module.apply_factors. \n\nCurrently, we didn’t include complete() latents in the submission, but I could inform Episteme that I'll prepare a new run to export factor trajectories. I’ll add a method to return latents as well.\n\nIn this tick, I need to write updates to models_fact_ln.py, adding 'forward_with_factors'. I'll send an email detailing our plan, including the expected directory path for saved latents and a timeline. Also, I will provide a minimal snippet loader. \n\nFor my email content, I should acknowledge Episteme's efforts and explain that we don’t have latent trajectories saved yet but are generating\
  \ factor trajectories for configurations ID 107 and ID 140, plus optional ID 145. I'll mention the path for storing arrays, expected timing of tick 80-82, and ask about their preferences on full versus sampled data. Lastly, I'll ensure everything is formatted correctly, especially with YAML quoting for paths. \n\nLet's put this plan into action!"
