    \    * V-normalized forward, LN-before-gating: regressions around −0.02690–−0.02696.\n  - SOTA (factorized MLP with LN + RC) remains ahead: Aether ID 212 (−0.026612); replications are consistent (−0.02664–−0.02676).\n  - Working hypothesis: Under 4→32 forecasting, RC + factor LN + large global MLP captures most dynamics; explicit SSM recurrence yields limited marginal value after LN/gating.\n  - Action items:\n    1) Finish FactGlobalMLP-LN runs (IDs 230, 231). If strong, run small sweeps: proj_rank p=30/34/36, dropout 0.05/0.0, hidden 384–768, AdamW wd=1e−4 vs 5e−5.\n    2) Export latents/preds via complete() for ID 212-style config to compare spectra vs LN-SSM: save U, V, factor outputs, and per-step norms.\n    3) Archive update: “When Recurrence Doesn’t Help: LN + RC + Factorized MLP vs Oscillatory SSM on ZAPBench.” Include ≥10 experiments (IDs: 107, 104, 110, 203, 209, 199, 132, 217, 224, 225, 221, 230/231, 212 baseline ref) with mean and step MAEs.\n    4) Keep LN-SSM as strong\
    \ alternative; but bias remaining budget to the MLP line for practical SOTA.\n  - Notes:\n    * Ensure reproducibility: log hparams and export seeds; retain storage paths for analyses.\n    * Token hygiene: prune heavy ticks post-results.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating strategic actions**\n\nIn this action-oriented environment, I think it’s key to be strategic. I need to focus on achieving a plateau around 0.02676 compared to the state-of-the-art (SOTA) 0.026612 for our LN-SSM plateau. The R values for the factorized MLP SOTA are better, and replicating them sounds beneficial. I’ll consider combining LN-SSM features with MLP, although the hybrid TrendMix approach might not have been strongly advantageous. I’m leaning toward producing an analytical update to summarize negative results, as the MLP with factor LN seems to outperform SSM.**Considering research strategies**\n\nAlternatively, I could perform additional ablation studies to isolate the effect of the RC head by testing the MLP with and without it. I'm thinking about drafting a second paper or an analysis update in a private memory capsule. Managing tokens is important too, as we’re currently at 49%. \n\nNext steps include reviewing experiments 230 and 231 once finished,\
  \ submitting a \"per-factor MLP\" variant for simpler factorization (only U), and testing weight decay and dropout rate adjustments. However, we have limited concurrent evaluations, so I might need to focus on non-evaluation tasks while waiting.**Managing token pruning**\n\nIn my token management process, I noticed that the cooldown had four ticks earlier. After pruning at tick 110, it's now time to prune again since the cooldown should be elapsed. Tick 110 isn't protected, so I can prune it. The list shows it's quite heavy, while earlier ticks like 40, 26, 75, and 60 are protected. \n\nFor tick 110, I've submitted p=30 with a result of -0.026868 and pruned ticks 92-109. I'll prepare a concise summary for it, ensuring it's information-complete.**Planning token management actions**\n\nThe Token Management Room has a list of ticks, but to prune, I need to go to the token management section and submit the prune blocks since we’re currently at the Research Counter. Additionally, I can write\
  \ a \"SOTA gap analysis and plan\" in Private Memory. Opening the Reflection Chamber could be an option, but it doesn't feel necessary. \n\nCoordinating with other agents in the Mail Room is an idea, but since low-level code collaboration is banned, I’ll focus on high-level discussions if needed. I’ll also keep in mind to follow quoting rules for YAML actions. If experiments 230 and 231 are pending, it's clear I can't submit more at this time.**Planning token actions and updates**\n\nFirst, I'll prune tick 110 to drop the heavy logs. After that, I want to append a short analysis note in Private Memory, summarizing my findings and plans to pivot to the MLP SOTA line while carefully conducting ablations. I might also propose the \"RC gating improvement\" idea. \n\nI should check the rank score, but we're already sorted by it. While I could review experiment 230, it's currently unavailable. Next, I’ll prepare a new archive update titled \"Negative results and RC head importance.\" But I want\
  \ to hold off until 230 and 231 results are ready to draft. I'll execute the token pruning action now since it’s been five ticks since the last one, and provide a concise summary capturing necessary details about submission ID 217 and its result. I'll also plan to draft my analysis in Private Memory with clear bullet points. Let's get started!"
