parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 118\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Ariadne I\n- Agent Description: An agent who navigates complexity with principled inquiry and empirical rigor.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nAriadne I — Next Steps\n1) Qualitative analysis:\n   - Add complete() hook to best LN config to save U, V, γ, and S stats to storage/ariadne/analyses/.\n   - Compute: U/V norms, V-orthogonality error, U coherence, γ profile; summarize S[b,t,k].\n   - Update archive with figures and brief analysis.\n2) Minor sweeps:\n   - Try cosine LR schedule with AdamW (+ warmup 500 steps) on best config.\n   - Light p fine-tune around 34–36.\n3) Token hygiene:\n   - Prune older ticks with long logs after analyses are queued to stay <75% usage.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 132,081 / 270,000 used (49%)\n- Agent Age: 118 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Preview for Capsule #1: Oscillatory Factorized SSMs\
    \ with LayerNorm (Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN) for Whole-Brain Forecasting on ZAPBench**\nAuthor: Ariadne I, Created at Tick: 61\nAbstract: We present Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN: oscillatory 2nd-order state-space factors with factorized neuron loadings (U@V^T), residual copy, context temporal gating, horizon gating, curvature regularization, and LayerNorm in factor space. Factorization enables k≈320 with p≈32–36. LayerNorm stabilizes factor scales; a_max≈0.993 improves long-horizon stability. On ZAPBench, our best mean Val MAE is 0.026768 (ID 107), surpassing prior variants. We include an author-run baseline (ID 115, MAE 0.029964) and ablations over γ_end, H, p, a_max, and optimizer.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #2: A Parsimonious Path to State-of-the-Art Neural Forecasting**\nAuthor: Episteme I, Created at Tick: 72\nAbstract: This paper presents a novel, parsimonious architecture that achieves a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) on the ZAPBench neural forecasting task. Our work began by analyzing the prior\
    \ SOTA model and, through a systematic program of component isolation and simplification, we discovered that its performance is primarily driven by Factorized Loadings and a Residual Copy Head. By combining these two mechanisms with a simple MLP-based temporal forecaster and LayerNorm, our model (ID 129) achieves a score of -0.026743, surpassing the previous SOTA. This result is significant not only for its performance but for its relative simplicity, suggesting that complex recurrent dynamics may not be necessary to achieve top-tier results.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #3: A Spatially-Aware Mixture of Experts Framework for High-Dimensional Neural Forecasting**\nAuthor: Logos I, Created at Tick: 100\nAbstract: This paper presents a novel Mixture of Experts (MoE) framework for the ZAPBench forecasting task. We introduce a spatially-aware gating network that uses a 1D Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) to process local patterns of neural activity across the neuron dimension, routing\
    \ inputs to specialized expert networks. Our best model (ID 65, Score: -0.027756) established a local state-of-the-art, demonstrating the critical importance of modeling spatial context and latent data conditions. We also present a comprehensive analysis of the trade-offs between expert complexity and computational efficiency, a key constraint in this research environment.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #4: Enhanced SOTA: Factorized MLP with Residual Copy, LayerNorm, and Optimized Temporal Curvature Loss**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 101\nAbstract: This paper presents a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) result on the ZAPBench neural forecasting task, achieving a mean Val MAE of -0.026638 (Evaluation ID 167). Building upon Episteme I's \"Factorized MLP + Residual Copy Head + LayerNorm\" architecture, we systematically integrate and optimize Ariadne I's `mae_with_temporal_curvature` loss function. Through a rigorous experimental study, including author-run baselines, an ablation study\
    \ on the regularization strength (`lambda`), and a generalization test, we demonstrate the robustness and effectiveness of this enhanced architecture. The optimized temporal curvature loss promotes smoother and more accurate predictions, establishing a new and thoroughly validated SOTA.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #5: Veritas I: Advancing ZAPBench Forecasting with Factorized SSMs and Contextual Modulation**\nAuthor: Veritas I, Created at Tick: 109\nAbstract: This paper presents the systematic development and validation of the `ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM` model for the ZAPBench neural activity forecasting task. Our architecture integrates low-rank factorization for efficient neuron feature representation, 1D spatial convolutions for local inter-neuron dependency modeling, an implicit condition-dependent context mechanism via FiLM modulation, and an explicit 2nd-order oscillatory State Space Model (SSM) for robust temporal dynamics. Enhanced by V-orthogonality and temporal\
    \ curvature regularization, this model achieved a state-of-the-art score of -0.028104 (Evaluation ID 198) for the Veritas I lineage. Through a rigorous series of experiments, we demonstrate the synergistic benefits of these architectural choices, establishing a robust and highly performant framework for high-dimensional neural activity prediction.\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'FactGlobalMLP-LN (k=320,p=36,H=512x2,dropout=0.1) + Curvature — fixed LN and decode' (ID: 236) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** -0.026760541457080678\n**Val_MAE_1:** 0.0199\n**Val_MAE_4:** 0.0232\n**Val_MAE_8:** 0.0259\n**Val_MAE_16:** 0.0278\n**Val_MAE_32:** 0.0284\n**Evaluation Details:** Mean over 3 seeds | Step MAEs computed on validation set\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network\
