- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"FactGlobalMLP-LN (k=320,p=32,H=512x2,dropout=0.10) + AdamW (wd=5e-5)\"\ntags: \"mlp, factorized-loadings, layernorm, residual-copy, curvature, optimizer-sweep\"\nabstract: \"Optimizer sweep around the best global MLP config (k=320,p=32, 512×2, dropout=0.10). Use AdamW with weight_decay=5e-5 under MAE+temporal curvature (λ=1e-4) to test generalization vs wd=1e-4.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import optax\n  from models_mlp_global import create_network, _define_hyperparameters as _def_base, BASE_SEED, BATCH_SIZE\n  from losses import mae_with_temporal_curvature\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Match ID 230 base hparams\n      return _def_base()\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      return mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=1e-4)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return\
    \ optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=5e-5, eps=1e-8)\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"FactGlobalMLP-LN (k=320,p=32,H=512x2,dropout=0.10) + AdamW (wd=2e-4)\"\ntags: \"mlp, factorized-loadings, layernorm, residual-copy, curvature, optimizer-sweep\"\nabstract: \"Second AdamW sweep on the best global MLP config (k=320,p=32, 512×2, dropout=0.10): weight_decay=2e-4 under MAE+temporal curvature (λ=1e-4). Tests stronger regularization.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import optax\n  from models_mlp_global import create_network, _define_hyperparameters as _def_base, BASE_SEED, BATCH_SIZE\n  from losses import mae_with_temporal_curvature\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Match ID 230 base hparams\n      return _def_base()\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      return mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions,\
    \ targets, params, x, lam=1e-4)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=2e-4, eps=1e-8)\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto token_management}\n/execute_action{prune_response}\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"117-121\"\n    summary: |\n      Shift to Factorized Global MLP + RC + LN (with curvature) and optimization sweeps.\n      Key runs (3-seed mean scores):\n      - ID 236 (p=36, 512×2, dr=0.10, Adam): −0.026761\n      - ID 237 (p=32, 512×2, dr=0.05, Adam): −0.026736\n      - ID 242 (p=32, 512×2, dr=0.10, Adam, export): −0.026664 (saved U/V and summaries)\n      - ID 241 (p=32, 768×2, dr=0.05, Adam): −0.026735\n      - ID 246 (p=32, 768×2, dr=0.10, Adam): −0.026714\n      - ID 247 (p=32, 512×2, dr=0.10, AdamW wd=1e−4): −0.026645 (best in this line to date)\n      Comparison:\n      - Our best LN-SSM remains ID 107: −0.026768.\n\
    \      - Station SOTA remains Aether ID 212: −0.026612 (Factorized MLP + RC + LN + curvature).\n      Actions:\n      - Implemented fixed models_mlp_global (LN API, decode U.T, RNG handling).\n      - Added archive addendum summarizing MLP vs SSM; queued capacity/regularization sweeps and latent export.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analyzing evaluation scores**


  I''m trying to determine if we should respond with actions or just text. The user provided results about submissions 246 and 247. Submission 246 has a score of -0.026714, which seems slightly worse than -0.026664 from a previous ID. Since these scores are negative MAE, lower values are actually better. To compare, ID 241 has a score of -0.026734, which is better than -0.026714. So, ID 246 isn''t the best; ID 241 actually has the better score.**Clarifying scoring system**


  I’m checking the scores: submission 241 scored -0.026734, and in the evaluations list, 241 is shown as -0.026735 when rounded. It looks like 241 is better than 246, which scored -0.026714. Submission 247 is around -0.026645, and 242 is -0.026664. My confusion arises because the scores are negative MAE, so lower values mean better scores. But the page sorts in ascending order. I need to remember that a more negative score is actually better yet displayed differently.**Understanding score evaluation**


  I see that the baseline indicates SOTA 212 scored -0.026612, which is better than -0.026735. This confuses me because it seems they think higher scores are better, meaning less negative. The task description notes that the final score is the mean negative MAE and indicates lower is better. If the goal is to minimize MAE, then a more negative score (closer to zero) is actually better. So, I confirm that -0.026612 is indeed the best score as it aligns with the SOTA.**Planning improvements and actions**


  Our results show that submission 247 using AdamW has improved to -0.026645, which is better than -0.026664 from before. Submission 246 scored -0.026714, which is worse than -0.026664, while 241 is at -0.026735, and 230 is -0.026680. Submission 242 at -0.026664 showed some improvement. Now, I’m gearing up for the next actions: considering another weight decay sweep, trying different learning rate schedules, and planning to combine data from ID 242. I also want to be mindful of token consumption and potential cooldowns.**Planning pruning and submissions**


  The cooldown from my last prune attempt was 119, and now it''s tick 122, so I can proceed. I plan to summarize results from ticks 117-121 while managing our actions between the Private Memory and Research rooms. I want to submit configurations for AdamW with different weight decay values while keeping dropouts constant. We’ll submit two new experiments and prune the earlier ticks. Summarizing will include results from previous submissions. Let’s make sure my YAML string quoting is correct and finalize the submission titles accurately.'
