- text: "/execute_action{goto token_management}\n/execute_action{prune_response}\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"114\"\n    summary: |\n      Implemented and fixed Global Factorized MLP + RC + LN (models_mlp_global.py): corrected Flax LayerNorm API, decode with U.T, and dropout RNG handling. Baseline run (ID 230) reached −0.026680; established parity with the factorized-MLP line and set up for optimizer/capacity sweeps.\n  - ticks: \"116\"\n    summary: |\n      Capacity/regularization sweeps for Global MLP: H=768x2 (ID 246) −0.026714; dropout 0.05 (ID 237) −0.026736. AdamW wd=1e−4 (ID 247) improved to −0.026645 (best so far). Prepared utilities and plan to export latents/preds from best configs.\n  - ticks: \"122\"\n    summary: |\n      AdamW sweeps: wd=5e−5 (ID 252) −0.026654; wd=2e−4 (ID 253) −0.026661; best remains wd=1e−4 (−0.026645). Larger MLP (H=768x2) confirmed at −0.026714. Documented plan: schedule ablations, mild p fine-tune, and complete() export on best.\n```\n/execute_action{storage\
    \ write ariadne/analysis_utils.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import json\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\n  def uv_stats(U: jnp.ndarray, V: jnp.ndarray):\n      \"\"\"\n      Compute basic stats for factorized loadings:\n      - U: (N,p), V: (k,p)\n      Returns a Python dict with scalar/1D summaries.\n      \"\"\"\n      # Column norms of U (p,) and column norms of V^T (p,)\n      u_col_norms = jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0)\n      v_col_norms = jnp.linalg.norm(V.T, axis=0)\n\n      # V orthogonality error ||V^T V - I||_F^2\n      Gv = V.T @ V  # (p,p)\n      Iv = jnp.eye(Gv.shape[0], dtype=Gv.dtype)\n      v_ortho_err = jnp.sum((Gv - Iv) ** 2)\n\n      # U coherence: off-diagonal absolute inner products of U columns\n      Gu = U.T @ U  # (p,p)\n      du = jnp.sqrt(jnp.clip(jnp.diag(Gu), 1e-12))  # guard\n      # Normalize to correlation-like measure\n      Gu_norm = Gu / (du[:, None] * du[None, :])\n      off_diag = jnp.abs(Gu_norm - jnp.eye(Gu_norm.shape[0], dtype=Gu_norm.dtype))\n      u_coh_max\
    \ = jnp.max(off_diag)\n      u_coh_mean = jnp.mean(off_diag)\n\n      return {\n          \"u_col_norms_mean\": float(jnp.mean(u_col_norms)),\n          \"u_col_norms_std\": float(jnp.std(u_col_norms)),\n          \"v_col_norms_mean\": float(jnp.mean(v_col_norms)),\n          \"v_col_norms_std\": float(jnp.std(v_col_norms)),\n          \"v_ortho_frob2\": float(v_ortho_err),\n          \"u_coherence_max\": float(u_coh_max),\n          \"u_coherence_mean\": float(u_coh_mean),\n      }\n\n  def save_stats_json(path: str, stats: dict):\n      # Convert to pure Python types and save\n      def to_py(x):\n          try:\n              return float(x)\n          except Exception:\n              return x\n      stats_py = {k: to_py(v) for k, v in stats.items()}\n      with open(path, \"w\") as f:\n          json.dump(stats_py, f, indent=2)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Testing hyperparameters and tracking experiments**\n\nI'm considering testing learning rates of 8e-4 and 1.2e-3 with AdamW at 1e-4, keeping dropout at 0.1. We planned to use a dropout of 0.12, but we need to isolate those variables first. I want to incorporate a complete() hook to export latents from the best config, saving U, V, and possibly predictions. It’s essential to adhere to token limits and provide two distinct submissions for the learning rates while being aware of running experiments.**Managing experiments and submissions**\n\nAt tick 127, I'm seeing that the running experiments labeled 262 and 261, created at tick 126, are still active. This means I can't submit new experiments yet since the system allows only two concurrent runs. I can, however, prepare code, write to storage, or plan future actions. Adjusting token management seems like a good option, and I could also work on writing the complete() hook into the models_mlp_global wrapper or create an \"\
  analysis_utils.py\" file for export functions. But I have to ensure the complete() function is included in the submission file.**Planning future submissions and results management**\n\nSince we have running experiments, we shouldn't try submitting anything new right now. We can confirm our current results by checking for a sorted score, which is already done. My plan is to queue learning rate sweeps of 8e-4 and 1.2e-3 along with a dropout of 0.12 after experiments 261 and 262 finish. I also want to update private memory with our plans and the ablation results, and maybe prune older ticks to manage tokens more efficiently. But I’ll need to check the cooldown status to see if that’s possible.**Managing token cooldown and pruning**\n\nThe Token Management shows a cooldown count, with 4 ticks indicated at tick 123, but not at 124. We haven't attempted a prune yet at 126. I can prune tick 114, which had heavy logs and code. I'm also looking at tick 116 and tick 122 for pruning. Following the\
  \ guidelines, it seems we can prune again since the last action was at tick 122. Now that we're at tick 127, I should prepare good summaries and prune ticks 114, 116, and 122 together. That sounds efficient!**Updating archives and creating analysis utilities**\n\nI can update the archive since our initial paper was accepted earlier, but I'm hesitant because it might require substantial content. The list shows no capsules, though I remember an earlier claim about acceptance. I could also implement the complete() function for MLP to export U and V, writing an exporter script to handle that. However, actual exports will have to wait for the next submission. \n\nFor now, I'll manage tokens by pruning large ticks 114, 116, and 122 while ensuring concise summaries for each. I'll write analysis utilities for computing norms and coherence, using jax.numpy for calculations. Finally, I'll compile my commands accordingly."
