parts:
- text: "/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"FactGlobalMLP-LN (k=320,p=32,H=512x2,dr=0.10) + Adam(lr=9e-4) + GradClip(1.0)\"\ntags: \"mlp, factorized-loadings, layernorm, residual-copy, optimizer, gradient-clipping\"\nabstract: \"Learning-rate sensitivity: Adam (no wd) at lr=9e-4 with global grad clipping (norm=1.0) on the best global MLP. Curvature loss λ=1e-4.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import optax\n  from models_mlp_global import create_network, _define_hyperparameters as _def_base\n  from losses import mae_with_temporal_curvature\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      hp = _def_base()\n      hp['proj_rank'] = 32\n      hp['dropout_rate'] = 0.10\n      hp['learning_rate'] = 9e-4\n      return hp\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      return mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=1e-4)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      # Use passed learning_rate (set\
    \ to 9e-4 in hparams)\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8)\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"FactGlobalMLP-LN (k=320,p=32,H=512x2,dr=0.11) + Adam(lr=1e-3) + GradClip(1.0)\"\ntags: \"mlp, factorized-loadings, layernorm, residual-copy, optimizer, regularization\"\nabstract: \"Dropout sensitivity: increase dropout from 0.10 to 0.11 under Adam (no wd) + global grad clipping (norm=1.0) on the best global MLP. Curvature loss λ=1e-4.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import optax\n  from models_mlp_global import create_network, _define_hyperparameters as _def_base\n  from losses import mae_with_temporal_curvature\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      hp = _def_base()\n      hp['proj_rank'] = 32\n      hp['dropout_rate'] = 0.11  # test slightly stronger regularization\n      hp['learning_rate'] = 1e-3\n      return hp\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions,\
    \ targets, params, x):\n      return mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=1e-4)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8)\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering pruning and archiving**\n\nI could implement a submission with complete() to save training metrics, but since each run relies on compute_loss, it should have minimal overhead. Now, I want to check on pruning tokens again since there's a cooldown of 4 ticks, meaning I can prune again at tick 144 after using it at tick 140. The Dialogue History indicates heavy usage from ticks 136-139, so I should evaluate pruning those to cut down on token usage, which is currently at 54%. Then there's the note about using Public Memory or Archive for verified content. What’s the next best step?**Exploring adjustments for optimization**\n\nI’m considering setting additional runs with small adjustments, like a clip norm of 1.0. I think the learning rate might be best at around 1e-3, given that 1.1e-3 and 8e-4 haven't worked well for us. Maybe trying 9e-4 or 1.05e-3 could be good options. \n\nI also wonder about the gradient clip's location in the optax chain, since we're already\
  \ clipping like Aether. Are we normalizing U? The models_mlp_global seems to use normalize_u as true. Testing a v-normalized forward could be interesting, although it didn't help before. \n\nThen there's dropout; we tried 0.09, resulting in -0.026711, so 0.11 might be worth testing, especially under Adam. Small improvements are expected, but I want to refine this further!**Considering layer normalization options**\n\nI’m thinking about switching to pre-LN for factor_out or adjusting the LN epsilon. Aether used LN across the factor dimension with epsilon set at 1e-6, while I believe we default to LN. Earlier in our model, we also used LN. The models_mlp_global might be using LN too, and I've fixed the API. \n\nIt could be interesting to test epsilon at 1e-5 versus 1e-6, though the difference seems minor. The latest scoreboard shows improvements from Logos and others, prompting the agent to ask for next actions. Without delay, I plan to generate two more runs: 512x2 with Adam 1e-3 and a\
  \ dropout of 0.11, and also a run with Adam at 9e-4. That seems like a good approach!**Setting up gradient clipping and optimization**\n\nI’m considering testing gradient clip at 1.0 along with gradient clipping at the last step versus the chain order, but we’ve already used the correct chain. Another potential improvement is gradient centralization, though that's not available. \n\nI plan to submit two actions and ensure I'm in the right room, the Research Counter. I’ll use the /execute_action{submit 1} commands in YAML. I should also craft content that references models_mlp_global import and set the dropout rate parameter. \n\nIt’s crucial to ensure the learning rate parameter is set correctly, either in _define_hyperparameters or when passing it to create_optimizer. Previously, we did set hp['learning_rate'], and for 0.0009, I can set hp['learning_rate'] to 9e-4. From there, create_optimizer will utilize the learning_rate variable appropriately.**Adjusting learning rates and submissions**\n\
  \nIn the previous code, we replaced the learning_rate variable with a constant. For the 8e-4 rate, we set lr=8e-4 directly in create_optimizer. Now, for 9e-4, we can keep 'learning_rate' in hyperparameters and use the default in create_optimizer. \n\nI plan to submit two configurations: one with \"Adam(lr=9e-4) + GradClip(1.0)\" and dropout of 0.10, and another with \"Adam(lr=1e-3) + GradClip(1.0) + Dropout=0.11\" or 0.095. \n\nAlso, I noticed we could prune the heavy tick 136 to save tokens, though it's not necessary right now. I'll prepare the YAML commands for the submissions."
