- text: "/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Status @t152 and immediate plan:\n  - Best in our line: 768×2, dr=0.10, Adam lr=9e-4, Clip=1.0 → −0.026608 (ID 306).\n  - Running now: 768×2, dr=0.09 with (lr=9.5e-4, clip=1.0) [ID 318] and (lr=1e-3, clip=0.9) [ID 319].\n  - Next (queue when slots free):\n    1) 768×2 + Adam lr=1e-3, Clip=1.0, dr=0.09 (confirm best corner).\n    2) 768×2 + Adam lr=9e-4, Clip=0.95, dr=0.09 (fine-grained clip).\n    3) 768×2 + Adam lr=9.5e-4, Clip=1.0 + cosine decay (warmup 500 steps).\n  - Stretch goals: 832×2 if runtime allows; keep curvature λ=1e-4.\n  - Paper: add MLP results section contrasting with LN-SSM; include IDs 306, 315–319 and SOTA refs (259, 285).\n```"
thinking_content: '**Considering optimization strategies**


  I’m thinking about trying a dropout rate of 0.095 for fine-tuning. Also, I want to explore a learning rate schedule with cosine decay and warmup, since that was suggested earlier but not used. We utilized AdamW with cosine decay previously, which regressed, but it might work better for an MLP. I need to ensure I provide the content code in the submission YAML and import the optax schedule. Setting up the optimizer to return the correct configurations will be essential for training stability.**Evaluating training configurations**


  I need to be cautious when reading defaults.py for the training loop because the create_optimizer signature requires optax.GradientTransformation. I found that using weight decay with AdamW and clipping at 1.0 resulted in a worse outcome than Adam alone. It seems like sticking with Adam is the way to go. I considered increasing the batch size from the default of 8 to 12, but I worry about GPU memory limits. Changing it could lead to issues and break training, so I’ll stick to the baseline settings.**Exploring MLP configurations**


  I’m considering proposing a "Deeper Factor-Space MLP" with dimensions of 896×2, but I need to weigh the compute costs and potential memory or timing limits. It might still run within 45 minutes. Since increasing to 768 had slight improvements, stepping up to 832×2 could help even more. I also thought about using 512×3 for deeper depth, similar to what Aether used. Dropout adjustments are worth exploring too; while 0.09 gave modest improvements, I might try 0.085 along with a learning rate of 9.2e-4. But I can''t submit any new experiments right now because I already have two pending.**Preparing for next steps**


  I think I can use this time to prepare and wait, focusing on reading results or planning, or maybe updating the paper in the archive since earlier results were accepted. It sounds good that we had a score of 9/10. I might want to include new MLP results as well. Also, I’m thinking about maintaining token efficiency and providing minimal commentary. Even though the scoreboard is already sorted, I could consider ranking by score again. I’ll keep reflecting on strategies to maintain momentum.**Planning next actions**


  For this turn, I plan to wait for the ongoing experiments to finish. In the meantime, I can pre-stage the code for the 832 scheduler variant and adjust the optimizer with warmup cosine settings. However, I won’t submit anything new while two experiments are already running. I might think about using optax.adamw with decoupled weight decay on specific weights, but it would complicate things. I’ll keep track of my next steps in Private Memory to stay organized but avoid unnecessary actions and tokens for now.'
