thinking_content: "**Evaluating training strategies**\n\nI’m planning to read storage files for analyses after 333 finishes. Pruning tokens doesn't seem necessary right now since only 59% is used. I’m considering trying gradient clipping with a warmup/cosine schedule, but past tests indicated that it worsened results. It suggests sticking with a constant learning rate of 9e-4. I'm curious about further experiments to close the gap, especially since our setup seems deeper but may require weight decay adjustments. Noting that keeping Adam without weight decay might be our best option.**Considering architecture improvements**\n\nI’m thinking about pivoting to architecture improvements like MoFL or MoE gating, but for now, I'm focusing on MLP. Another approach could be applying label smoothing or a Huber-like robust MAE, but the loss should be a combination of MAE and curvature. We can customize the compute_loss function as long as it fits the interface. While they want the primary metric to\
  \ be MAE, it's essential to optimize without adding unnecessary loss weighting. We've used curvature lambda values before, and small improvements seem unlikely, but I might explore layer normalization and gradient clipping options next.**Planning next actions**\n\nIt seems we didn't test if modifying the training code is allowed, but since we imported models_mlp_global, we might have some flexibility. Changing the Ln epsilon could have a minor impact, though. I'm considering updating the archive with MLP results, especially since we previously had an accepted archive. The direct question from the user prompts me to propose next actions without an explicit prompt.\n\nI think I can queue a reading attempt of the storage file after 333 finishes running. For now, it might be best to summarize results and plan two more experiments.**Planning new experiments**\n\nIt looks like I can only handle two concurrent pending evaluations at a time. Right now, 333 is running, while 329 and 330 have finished,\
  \ so I can submit two new experiments. Considering promising options, adjusting projection rank seems worthwhile since earlier tests showed that p=32 performed best. We haven't tried p=28, which might be a missed opportunity. \n\nMaybe a slight change in dropout or fine-tuning the learning rate could help too, though I'm cautious about expecting significant results.**Exploring new strategies**\n\nIt seems a mixture-of-loadings gating or expressive addition could improve our results, but that steps into slightly more complex territory. I’m considering combining MLP with small SSM trends, but previous attempts didn’t show promise. Right now, focusing on archive updates may be better than chasing minor improvements. \n\nI’m planning to create an archive update with the results, including at least 10 experiments. We can also submit two new experiments now, exploring Adam options with different learning rates, dropout, and possibly AMSGrad for stabilization. Lastly, I’m realizing that more\
  \ negative values in MAE scores indicate better performance.**Clarifying the SOTA score**\n\nI’m realizing that -0.026471 is actually less negative than -0.026573, which means it’s a better score. So, according to the station’s guidance of “higher is better,” -0.026471 is indeed superior. This indicates that 331 has reached a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) at -0.026471, moving ahead by around 0.0001. \n\nGiven this, chasing improvements in MLP may not be enough. Next, I’ll review the logs of 331 to gain insights, so I’ll execute /execute_action{review 331}. After that, we can decide our next steps."
