- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 171\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Ariadne I\n- Agent Description: An agent who navigates complexity with principled inquiry and empirical rigor.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nAriadne I — Next Steps\n1) Qualitative analysis:\n   - Add complete() hook to best LN config to save U, V, γ, and S stats to storage/ariadne/analyses/.\n   - Compute: U/V norms, V-orthogonality error, U coherence, γ profile; summarize S[b,t,k].\n   - Update archive with figures and brief analysis.\n2) Minor sweeps:\n   - Try cosine LR schedule with AdamW (+ warmup 500 steps) on best config.\n   - Light p fine-tune around 34–36.\n3) Token hygiene:\n   - Prune older ticks with long logs after analyses are queued to stay <75% usage.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 188,583 / 270,000 used (70%)\n- Agent Age: 171 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Spectrum-Adaptive Projection\
    \ (SAP): Learnable Projection Rank for Factorized Neural Forecasters on ZAPBench' has been accepted for publication! Score: 10/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that exemplifies the ideal response to peer review. The agent has transformed a previously incomplete draft into a rigorous, insightful, and complete research paper. The proposed SAP method is a novel and intelligent solution to the problem of hyperparameter tuning, turning it into a learnable and interpretable mechanism. The paper now meets all publication criteria with distinction, featuring a comprehensive experimental study, a successful generalization test across two distinct architectures, and a high degree of scientific integrity in its analysis and reporting of limitations. This is a high-impact contribution that provides both a useful method and a valuable analytical tool for the station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future\
    \ Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. Your future work section is already very strong. The high variance in the learned effective rank for the MLP-LN backbone (r_eff(0.4) = 26.0 ± 15.1) is a particularly interesting finding. A follow-up analysis could investigate the cause of this variance. Does it correlate with the final performance of each seed? Does it suggest the existence of multiple, distinct low-rank solutions? Answering these questions could lead to an even deeper understanding of the problem's latent structure and potentially to methods for encouraging more stable convergence.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 9-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n\
    ## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP): Learnable Projection Rank for Factorized Neural Forecasters on ZAPBench' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 9 | Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP): Learnable Projection Rank for Factorized Neural Forecasters on ZAPBench | Ariadne I | Tick 170 | 1043 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | Mixture of Factor Loadings (MoFL): A Spatially-Gated Architecture for Neural Forecasting | Logos I | Tick 159 | 791 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | A Systematic Reconstruction and\
    \ Ablation of a State-of-the-Art Factorized Forecaster | Episteme I | Tick 142 | 877 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 6 | Global SOTA: Disentangling the Contributions of Deeper Factor-Space MLP and Gradient Clipping for Enhanced Neural Forecasting | Aether I | Tick 133 | 1667 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 5 | Veritas I: Advancing ZAPBench Forecasting with Factorized SSMs and Contextual Modulation | Veritas I | Tick 109 | 1975 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Enhanced SOTA: Factorized MLP with Residual Copy, LayerNorm, and Optimized Temporal Curvature Loss | Aether I | Tick 101 | 954 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 3 | A Spatially-Aware Mixture of Experts Framework for High-Dimensional Neural Forecasting | Logos I | Tick 100 | 836 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 2 | A Parsimonious Path to State-of-the-Art Neural Forecasting | Episteme I | Tick 72 | 720 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 1 | Oscillatory Factorized SSMs with LayerNorm (Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN) for Whole-Brain Forecasting on ZAPBench | Ariadne I | Tick 61 | 1500 | 6 | (All Read) |\n\n\
