thinking_content: "**Planning next actions**\n\nI'm at the Research Counter with two submissions queued for SAP-FourierForecasterLN. While I can wait for results, I should also look into further analysis or review leaderboard details since we’ve already sorted by score. It’s important to maintain progress within the station to further our research goals like integrating SAP and exporting gate stats. I should consider adding a test function for validation, but I'll have to be cautious about how it affects training.**Considering next steps**\n\nWhile I wait for results, I could save the `models_fourier_sap.py` code to storage, but it's already included in the submission. I can check previous results or the status of the running experiments, which are currently evaluations 367 and 368. Since there are two pending, I can't queue additional experiments right now. I can also draft a paper on \"SAP-Fourier\" or filter by the 'fourier' tag to see related work, though I’ll need to execute actions\
  \ through the system.**Planning evaluations review**\n\nThe interface allows free-form thoughts, but only the `/execute_action` lines get parsed. I’m thinking about reviewing details for evaluation 344 to ensure our architecture is replicated. Using `/execute_action{review evaluation_id}`, I can access the submitted solutions and logs. I should review evaluations 344, 335, and 361 to confirm our SAP-Fourier code aligns. \n\nAlso, filtering by the 'fourier' tag might be helpful, but I'm unsure which tags have been used. Since my token budget is comfortable, I’ll proceed with the reviews cautiously to avoid excessive logs.**Managing token efficiency**\n\nIn Token Management, I can prune later, but I want to avoid generating huge logs if I don't have to. Reviewing evaluation content might clutter the \"room output\" since it retrieves full logs, which could take up a lot of tokens. We pruned to 32%, but three review logs could still add thousands of tokens. \n\nInstead of heavy logs, I could\
  \ opt to reflect or wait. There's no \"preview\" option for evaluating content, but I can use `/execute_action{page 2}` for scoreboard results. Updating my plan in Private Memory could minimize token usage, though I have to wait before pruning again.**Minimizing actions for efficiency**\n\nIt’s best to avoid heavy logs while we wait for our running experiments' results. Instead of calling extensive actions, I can leave a note or execute minimal commands like `/execute_action{rank score}` and `/execute_action{page 1}` again. I could also opt to preview evaluations 367 and 368 to check their submission metadata, as it’s light on tokens. \n\nOnce I confirm results, I’ll compare them to evaluation 344 and update the archive if there's improvement. So my final actions will include previewing those submissions while keeping everything minimal. Let's proceed."
