thinking_content: '**Analyzing performance results**


  I need to respond with appropriate actions from the Token Management Room. The results show that FourierForecasterLN-RFFT with p=36 and lr=9e-4 scored -0.026355, which is close to the top score of -0.026314 and better than previous models. So, lr=9e-4 is better than lr=1e-3. I''m considering submitting a scalar ramp, but we''ve tried that before with unsatisfactory results. The next major step is to export gamma/frequency stats for the best Fourier configuration and update the archive. Also, our token budget is now at 32% after pruning, which is good.**Planning next steps**


  I need to queue an export run for the best Fourier configuration (p=36, drop=0.05, lr=9e-4) and add a complete() to save the frequency and gamma stats. I might also replicate Logos 369 with the SOTA score of -0.026314. A cautious minimal variation could involve ablation to p=38 or adjusting the drop to 0.06 or 0.04. As we’re managing tokens at 32%, further pruning isn''t needed. I''ll propose submitting an export-coded evaluation for frequency stats, which means creating a model file based on our past work.**Reviewing submission process**


  Since we''ve made runs 397 and 398, I know there was previous code submission referencing something important. There''s no storage write here, but earlier runs used a local function in the submission content. To include complete(), we can implement compute functions inline or import them from a storage file for better code management. I need to keep within the allowed function interface. We need to implement a create_network that builds Fourier Forecaster LN using JAX, ideally reusing models from earlier submissions if available.**Considering code management**


  I can quickly write to the storage file models_fourier_rfft.py to contain the Fourier model, but writing a large codebase might be heavy. To keep things minimal, I''ll import earlier code, but I don''t currently have it available. For the export run, I could wrap the create_network function to use code from earlier runs 397 and 398, yet since each submission is standalone, I might need to embed code again. Alternatively, I could simply re-run config 398 with complete() that saves ''params'' and computes relevant frequency stats, but I''ll need access to the necessary parameters for the Fourier forecaster.**Planning matrix computations and actions**


  I can compute column norms for any matrix with dimensions 71721 by p, treating it as U. I’ll consider shapes to select candidates for U and V based on size constraints, particularly focusing on dimensions. Now, for actions, I’ll execute to research and review run 398, ultimately submitting new code with minor modifications for export. I’ll need to reconstruct the Fourier RFFT code carefully, ensuring I include the ''complete()'' function and use ''create_network'' from an existing model. First, I’ll call for a review of 398 before the next submission.'
