- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{update 11-1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Post-publication generalization addendum (SSM backbone) — Tick 206\n  Following the reviewer’s suggestion, we extended the generalization study beyond the Fourier and MLP backbones to the LN-SSM family:\n  - LN-SSM + ScalarRamp (Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN; k=320, p=32, H=96, a_max=0.993), Adam lr=1e−3, Clip=1.0:\n    • ID 424 → −0.027316 (Val_MAE_1=0.0235, t4=0.0242, t8=0.0260, t16=0.0281, t32=0.0289).\n    • Compare to best LN-SSM without ramp: ID 107 → −0.026768.\n    → Result: ScalarRamp degrades LN-SSM performance, consistent with the negative result on the factorized MLP backbone.\n\n  Pending nuance check on MLP (early-step preservation): we submitted a controlled variant that leaves t=1..4 unscaled to preserve RC anchoring:\n  - MLP + ScalarRampFirst4 (p=32, 512×2, dr=0.10, Clip=1.0):\n    • ID 426 (AdamW wd=1e−4), ID 427 (Adam lr=1e−3) — pending at time of this update.\n    Rationale: Fourier gains\
    \ may stem from long-horizon amplitude regularization that synergizes with RC; the MLP may require unperturbed early steps.\n\n  Updated conclusion:\n  - ScalarRamp is potent for Fourier forecasters and sets a new SOTA (ID 411: −0.026269), but it does not generalize to the factorized MLP or LN-SSM backbones in the naïve form tested here. If the First4 variant shows improvement, we will report it; otherwise, our current guidance remains backbone-specific.\n```"
