- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{update 11-1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Addendum (MLP Learnable γ[t]) — Tick 209\n  We tested a learnable horizon ramp on the MLP backbone (initialized to 0.25→0.99 with weak priors for ramp closeness and smoothness):\n  - MLP + LearnableGamma (p=32, 512×2, dr=0.10, Clip=1.0)\n    • ID 429 (AdamW wd=1e−4): −0.026824\n    • ID 430 (Adam lr=1e−3): −0.026772\n  Comparison:\n  - Naïve ScalarRamp (all steps): ID 418/419 → −0.026890 / −0.027007\n  - ScalarRampFirst4 (no scaling t≤3): ID 426/427 → −0.026919 / −0.026811\n  - Strong MLP baseline (no ramp): ID 247 → −0.026645\n\n  Conclusion:\n  - Learnable γ[t] improves over naïve/First4 ramp variants on MLP but still underperforms the baseline, reinforcing that fixed or lightly-regularized horizon scaling is not beneficial for the MLP backbone.\n  - Together with the LN-SSM result (ID 424: −0.027316 vs best −0.026768), our guidance stands:\n    • Use ScalarRamp for Fourier (LN-RFFT) where it is\
    \ consistently helpful (ID 411: −0.026269 SOTA).\n    • Avoid ScalarRamp on factorized MLP and LN-SSM; even a learnable γ[t] does not close the gap to baseline.\n\n  Next steps (if pursued): a more expressive, backbone-aware temporal modulator (e.g., per-factor γ_k[t] with stronger priors and explicit early-step preservation) or integrating ramp learning into the frequency domain where it synergizes with LN-RFFT.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering next steps**\n\nI'm thinking about experimenting with a different ramp schedule for the MLP, perhaps adjusting gamma_end to something like 0.95. We’ve already tried ramp and First4 but didn't see positive results, so I might conclude that generalization for both MLP and LN-SSM isn't looking good and plan to publish an addendum with the learnable gamma findings. \n\nAlso, it seems we can't prune more ticks right now because of cooldown, so I'll focus on short messages and plan the next research tasks while ensuring continuous operation.**Planning updates and adjustments**\n\nI’m considering whether to adjust page_size or filter to see the \"learnable\" tag, but it might not be necessary since we're at a safe token budget of 56%. \n\nThis turn involves going to the archive and updating capsule 11-1 with our findings on learnable gamma (IDs 429, 430) and the MLP results, which still aren't positive compared to naive ramp. I might propose that we stop pursuing\
  \ ramp on MLP/SSM and pivot instead. There’s no need to submit more experiments for now, and I should keep my answers concise.**Finalizing archive update**\n\nI’m noticing a potential error regarding the ID numbering of the archive capsule. The initial message index is 1, and I see that update 11-1 was used previously. I can either update this or choose to \"reply 11\" to add a new message, but both are valid options.\n\nFor this update, I’ll go ahead with update 11-1. I need to ensure the addendum content is concise, so I'll prepare it with the key details on learnable gamma and MLP improvements while noting that they still fall below the strong MLP baseline.**Final thoughts on model performance**\n\nI’m noticing that the step MAEs are nearly unchanged in the early stages but start to degrade in the mid to late phases. My conclusion is that even the learnable ramp doesn’t seem to help the MLP. Combining this with earlier negative results for SSM, the final guidance will be to use ScalarRamp,\
  \ but only for Fourier. \n\nAlright, I’m ready to put this all together and move forward with the recommendations!"
