  I''m figuring out the relationship between -0.026269 and -0.026312. It turns out that -0.026269 is actually larger because it''s less negative — so, better! I remember earlier that a better MAE decreases from -0.02730 to -0.02676, indicating improvements. So, the closer to zero is the preferred score. They state -0.026269 is the new SOTA compared to -0.026272. I''ve got it: smaller negative values are healthier, so ID 435’s score of -0.026328 is indeed worse.**Analyzing ID 435 trial**


  The logs show that for ID 435, trial 2 has a best validation score of 0.026312, but the score of -0.026328 isn''t SOTA. Since the 60-minute trials are still available, I can test the H sweep of 152 and 168. The plan outlines queuing these with a learning rate of 9e-4 and a drop of 0.05. I want to ensure I present actions without heavy formatting. Currently, we’re at 167k tokens. I''ll craft two submissions for the Fourier SOTA sweeps with variations in hidden size.**Preparing submission titles**


  I plan to keep the content code skeleton the same as before, only changing the hyperparameters. I have two submissions ready:


  - Title: "FF-LN ScalarRamp (p=36, H=152, drop=0.05) + Adam(lr=9e-4), Clip=1.0 — 60min"

  - Title: "FF-LN ScalarRamp (p=36, H=168, drop=0.05) + Adam(lr=9e-4), Clip=1.0 — 60min"


  I’ll stick with a drop of 0.05 for the H sweep while maintaining gamma at 0.99. I also consider adding a reproduction submission for SOTA ID 411 to gauge improvements. The focus remains on commands while addressing previous compute loss errors.**Compiling submissions elegantly**


  I need to compile the submissions elegantly, so I''ll produce two commands for execution: `/execute_action{submit 1}` with YAML content code utilizing `models_fourier_ramp` to import `create_network` and `export_stats`. My focus here is just on generating those commands without getting too detailed or complicated. I’ll keep it streamlined to ensure clarity. Let''s get those commands ready!'
