    \ 429 −0.026824, ID 430 −0.026772) vs strong baseline ID 247 −0.026645.\n      - LN-SSM: ramp hurts (ID 424 −0.027316) vs best LN-SSM ID 107 −0.026768.\n\n      Archive:\n      - Paper accepted (ID 11): ScalarRamp sets Fourier SOTA and fails to generalize to MLP/LN-SSM; addenda include SSM result and MLP First4/Learnableγ updates.\n\n      Plan:\n      - Finish H=152/168; if any beat −0.026269 by ≥2e−5, replicate.\n      - If not, micro-sweep dropout around 0.045 (e.g., 0.047, 0.043) at H=160, lr=9e−4 under 60-min timeout.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Planning for submissions**


  I’m analyzing the performance metrics. It looks like p=36 is producing the best results, while p=40 regressed, and p=34 was prior evidence. I want to try dropout rates around 0.047. However, I can submit only two concurrent updates, since 439 and 440 are already running. To stay within this limit, I’ll record my plan in private memory. I''ll also consider trimming down more ticks in Token Management for efficiency.**Pruning logs for efficiency**


  I’m looking at cooldowns and realizing we might prune again after tick 212. Since I added a block for ticks 191 to 197 at tick 208, it seems we’re in a good spot to reduce token usage by pruning older logs from 198 to 206. I see that this range has high token counts, so I’ll aim to compress summaries while ensuring no overlaps with previously pruned blocks. I’ll focus on pruning from 198 to 205 to optimize further.**Preparing to prune blocks**


  I’m getting ready to prune blocks between ticks 198 and 205. I want to summarize the Fourier ramp results and MLP generalization into a cohesive overview. While I can optionally preview the "filter tag Fourier," it’s not strictly necessary. I’m planning to submit my action to private memory, then proceed with the prune response. Additionally, I''ll also think about updating the archive with improvements. I need to ensure I handle YAML quoting correctly, especially with special characters and colons.**Creating a summary**


  I’m working on a good summary of key changes. First, the extended improved Fourier with H=160 and a drop adjustment from 0.045 to -0.026329 shows promise, as the lower learning rate of 8.5e-4 resulted in -0.026416. Importantly, drop adjustment seems to help more than lowering the learning rate. I’ve noted that the LN-SSM and scalar ramp performed poorly, while the MLP with initial configurations showed some improvement. The archive accepted my response, and now I''m planning next steps to refine drop and H, possibly proposing research with a filter.'
