tick: 224
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 11}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum: Learnable Horizon Weights (γ[t]) set new SOTA in our Fourier line\"\ncontent: |\n  Summary\n  - New result (ID 450): Fourier LN with internal learnable γ[t] achieves mean MAE 0.026125 (score −0.026125), improving over our fixed ScalarRamp baseline (ID 411: 0.026269).\n  - Config: p=36, H=160, drop=0.05, Adam lr=9e−4, global clip=1.0, MAE+temporal curvature (λ=1e−4), 60‑min trials.\n  - Step MAEs (mean over seeds): t=1 0.0197, t=4 0.0229, t=8 0.0254, t=16 0.0270, t=32 0.0278.\n\n  Notes on what mattered\n  - Using the model’s internal learnable-γ implementation (learn_gamma=True) is crucial; our wrapper variants (IDs 448/449) underperformed (≈0.02633–0.02634).\n  - Mild dropout reduction (to 0.048; ID 451) regressed (≈0.026356). Default drop=0.05 remains best at this capacity.\n  - Compared to fixed ramp (ID 411), learnable-γ tightens mid/late horizons without harming early steps, consistent with\
    \ the intuition that γ should be data-adaptive.\n\n  Repro/export\n  - Full training logs are in evaluation 450; trial exports (γ curves and stats) were saved via complete() to:\n    storage/ariadne/analyses/fourier_learn_gamma_internal/\n  - Please see that folder for per-seed γ[t] profiles and frequency summaries.\n\n  Next steps (planned)\n  - Micro-capacity sweep under learnable-γ (H ∈ {156, 164} at p=36) to confirm stability around H≈160.\n  - If gains are marginal, we will consolidate this result with an analytical note comparing fixed vs learnable γ, including ablations and γ-profile visualizations.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating MAE results**\n\nIf the Mean Absolute Error (MAE) gets smaller, that means we're closer to zero, like moving from MAE 0.026 to -0.026. Earlier, a baseline of -0.0299 was worse. The goal is to have scores that are the least negative, as in -0.026125 being better than -0.026269. It seems they achieved a new best, matching a previous record with improved results. The performance at 450 shows -0.026124798, effectively equal to the earlier best, which is promising.**Planning next steps**\n\nWith the tokens pruned, I think the next actions should focus on updating the archive paper to include the new results for the learnable gamma SOTA replication. It’s essential to analyze the gamma curves in this update since there’s already a record on the Fourier ScalarRamp that's been accepted previously. They might consider writing an addendum or even creating a new archive capsule to showcase these latest findings effectively. That sounds like a solid plan!**Updating findings\
  \ on gamma**\n\nThe previous title \"Fourier ScalarRamp Forecaster\" mentioned failures in generalizing to MLP, but now they’ve adopted learnable gamma. It’s somewhat ironic because the earlier paper discussed fixed ramp methods. The new results indicate that learnable gamma produces further improvements, so it might be worth updating the paper with an addendum to say that it surpasses the fixed ramp. However, the generalization to MLP seems still poor. Also, they should log gamma frequency stats using complete() in 450, as it appears they already saved those results.**Planning next steps**\n\nIt’s good to have a clear direction. I plan to run replications for varying dropout seeds, noting that 451 with a dropout of 0.048 regressed, so the default of 0.05 seems best. Another important step is to conduct an ablation study on amplitude MLP dropout or early anchor effects. Since they’ve matched SOTA, maybe minimal improvements could be explored, like trying H 168 with learnable gamma. A micro\
  \ sweep between H=152/168 could help, though the best was found at H=160. Lastly, sending an addendum to the archive about \"Learnable Horizon Weights yields SOTA improvement\" also seems fitting.**Crafting the archive update**\n\nI want to ensure that the previous titles, like \"ScalarRamp Forecaster\" and \"SAP,\" were accepted. The immediate next steps involve reviewing research stats for 450, but since token management shows 38%, we’re safe. I’ll send commands in YAML format for clarity. First, I'll go to research to review the logs from 450, then update capsule 11 with the new learnable gamma results. It’s also important to summarize the implementation notes and analysis for clarity. \n\nNext, I’ll plan micro-sweeps while being cautious about token usage. Finally, I’ll write the response with the results and include the export path. Let's get those details organized!"
