parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Update — Fourier LN with Learnable Gamma: New SOTA and plan\n  Result:\n  - ID 450 (learn_gamma=True) achieved mean MAE 0.026125 (score −0.026125), matching Lumina ID 443 and improving on our fixed ramp (ID 411: 0.026269).\n  - Per-seed best MAEs (approx): 0.026040 (seed 43), 0.026275 (seed 44), 0.026059 (seed 45); balanced and robust.\n  - ID 451 (drop=0.048) regressed to 0.026356 → keep drop=0.05.\n\n  Pending micro-sweeps:\n  - H=156 (ID 453) and H=164 (ID 454) under learnable γ to confirm capacity stability around H≈160.\n\n  Exports:\n  - γ[t] curves and stats saved via complete() at storage/ariadne/analyses/fourier_learn_gamma_internal/ (ID 450).\n  - Will export for H=156/164 once complete for comparison.\n\n  Next steps (paper and experiments):\n  - Archive: add a concise section comparing fixed-ramp vs learnable-γ (include IDs 411, 435, 439, 440, 445, 446, 450, 451, 453, 454). Show mean\
    \ MAE and per-seed spread; plot γ[t] profiles.\n  - Micro-sweeps (only if needed): LR ±2.5% around 9e−4; clip=1.0 (keep fixed unless instability appears).\n  - Defer non-essential variants (priors/anchors) since internal γ already optimal; avoid scope creep.\n\n  Big-picture:\n  - Fourier LN + learnable γ decisively exceeds our best MLP and SSM lines.\n  - Focus on consolidation: reproducibility, clear ablations, and interpretability (γ dynamics vs horizon).\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning experiments and analysis**\n\nI see that we're at 39% tokens, so that seems fine. The user's message about the station doesn't include a specific request, so I think I should act as a research agent. I could queue more experiments, conduct analysis exports, or update the archive more thoroughly. \n\nI’m considering submitting two more micro sweeps, maybe testing dropouts around 0.05 like 0.052 or 0.048. I know 0.048 has regressed previously, so maybe 0.052 won't help either. I’ll think about testing a learning rate micro-sweep, keeping the dropout at 0.05 stable.**Considering gamma testing and archiving**\n\nI've already tested 0.95 and 1.05 for the fixed ramp, resulting in -0.026230 both times. For the learnable gamma, the status is still unknown, but maybe that's not critical.\n\nI’ve exported the gamma/frequency stats for ID 450 using the complete() function. Now, I could either create a new capsule in the Archive for the Fourier LN LearnableGamma SOTA result\
  \ or update the existing \"Fourier ScalarRamp Forecaster\" capsule to include the learnable gamma variant. Since we previously posted an addendum, we should be fine adding to the archive. \n\nI also need to document the attempts at cross-backbone generalization, focusing on Fourier LN improvements.**Exploring learnable gamma and improvements**\n\nI'm considering testing \"learnable gamma\" for the MLP line again. The MLP uses a residual copy plus factor outputs, while gamma gating relates to horizon gating. Previously, we found that \"learnable gamma\" for MLP ramps was below the baseline, so it might not be helpful.\n\nFor Fourier LN with learnable gamma, we could test a \"proj_rank p sweep\" around 36, possibly at 34 and 38, since 36 showed promise before. \n\nAnother area to look into is gradient clipping; we've used 1.0, but perhaps we should consider adjustments like 0.9 or 1.05, even though it may not be necessary for learnable gamma.**Testing optimizers and dimensions**\n\nI’m considering\
  \ testing AdamW versus Adam; it could lead to degradation. Previously, Adam without weight decay was best for MLP, and it might not be necessary for Fourier LN.\n\nAnother idea is testing amplitude MLP dropout, but past modifications haven't been helpful.\n\nI'm also thinking about \"Rank_k\" with k=320; we could experiment with k=352 or 288, though it will be compute-heavy.\n\nGiven we're focusing on p=38 with learnable gamma, I’m hesitant. We might prefer even numbers due to the spectrum, but it's not strictly necessary. Since we have a SOTA of -0.026125, I wonder if we should publish a high-level summary. The user hasn't specified a next step, so we could continue exploring micro-sweeps.**Organizing tasks and reflections**\n\nI'm thinking about replicating setup 450 with slightly different seeds to check stability. Since evaluation already runs on three seeds, I’m confident it’s robust.\n\nI should provide reflection as recommended by earlier \"Automatic System Tips.\" Also, it's time\
  \ to update my summary in Private Memory.\n\nAs for my next steps, it could be useful to explore \"p=38\" with learnable gamma and various adjustments. Additionally, I might want to tweak BATCH_SIZE and curvature penalty lam. \n\nConsidering we have an impressive score of -0.026125, I should plan to finalize my analysis for the learnable gamma and draft a thorough paper while being mindful of any pending submissions. Finally, updating my Private Memory draft seems like the next logical action."
