    \ exports for the p-sweep runs upon completion.\n\n  Next\n  - If p-sweep confirms p≈36, we will finalize a consolidated report comparing fixed ramp vs learnable γ with: (i) capacity sweeps (H), (ii) p-sweeps, (iii) per-seed variability, and (iv) γ-profile visualizations.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing performance metrics**\n\nOkay, so I’ve confirmed that -0.026976 is indeed higher (less negative) than -0.027294, which means that a larger (less negative) score is considered better. The goal here is to minimize MAE, as a more substantial negative MAE indicates a better outcome. So, comparing scores, ID 450 at -0.026125 is the best, followed closely by ID 454 at -0.026248. I’m also seeing that H=160 performs better than H=164. We’re still waiting on the results for our submitted p-sweeps. Next steps might include considering different learning rates and updating our archives with the latest findings.**Evaluating prune actions**\n\nIt seems like the cooldown is active after the last prune at tick 224. Now we’re at tick 228, which means we should be allowed to prune again since it's been the 4 ticks required. I can prune some older ticks with large logs, such as tick 223 with 3642 length, 222 with 1022, and 221 with 549. I’m wondering if it makes more sense to\
  \ prune even earlier ticks like 135 as well. The biggest remaining tick is 136 with 8264, but we previously pruned ticks 136-138. Interesting that tick 140 still has tick 136 present!**Reviewing pruning and experiments**\n\nIt looks like I double-checked our earlier pruning and confirmed that ticks 136-138 were indeed pruned, and the earlier \"System: Pruned\" messages might have just summarized that. I noticed that the current Dialogue History only shows ticks 223, 222, 221, and some others, while the pruned ones are no longer there. Given that token usage is at 117k of 270k, it seems safe. Instead of pruning now, I think it might be better to submit the next experiments, focusing on testing p=36 again with dropout variations. Also, exporting interpretability data could be useful, but maybe writing to storage isn't necessary right now.**Updating archives and performance**\n\nI’m considering adjusting our base method to use \"complete()\" for H=160, similar to what we did with ID 450.\
  \ Now, I think it’s time to submit an updated archive titled \"Fourier LN: learnable gamma\" or revise our existing \"Fourier ScalarRamp Forecaster: Simple Horizon Gating Sets SOTA...\" with an addendum. We should update the main message using `/execute_action{update id}`. I'm also thinking about creating a new archive capsule summarizing \"Learnable gamma horizon weights.\" The results show Lumina 443 and our ID 450 both tied at -0.026125, but there might be others that perform even better!**Planning next steps**\n\nI see that our new SOTA is -0.026125, improving from -0.026269. We had previously pruned certain ticks, allowing us to focus on potential adjustments to capacity or learning rates to aim for further improvements. The best results seem to come from H=160. While experimenting, we should also think about using a slight label smoothing or possibly adjusting dropout rates. \n\nTo keep things organized, we’ll plan to update the archive with our findings. I'll summarize micro-sweep\
  \ results and submit minor learning rate tests next. We can’t queue more experiments until the current p-sweeps finish, so let’s not add anything new to the queue just yet!**Summarizing p-sweep status**\n\nI need to prepare a summary regarding the pending p-sweep results. The micro-sweep for H confirms that 160 is optimal. ID 454 registered a result of -0.026248, which is better than -0.026269 but not as good as -0.026125. I'm noticing a bit of confusion with the \"meta\" command; it seems some actions may not be recognized, so it's safer to avoid using that for now. \n\nNext, I’ll add commands to go to the archive and reply with the summary regarding the micro-sweeps, mentioning the p-sweep results will follow. Let’s focus on taking these steps!"
