tick: 230
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"FactGlobalMLP-LN + Learnable Gamma (p=32,H=512x2,dr=0.10) — Adam lr=9e-4, Clip=1.0\"\ntags: \"mlp, layernorm, factorized-loadings, learnable-gamma, residual-copy\"\nabstract: \"Generalization study: apply learnable per-horizon weights γ[t] to a strong Factorized Global MLP + RC + LN backbone (p=32, H=512×2, dr=0.10). Training: Adam lr=9e−4, global clip=1.0, MAE+curvature (λ=1e−4), 60min trials. Tests whether learnable γ benefits non-Fourier models.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n\n  from losses import mae_with_temporal_curvature\n  from models import ResidualCopyHead  # residual copy head (tested earlier)\n\n  class FactGlobalMLP_LearnGamma(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int = 320\n      proj_rank: int = 32\n      hidden_size: int = 512\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.10\n      output_horizon:\
    \ int = 32\n      normalize_u: bool = True\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          # x: (B, 4, N)\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          k = self.rank_k\n          p = self.proj_rank\n          T = self.output_horizon\n\n          # Factorized loadings: U (N,p), V (k,p)\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          if self.normalize_u:\n              U = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n\n          # Project inputs to factor space\n          # (B,4,N) @ (N,p) -> (B,4,p); then (B,4,p) @ (p,k) -> (B,4,k)\n          xU = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,pk->btk', xU, V.T)  # (B,4,k)\n\n          # LayerNorm over factor dim\n          ln = nn.LayerNorm(axis=-1, use_scale=True, use_bias=True)\n          factors_n = ln(factors_in)  # (B,4,k)\n\n          # Per-factor MLP over\
    \ 4-step context -> 32-step forecast\n          f_flat = factors_n.reshape(B * k, T_in)      # (B*k, 4)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat); h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(h); h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          f_out = nn.Dense(T)(h).reshape(B, k, T).transpose(0, 2, 1)   # (B,T,k)\n\n          # Decode back to neurons via factorized loadings: z=(B,T,p), y=(B,T,N)\n          z = jnp.einsum('btk,kp->btp', f_out, V)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btp,np->btn', z, U)\n\n          # Learnable per-horizon weights γ[t] in (0,1)\n          def gamma_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              # initialize near a mild ramp 0.25->0.99 for stability\n              t = jnp.linspace(0.25, 0.99, shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n              logits = jnp.log(t) - jnp.log(1.0 - t)\n              return logits.astype(dtype)\n\
    \          gamma_raw = self.param('gamma_raw', gamma_init, (T,))\n          gamma = nn.sigmoid(gamma_raw)[None, :, None]  # (1,T,1)\n          y_factor = gamma * y_factor\n\n          # Residual copy head\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=T)(x)\n          return y_copy + y_factor  # (B,T,N)\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 9e-4,\n          'rank_k': 320,\n          'proj_rank': 32,\n          'hidden_size': 512,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'normalize_u': True\n      }\n\n  class Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.model = FactGlobalMLP_LearnGamma(**kwargs)\n          self.needs_rng = True  # for Dropout\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          # Pass dropout rng if provided\n  \
    \        return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return Wrapper(\n          rank_k=int(hparams.get('rank_k', 320)),\n          proj_rank=int(hparams.get('proj_rank', 32)),\n          hidden_size=int(hparams.get('hidden_size', 512)),\n          dropout_rate=float(hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.10)),\n          normalize_u=bool(hparams.get('normalize_u', True)),\n      )\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      return mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=1e-4)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8)\n      )\n```\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"FactGlobalMLP-LN + Learnable Gamma (p=32,H=768x2,dr=0.10) — Adam lr=9e-4, Clip=1.0\"\ntags: \"mlp, layernorm, factorized-loadings, learnable-gamma, residual-copy\"\nabstract:\
    \ \"Generalization at larger width: apply learnable γ[t] to a stronger MLP (H=768×2). Same training as 512×2 (Adam lr=9e−4, clip=1.0, dr=0.10, λ=1e−4, 60min). Tests whether learnable γ scales with capacity.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n\n  from losses import mae_with_temporal_curvature\n  from models import ResidualCopyHead\n\n  class FactGlobalMLP_LearnGamma(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int = 320\n      proj_rank: int = 32\n      hidden_size: int = 768\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.10\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      normalize_u: bool = True\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          k = self.rank_k\n          p = self.proj_rank\n          T = self.output_horizon\n\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(),\
    \ (k, p))\n          if self.normalize_u:\n              U = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n\n          xU = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,pk->btk', xU, V.T)  # (B,4,k)\n\n          factors_n = nn.LayerNorm(axis=-1, use_scale=True, use_bias=True)(factors_in)\n\n          f_flat = factors_n.reshape(B * k, T_in)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat); h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(h); h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          f_out = nn.Dense(T)(h).reshape(B, k, T).transpose(0, 2, 1)  # (B,T,k)\n\n          z = jnp.einsum('btk,kp->btp', f_out, V)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btp,np->btn', z, U)\n\n          def gamma_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              t = jnp.linspace(0.25, 0.99, shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n              return (jnp.log(t)\
    \ - jnp.log(1.0 - t)).astype(dtype)\n          gamma_raw = self.param('gamma_raw', gamma_init, (T,))\n          gamma = nn.sigmoid(gamma_raw)[None, :, None]\n          y_factor = gamma * y_factor\n\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=T)(x)\n          return y_copy + y_factor\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 9e-4,\n          'rank_k': 320,\n          'proj_rank': 32,\n          'hidden_size': 768,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'normalize_u': True\n      }\n\n  class Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.model = FactGlobalMLP_LearnGamma(**kwargs)\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input, training=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def\
    \ create_network(hparams):\n      return Wrapper(\n          rank_k=int(hparams.get('rank_k', 320)),\n          proj_rank=int(hparams.get('proj_rank', 32)),\n          hidden_size=int(hparams.get('hidden_size', 768)),\n          dropout_rate=float(hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.10)),\n          normalize_u=bool(hparams.get('normalize_u', True)),\n      )\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      return mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=1e-4)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8)\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating learnable gamma in models**


  I''m analyzing the learnable gamma performance in the MLP architecture. I''ve noticed it ramps below the baseline. Interestingly, the internal implementation in Fourier models worked better than the wrapper gating approach. It seems like the MLP doesn''t currently include this internal learnable gamma, but I should consider adding it with appropriate layer normalization interactions. There''s also a need to test learnable gamma in the SSM architecture, even though previous tests showed poor performance. I may need to tweak some code for that.**Implementing learnable gamma in MLP**


  I''m thinking about applying learnable gamma to the MLP baseline best configuration, specifically the FactGlobalMLP-LN setup. This involves using AdamW or Adam with clipping. The current state-of-the-art SOTA for MLP is at -0.026608 with a specific configuration. I believe we can replicate this within our own code line. To implement learnable gamma, I''ll add a parameter and ensure predictions are modified appropriately. It looks like I need to revise either the network wrapper or the compute_loss function to make this work correctly.**Exploring implementation of learnable gamma**


  I’m considering how to incorporate the learnable gamma in the forward pass of the network. It seems simpler to create a wrapper class that multiplies predictions by this gamma parameter. The `create_network` function should return the network with the required initialization and application. I can store the parameters in Flax, which allows for a clean integration. Alternatively, I could apply gamma directly in the `compute_loss` function. Essentially, I need to define a new model for submission that includes both the base MLP and the additional gamma parameter.**Considering model structure with learnable gamma**


  I''m exploring how to utilize an external base network as a submodule in Flax. One option is to create a wrapper module that initializes the base network and multiplies its outputs by gamma. The existing `create_network` function returns a Wrapper class with a reference to my Factorized MLP. I can implement a new `LearnGammaWrapper` that contains the base model and includes the gamma parameter in the parameter tree. Defining a new `nn.Module` in the submission code to hold the base submodule will simplify this process.**Navigating learnable gamma implementation**


  I''m trying to figure out how to incorporate learnable gamma into the model without having direct access to the base Flax Module class. One option is to adjust the compute_loss function to factor in gamma, but that implies a parameter is needed. Alternatively, I can define a new Flax Module to hold the base model and the gamma parameter. I need to ensure gamma is included in the parameter tree correctly. The goal is to run experimentations using MLP with and without learnable gamma, documenting all results for submission.**Implementing the model with learnable gamma**


  I’m considering defining the entire MLP model code in the submission to meet requirements, though it may be heavy. A simplified Factorized MLP with LN and RC head could work within the token limit. I need to ensure the model shapes are accurate, especially if I''m introducing learnable gamma, which requires modifying the compute_loss function. I’m aiming to implement a custom network that defines the Factorized MLP and gamma parameter, while keeping dropout and other elements manageable. I''ll finalize the architecture carefully to satisfy the generalization requirement, even if performance isn''t perfect.**Preparing model submissions**


  I’m planning to create two submissions: MLP-LN + LearnableGamma with dimensions of 768x2 or 512x2. I’ll focus on a simplified model with two Dense(512) layers using gelu and dropout, plus a decode layer set to Dense(32). Implementation will include a ResidualCopyHead and make sure to utilize a layer norm across the k dimension. I''ll also include compute_loss with a curvature term and set up the optimizer with Adam. After implementing these features, I’ll submit the evaluations. I need to keep track of the token budget too!'
