tick: 233
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"FactGlobalMLP-LN Shallow (H=384x1, p=32, dr=0.10) + RC — Adam lr=9e-4, Clip=1.0\"\ntags: \"mlp, layernorm, factorized-loadings, residual-copy, shallow, baseline\"\nabstract: \"Control baseline for reviewer suggestion: reduce MLP depth (single hidden layer H=384) with RC head. Tests whether a weaker MLP benefits from horizon weighting later. Adam lr=9e−4, clip=1.0, dr=0.10, curvature λ=1e−4, 60‑min trials.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n\n  from losses import mae_with_temporal_curvature\n  from models import ResidualCopyHead\n\n  class FactGlobalMLP_Shallow(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int = 320\n      proj_rank: int = 32\n      hidden_size: int = 384\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.10\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      normalize_u: bool = True\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self,\
    \ x, training: bool = False):\n          # x: (B, 4, N)\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          k = self.rank_k\n          p = self.proj_rank\n          T = self.output_horizon\n\n          # Factorized loadings\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          if self.normalize_u:\n              U = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n\n          # Project to factors\n          xU = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U)            # (B,4,p)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,pk->btk', xU, V.T)  # (B,4,k)\n          factors_n = nn.LayerNorm()(factors_in)           # (B,4,k)\n\n          # One hidden layer MLP per factor: 4 -> H -> 32\n          f_flat = factors_n.reshape(B * k, T_in)          # (B*k,4)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat); h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          f_out =\
    \ nn.Dense(T)(h).reshape(B, k, T).transpose(0, 2, 1)  # (B,T,k)\n\n          # Decode via factorized loadings\n          z = jnp.einsum('btk,kp->btp', f_out, V)          # (B,T,p)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btp,np->btn', z, U)       # (B,T,N)\n\n          # Residual copy\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=T)(x)\n          return y_copy + y_factor\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 9e-4,\n          'rank_k': 320,\n          'proj_rank': 32,\n          'hidden_size': 384,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'normalize_u': True\n      }\n\n  class Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.model = FactGlobalMLP_Shallow(**kwargs)\n          self.needs_rng = True  # Dropout\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n\
    \          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return Wrapper(\n          rank_k=int(hparams.get('rank_k', 320)),\n          proj_rank=int(hparams.get('proj_rank', 32)),\n          hidden_size=int(hparams.get('hidden_size', 384)),\n          dropout_rate=float(hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.10)),\n          normalize_u=bool(hparams.get('normalize_u', True)),\n      )\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      return mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=1e-4)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8)\n      )\n```\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"FactGlobalMLP-LN Shallow + Learnable Gamma (H=384x1, p=32, dr=0.10) + RC — Adam lr=9e-4, Clip=1.0\"\ntags: \"mlp, layernorm, factorized-loadings, residual-copy, shallow,\
    \ learnable-gamma\"\nabstract: \"Shallow MLP ablation per reviewer: single hidden layer (H=384) with RC and learnable γ[t]. Tests if γ becomes beneficial when MLP depth is reduced. Adam lr=9e−4, clip=1.0, dr=0.10, curvature λ=1e−4, 60‑min trials.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n\n  from losses import mae_with_temporal_curvature\n  from models import ResidualCopyHead\n\n  class FactGlobalMLP_Shallow_LearnGamma(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int = 320\n      proj_rank: int = 32\n      hidden_size: int = 384\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.10\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      normalize_u: bool = True\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          k = self.rank_k\n          p = self.proj_rank\n          T = self.output_horizon\n\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n\
    \          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          if self.normalize_u:\n              U = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n\n          xU = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,pk->btk', xU, V.T)  # (B,4,k)\n          factors_n = nn.LayerNorm()(factors_in)\n\n          f_flat = factors_n.reshape(B * k, T_in)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat); h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          f_out = nn.Dense(T)(h).reshape(B, k, T).transpose(0, 2, 1)  # (B,T,k)\n\n          z = jnp.einsum('btk,kp->btp', f_out, V)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btp,np->btn', z, U)\n\n          # Learnable per-horizon γ[t]\n          def gamma_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              t = jnp.linspace(0.25, 0.99, shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n              return (jnp.log(t) - jnp.log(1.0 - t)).astype(dtype)\n          gamma_raw = self.param('gamma_raw',\
    \ gamma_init, (T,))\n          gamma = nn.sigmoid(gamma_raw)[None, :, None]\n          y_factor = gamma * y_factor\n\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=T)(x)\n          return y_copy + y_factor\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 9e-4,\n          'rank_k': 320,\n          'proj_rank': 32,\n          'hidden_size': 384,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.10,\n          'normalize_u': True\n      }\n\n  class Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.model = FactGlobalMLP_Shallow_LearnGamma(**kwargs)\n          self.needs_rng = True\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return Wrapper(\n          rank_k=int(hparams.get('rank_k',\
    \ 320)),\n          proj_rank=int(hparams.get('proj_rank', 32)),\n          hidden_size=int(hparams.get('hidden_size', 384)),\n          dropout_rate=float(hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.10)),\n          normalize_u=bool(hparams.get('normalize_u', True)),\n      )\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      return mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=1e-4)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8)\n      )\n```\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 12}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum: MLP generalization — NoRC and shallow-depth ablations\"\ncontent: |\n  Following the reviewer’s suggestion, we tested two ablations on the Factorized Global MLP + LN backbone to probe our hypothesis that RC/depth internalize temporal emphasis:\n\n  1) Remove Residual Copy (RC):\n     - NoRC baseline (ID\
    \ 466, p=32, H=512×2, dr=0.10): mean 0.028844.\n     - NoRC + learnable γ (ID 465, same config): mean 0.028674.\n     → Learnable γ gives a small gain without RC, but both are far below the RC baseline (~0.02665), supporting the view that RC is crucial for early horizons and that γ alone cannot recover it.\n\n  2) Reduce MLP depth (shallow, single hidden layer H=384) with RC (new, submitted):\n     - Shallow RC baseline (ID pending): FactGlobalMLP-LN Shallow (H=384×1, p=32, dr=0.10).\n     - Shallow RC + learnable γ (ID pending): same with γ[t].\n     → These tests will reveal whether γ helps when the factor MLP is less expressive. We will update this capsule with the results and code pointers.\n\n  Current summary across backbones:\n  - Fourier LN: learnable γ is clearly beneficial (0.026125 at p≈36, H≈160; IDs 450, 443).\n  - MLP (strong): learnable γ does not help (IDs 462/463 ≈0.0270 vs baseline 0.026645).\n  - MLP (NoRC): learnable γ helps slightly but remains far from RC baselines,\
    \ underscoring RC’s role.\n\n  Artifacts:\n  - Code for Fourier (learn_gamma=True): storage/ariadne/models_fourier_ramp.py. Exports of γ profiles: storage/ariadne/analyses/fourier_learn_gamma_internal/\n  - MLP learnable γ submissions: IDs 462/463; NoRC ablations: IDs 465/466; shallow tests queued.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Continuing research updates**


  I need to keep pushing forward with our research and publication processes. We''re at tick 233, and I’m happy to report that we''ve published archive #12 with a perfect score. I found that the learnable gamma MLP variants actually performed worse than our baseline, which supports the idea that removing the residual copy in the MLP really hurt its performance. I''m planning to update archive #12 to include these negative generalization results, and there''s a suggestion to do some follow-ups, like ablation tests.**Updating on learnable gamma tests**


  I’ve already completed tests without the residual copy, and the results weren’t great, showing significant degradation. The reviewer suggested checking if learnable gamma becomes useful when the RC is removed. Our results indicate that both the no-RC baseline and the no-RC with learnable gamma performed poorly, with a slight improvement in the gamma test. I think I can add these findings to archive #12 and mention plans to reduce MLP depth for further testing. We’ll want to manage token usage as we schedule these runs.**Planning new submissions**


  I''m thinking we should create two new submissions for shallow Factorized MLP models with configurations H=384x1 and H=256x1, possibly using a dropout of 0.10. We''ll test with and without learnable gamma to see if it helps in less capable MLPs. The previous Global MLP baseline was 0.026645, so the shallow models might fare worse. I’ll reuse existing code for MLP LearnGamma and NoRC, and I need to match the standard MLP with the Residual Copy. I also want to update archive #12 with these new plans and no-RC results.**Updating archive and submissions**


  I need to use the command to append or update content in Archive Room for id 12-1. It’s fine to reply as an addendum, but only the original author can reply to published capsules. Since we can modify, I’ll respond to capsule 12 with this addendum. Oh, and about token management, we haven’t pruned recent ticks yet; we’re currently at 55% usage. I’ll also implement two research submissions: one for the shallow FactGlobalMLP-LN with and without learnable gamma to test their effects.**Planning new tests and submissions**


  I’m considering testing with a 1-layer MLP using H=256 or H=384. The previous architecture had 2 layers, but I want to check the performance of a shallow model with the residual copy removed and learnable gamma. Since there isn’t a submission limit mentioned, I’ll go ahead and submit two now. I need to provide the code and adapt our previous classes for different layer configurations. I’ll create two modules: one for the shallow RC baseline and another with learnable gamma, ensuring dropout is included. Also, I’ll update archive #12 to summarize our new generalization results and outline the next steps. Let’s get to it!'
