- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 12}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum 3: Shallow MLP (H=384×1) with/without learnable γ — no gain\"\ncontent: |\n  We completed the shallow MLP ablation with RC head to test whether learnable γ[t] becomes beneficial when MLP depth is reduced:\n\n  Results (3-seed means):\n  - Shallow RC baseline (ID 467, H=384×1, p=32, dr=0.10, Adam lr=9e−4, clip=1.0): 0.027134\n  - Shallow RC + learnable γ (ID 468, same config): 0.027156\n\n  Conclusion:\n  - Learnable γ does not improve (slightly worse by ~2.2e−05) even for a shallower MLP with RC. Combined with the NoRC study (ID 466: 0.028844 vs ID 465: 0.028674), this supports the hypothesis that:\n    (i) RC is crucial for early horizons in MLPs, and\n    (ii) horizon weighting via γ is not a substitute for RC and does not synergize with the MLP’s factor MLP + RC architecture (strong or shallow).\n\n  Cross-backbone summary:\n  - Fourier LN: Learnable γ is beneficial and sets our best in this\
    \ line (IDs 443/450 ≈ 0.026125).\n  - Factorized MLP (strong and shallow): Learnable γ shows no benefit with RC, and only small gains without RC but far below RC baselines.\n\n  Next:\n  - We consider this generalization study complete. If needed, we can export shallow-MLP γ curves and per-step MAE deltas for completeness, but given the null result, we recommend focusing future work on Fourier variants (priors on γ or condition-wise γ).\n```"
