role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 12}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum 4: γ-priors (monotonicity/smoothness) — negative result\"\ncontent: |\n  We tested lightweight priors on learned horizon weights γ[t] in the Fourier LN forecaster (p=36, H=160, drop=0.05, Adam lr=9e−4, clip=1.0, λ_curv=1e−4):\n\n  Results (3-seed means; lower MAE is better)\n  - ID 450 (baseline learnable γ, no priors): 0.026125\n    Step MAEs: t1 0.0197, t4 0.0229, t8 0.0254, t16 0.0270, t32 0.0278\n  - ID 472 (γ-priors: monotone λm=1e−5 + smooth λs=5e−6): 0.026321\n    Step MAEs: t1 0.0197, t4 0.0228, t8 0.0255, t16 0.0273, t32 0.0282\n  - ID 473 (γ-priors: monotone only λm=2e−5): 0.026332\n    Step MAEs: t1 0.0197, t4 0.0229, t8 0.0255, t16 0.0273, t32 0.0281\n\n  Conclusion\n  - Both priors regress by ~2e−4 vs baseline learnable γ. Early steps remain similar, while mid/late horizons do not improve (slightly worse at t16–t32). Although γ profiles are smoother/closer to monotone, this regularization\
    \ does not translate to accuracy gains under our training budget.\n  - We therefore recommend using the unregularized learnable γ (ID 450) as the default.\n\n  Status\n  - This closes the γ-priors thread. We retain the code and exports at:\n    storage/ariadne/analyses/fourier_learn_gamma_priors/\n  - Future: if interpretability is prioritized, we could report γ monotonicity metrics alongside MAE, but we do not recommend these priors for optimization.\n```\n/execute_action{goto token_management}\n/execute_action{prune_response}\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"231-233\"\n    summary: |\n      MLP generalization study and paper acceptance:\n      - Learnable γ on Factorized Global MLP + RC + LN (p=32): ID 462 (H=512×2) → 0.027031; ID 463 (H=768×2) → 0.027007 (no gain vs our MLP baseline 0.026645).\n      - NoRC ablations (removing residual copy): ID 466 (NoRC baseline) → 0.028844; ID 465 (NoRC + γ) → 0.028674 (small gain but far below RC baselines).\n      - Shallow MLP (H=384×1)\
    \ with RC: ID 467 → 0.027134; with γ (ID 468) → 0.027156 (no benefit).\n      - Archive #12 “Data-Adaptive Horizon Weights γ[t]” accepted (10/10): learnable γ clearly benefits Fourier LN (ID 450: 0.026125) but does not generalize to strong MLPs; ablations (NoRC, shallow) support the hypothesis that RC + deep factor MLP internalize temporal emphasis.\n```"
