- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 12}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum: Per-Condition Step-MAE and Learned γ[t] Profiles (Fourier LN + learnable γ)\"\ncontent: |\n  Summary\n  We exported the learned γ[t] (post-sigmoid of gamma_raw) and computed per-condition step MAEs on the validation set for our best Fourier LN + learnable γ configuration (p=36, H=160, drop=0.05, Adam lr=9e−4, clip=1.0). Results (storage paths below) show:\n  - γ[t] is data-adaptive and non‑monotonic across seeds: rises rapidly to ~0.90–0.94 by t≈20–24, then gently tapers to ~0.70–0.74 by t=32.\n  - Across seeds, monotonicity violation (total decrease) ≈0.23–0.25; slope energy ≈0.165–0.173.\n  - Condition-wise difficulty is highly uneven. Late-horizon errors are substantially higher for specific conditions (e.g., cond7, cond4), while others (cond1/cond6) remain clearly easier.\n  - Validation lacks one nominal condition (cond3); counts are zero and per-condition entries are NaN for that condition.\n\
    \n  Key artifacts (per-seed JSON):\n  - Learned γ[t]: storage/ariadne/analyses/fourier_learn_gamma_export_v2/trial_{42,43,44,45}.json\n  - Per-condition step-MAEs (and γ[t] again for convenience):\n    storage/ariadne/analyses/fourier_learn_gamma_per_condition/trial_{43,44,45}.json\n    (seed 43 mean MAE 0.026040; seed 44 0.026275; seed 45 0.026060)\n\n  Representative findings (seed 43):\n  - Overall step MAE rises from ~0.0201 at t=1 to ~0.0304 at t=32.\n  - Hardest conditions (e.g., cond7, cond4) reach ~0.040–0.041 by t=32, while easier conditions (cond1/cond6) are ~0.026–0.033 at t=32.\n  - Condition coverages vary widely (e.g., cond1 has 181 windows per step; cond7 grows from 32 to 63 per step; cond3 absent).\n\n  Interpretation and Implications\n  - A single global γ[t] can only reflect an average trade-off across conditions. The observed heterogeneity—especially at late horizons—suggests that mild input-adaptive adjustments to γ[t] could be beneficial, selectively emphasizing\
    \ late steps when patterns indicative of “hard” conditions are present (without using labels).\n  - Back-of-envelope: a 1–2% late-step error reduction on the high-error conditions, given their prevalence, could yield a net mean MAE gain on the order of 3e−5 to 1e−4.\n\n  Next (conservative) step (not yet executed; proposed):\n  - Add a tiny input-adaptive residual on γ_raw driven by a small MLP over 4-step global statistics (e.g., mean/std over neurons per step), scaled to ±0.05–0.10 before sigmoid. This preserves parsimony, avoids labels, and isolates the mechanism. All other hparams unchanged from ID 450.\n\n  We will proceed only if we can bound the expected gain >2e−5; otherwise, we will keep this as analysis-only documentation.\n\n  Reproducibility\n  - All exports are under the listed storage paths; seeds, hyperparameters, monotonicity violation, and slope energy are recorded per trial.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning analysis and modifications**\n\nI’m thinking about how to respond. The user hasn’t asked a question, so I should focus on actions we can take. In the Research Counter, I could produce an analysis addendum suggesting a small conditional gamma offset experiment.\n\nTo do this, I’ll submit code with a minor modification for learnable gamma by adding an input-adaptive offset to gamma_raw using a context network. I need to ensure I avoid condition labels for generalization, and I’ll have to implement a network wrapper to modify gamma properly.**Considering model adjustments**\n\nI’m realizing that while the models_fourier_ramp is stored, I haven't viewed its content yet. I could create a wrapper that applies custom logic to compute a residual delta from input x and add it to gamma_raw, but changing the Flax module’s parameters directly can be tricky. Instead, I could consider keeping the model intact and estimating a residual weighting on outputs during compute_loss\
  \ time. Another option would be to implement a \"post-hoc scaling\" of the predicted timesteps using an input-adaptive gate.**Exploring prediction modifications**\n\nI’m thinking through the limitations: only create_network and compute_loss are allowed. If I want to wrap the network to scale predictions after getting them, that might affect the training process since predictions are used to compute loss.\n\nWhen the framework calls network.apply(params, x) for predictions and then passes those to compute_loss, I need to be careful about modifying predictions. If gradients should flow back correctly, adding new learnable parameters via compute_loss isn't really feasible. I wonder if I could parametrize compute_loss with additional variables stored inside params, but that seems complicated.**Defining a network wrapper**\n\nI’m considering how to define create_network so it returns a wrapper object that adds residual scaling using parameters stored in the Flax model variables. To do this,\
  \ I’ll need to create a new Flax Module, which can be done in the submission content. I can define a class like LearnableGammaOffsetWrapper that contains the base model.\n\nWhile gamma is used in the model's computation, altering it directly isn’t straightforward. However, I could implement a different gating mechanism that multiplies output by a scalar dependent on x, which I think falls within the create_network architecture and allows for end-to-end training without breaking any rules.**Implementing a composite network**\n\nI’m planning to create a network that combines Fourier Layer Normalization with a learnable gamma baseline under a class called ModelPlusOffset. Inside the call, I’ll compute the base output and a corresponding offset vector. I want to apply a per-step multiplicative offset to the output, so I'll use an equation that incorporates scaling with a learned feature vector.\n\nI’m considering how to set up the parameters and manage the `compute_loss` function to maintain\
  \ curvature-lam, while ensuring we adhere to the Flax framework. I could define a combined parameters dictionary to accommodate both base and offset parameters, which seems necessary to keep the optimization structured. Overall, I’ll aim to keep implementations straightforward while fulfilling the requirements.**Planning archive updates and next steps**\n\nI’m concerned about whether Flax's apply will error if extra parameters are present. Base.apply seems to only query defined variable names, so extra keys might be ignored, but there’s a risk involved. \n\nGiven the complexity, I think it’s best to focus on our \"analysis only\" plan regarding per-condition analysis. The user mentioned deciding on condition-aware offsets based on predictions above a certain threshold, so I’ll update Archive #12 with a summary, propose a design for a soft input-adaptive offset, and create a code stub for later use. Plus, I need to manage token usage effectively!"
