parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 12}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum: Tiny input-adaptive residual gating hurts; halting this line\"\ncontent: |\n  We tested a label-free input-adaptive residual gate on top of Fourier LN + learnable γ:\n\n  Configs:\n  - ID 492: residual_scale=±0.07 (2-layer MLP over 4-step mean/std; y *= 1 + 0.07·tanh(g[b,t])) → mean MAE 0.026337 (regressed vs 0.026125).\n  - ID 494: residual_scale=±0.05 with per-condition exports — pending at time of writing.\n\n  Outcome:\n  - The ±7% modulation clearly degrades performance across seeds (0.026471 / 0.026327 / 0.026214).\n  - Hypothesis: output-time horizon scaling conflicts with the base model’s learned γ dynamics and curvature loss, introducing undesirable gradient/scale distortions.\n\n  Decision:\n  - Halt residual-output gating. If ID 494 confirms no gain at ±5%, we will treat input-adaptive residuals as negative results and keep the Fourier LN + learnable γ baseline unchanged.\n\n  Next:\n\
    \  - Keep analysis-only focus: per-condition profiles and γ[t] interpretation (already exported).\n  - Any future adaptation will target γ_raw within the model (not post-hoc output scaling) and only if analysis projects >2e−5 potential gain.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Planning next steps**


  I’m looking at evaluation 492, which has a result of -0.026337, worse than the baseline of 0.026125. This suggests that the residual gating might hurt performance. We need to consider stopping residual gating and adopting a final analysis-only approach, also updating the archive with this negative result. Evaluation 494 is queued, but we haven’t received the results yet. We could start preparing to read the exported analysis from the specified path, but we can''t prune tokens again yet since the cooldown is still active.**Preparing updates and experiments**


  I’m thinking of preparing a private memory update and an archive addendum to summarize the negative result. We could run a small ablation, using a residual scale of either 0.03 or 0.0 as a control since baseline results already exist. To isolate the mechanism, I want to test reducing the amplitude with a residual scale of 0.03 or consider incorporating gating into the gamma_raw residual instead. This could avoid destructive effects while still aligning with the gating concept.**Reassessing strategies**


  I''m realizing that modifying gamma_raw in the base model isn''t feasible without re-implementing it, since it''s not easily accessible via the wrapper. If we try simulating gamma adjustments through output gating, it could impact the model''s performance. Recent results show that adding residual gating has degraded performance by about 0.000212 compared to the baseline, which isn''t very encouraging. I think we should stop these experiments, document the negative findings, and possibly propose a strategy for a condition-aware training objective weighting, though we can’t use labels in training.**Considering label usage in experiments**


  I’ve realized that condition labels exist in the data, so there might be a way to use them in compute_loss without violating rules, since it’s not external data. But I’m wondering if that would still count as “label usage,” especially since earlier guidelines insisted on label-free gating. It could be a worthwhile experiment, but to play it safe, we should keep things analysis-only for now. I’ll create an archive addendum with the negative results and update the private memory meta, avoiding further experiments.**Defining next steps and actions**


  It seems like the user hasn’t asked for anything directly but has provided some outputs we need to address. I need to keep the response concise and outline the next actions. First, I’ll gather the analysis of evaluation 492 and update the archive. While we can''t implement a complete export for 492 since we used the default, 494 will have the export once it finishes. I need to write an archive reply summarizing the negative residual gating and suggest halting it. I might also update the private memory as part of this process.'
