parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write ariadne/analyses/README_fourier_learn_gamma.txt}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Fourier LN + Learnable Gamma (γ[t]) — Consolidated Repro Notes\n\n  Best config (60 min per trial, 3 seeds):\n  - Score (mean MAE): 0.026125  (IDs: 443 Lumina I; 450 Ariadne I; 485 re-run/export-fix; 489 per-condition export)\n  - Model: Fourier Forecaster with LayerNorm, Residual Copy, internal learnable γ[t]\n  - Hyperparameters:\n      proj_rank p = 36\n      hidden_size H = 160\n      dropout = 0.05\n      optimizer = Adam (no wd), lr = 9e-4\n      grad clip = 1.0\n      loss = MAE + temporal curvature (λ=1e-4)\n  - Files (Ariadne lineage):\n      Code: storage/ariadne/models_fourier_ramp.py\n      Exports (γ[t]): storage/ariadne/analyses/fourier_learn_gamma_export_v2/trial_{42,43,44,45}.json\n      Per-condition step-MAE: storage/ariadne/analyses/fourier_learn_gamma_per_condition/trial_{43,44,45}.json\n\n  Key findings:\n  - Learnable γ[t] >\
    \ fixed ramp (ID 411: 0.026269). Micro-sweeps confirm best near p≈36, H≈160.\n  - γ-priors (monotonicity/smoothness) regressed (IDs 472: 0.026321, 473: 0.026332). Use unregularized γ[t].\n  - MLP generalization: no improvement with learnable γ (IDs 462/463 ~0.0270). Shallow MLP also no help (IDs 467/468).\n  - Residual output-level modulation (input-adaptive gate) regressed (IDs 492: 0.026337, 494: 0.026301).\n\n  Analysis highlights:\n  - Learned γ[t] is non-monotonic: rises to ~0.90–0.94 by t≈20–24, then tapers to ~0.70–0.74 at t=32.\n  - Late-horizon difficulty concentrated in specific conditions (e.g., cond7/cond4); cond3 absent on validation.\n\n  Suggested future work (only if >2e-5 projected gain):\n  - Condition-aware γ offsets (using dataset condition labels) with careful regularization; export per-condition γ profiles.\n  - Interpretability note correlating γ dynamics with horizon-wise errors and condition difficulty.\n\n  Negative results to avoid:\n  - γ-priors (mono/smooth)\
    \ at small weights.\n  - Output-level residual gating of predictions for γ adaptation.\n```\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 12}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Consolidation: Fourier LN + learnable γ[t] (0.026125) — repro notes and scope closure\"\ncontent: |\n  We consolidate the Fourier LN + learnable γ[t] result at 0.026125 (IDs 443/450; re-run/export IDs 485/489). Repro details and artifact paths are recorded in storage/ariadne/analyses/README_fourier_learn_gamma.txt.\n\n  Main points:\n  - Best: p=36, H=160, drop=0.05, Adam lr=9e−4, clip=1.0, curvature λ=1e−4.\n  - γ-priors (monotone/smooth) regressed; output-level residual modulation regressed; both documented.\n  - MLP generalization remains negative (strong RC captures temporal emphasis).\n  - Analysis shows non-monotonic γ[t] and late-horizon difficulty concentrated in specific conditions.\n\n  Next steps (only if clearly beneficial): evaluate condition-aware γ offsets with labels; otherwise, focus on interpretability/analysis.\
    \ I am closing active sweeps on this line to avoid low-ROI experiments.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering final tasks**\n\nI’m pondering our next steps for final polishing. We might compile an archive to address the residual gating that hasn't been effective. I could also include a note about our negative results. However, since we’ve already added some detail, maybe we could also prune tokens further. Given our current cooldown and usage, we’re okay there. It looks like we’ve matched some performance metrics, but slight improvements might be challenging without further adjustments.**Deciding next steps**\n\nI’m considering our next actions, focusing on whether to implement condition-aware offsets if we predict gains above 2e-5. Our previous analysis predicted potential gains of 3e-5 to 1e-4, but the attempts at gating didn't help much. I think we could test adding small parameter adjustments based on condition labels, which we've previously logged. I want to clarify if we can use these labels for training since they’re part of the dataset, but we must adhere\
  \ to the constraints of our training framework.**Analyzing training labels**\n\nI'm looking at how the training labels are structured. It seems the network only works with the input data \\(x\\), and I wonder if the training loop might still pass labels to the compute_loss or create_network functions, even if that's not in the specified interface. I recall that to compute the per-condition MAE for exports, we needed to read labels during training. There could be ways to access validation labels through trial_data, but I’m not entirely sure if those labels are included.**Considering evaluation code**\n\nI see that we wrote code that conducts extra evaluation outside of training, but I’m limited to only overriding provided functions. I think we might have used complete() to rerun evaluations, but there's a warning about not creating custom scripts. Complete() runs after training, but I'm unsure if it allows access for validation data. Earlier, we successfully performed a per-condition export\
  \ with complete(), which gives me some hope. However, using condition-aware gamma offsets seems tricky if the training loop doesn't pass labels. A label-free approach using input factors could work, but previous attempts at output gating regressed, which makes me cautious.**Consolidating next actions**\n\nI’m realizing that, due to diminishing returns, it’s best to wrap up with consolidated results and stabilization tasks. Maybe I'll keep tokens in mind as I prune upcoming heavy usage. A few suggested actions pop up: \n\nI could update Archive #12 with the negative results and analyses we’ve done, maybe even open a Public Memory capsule, though I’m unsure about restrictions there.\n\nThen, I think about crafting a Reproducibility capsule or scheduling one more replication test with modified dropout rates. However, with minimal expected improvements, it might be wise to go for a short reflection summary and check in with the user about their expectations before finalizing actions."
